window.GEO=[{"id":"AFG","name":"Afghanistan","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇦🇫","latlng":[33,65],"q":[{"q":"Which Sufi poet was born in Balkh in 1207, before his family fled west?","a":"Jalal al-Din Rumi","o":["Jalal al-Din Rumi","Hafez","Omar Khayyam","Ferdowsi"],"f":"He wrote the Masnavi later in Konya, in present-day Türkiye. Hafez and Khayyam were Persian poets from cities in present-day Iran, and Ferdowsi wrote the Shahnameh."},{"q":"The Minaret of Jam, a UNESCO tower of about 65 meters in a remote valley, was built in the 12th century under which dynasty?","a":"The Ghurids","o":["The Ghurids","The Mughals","The Safavids","The Ottomans"],"f":"The Ghurids ruled from the mountains of central Afghanistan and, for a time, northern India. The brick tower may have inspired later minarets farther west."},{"q":"In 1747 a loya jirga chose a young commander as leader. He is widely called the founder of the modern Afghan state. Who was he?","a":"Ahmad Shah Durrani","o":["Ahmad Shah Durrani","Babur","Ismail I","Timur"],"f":"He is also known as Ahmad Shah Baba. The Durrani Empire he built became a major power from the Indus to eastern Iran."},{"q":"In buzkashi, riders on horseback compete to grab a heavy object and carry it to a goal. What is that object?","a":"A goat carcass","o":["A goat carcass","A stuffed leather ball","A wooden ring","A burning torch"],"f":"The name means goat pulling. It is played especially in the north, and a skilled chapandaz can control the carcass at a full gallop."},{"q":"Lapis lazuli mined for thousands of years at Sar-e-Sang supplied the blue stone used in ancient Egyptian jewelry. Those mines are in which region?","a":"Badakhshan","o":["Badakhshan","Helmand","Kandahar","Herat"],"f":"They are among the oldest known commercial mines. The same blue stone appears in the funeral mask of Tutankhamun."}]},{"id":"AGO","name":"Angola","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇦🇴","latlng":[-12.5,18.5],"q":[{"q":"Which 17th-century ruler of Ndongo and Matamba negotiated with the Portuguese and also fought them?","a":"Queen Nzinga","o":["Queen Nzinga","Queen Amina","Yaa Asantewaa","Nandi"],"f":"She ruled from about 1624 to 1663. Amina was a Hausa queen in what is now Nigeria, Yaa Asantewaa led Asante resistance in Ghana, and Nandi was the mother of Shaka."},{"q":"A huge waterfall on the Lucala River, once called the Duque de Braganca Falls, is now named what?","a":"Kalandula Falls","o":["Kalandula Falls","Victoria Falls","Tugela Falls","Augrabies Falls"],"f":"The drop is in Malanje province. Victoria Falls lie on the Zambia-Zimbabwe border, Tugela is in South Africa, and Augrabies is on the Orange River."},{"q":"A long war against Portugal ended in independence in which year?","a":"1975","o":["1975","1960","1961","1992"],"f":"Independence came on 11 November 1975, the same year as Mozambique. A civil war then lasted until 1992. The armed revolt had begun in 1961."},{"q":"A close-hold partner dance that grew in Luanda in the 1980s, from older semba and from zouk, is called what?","a":"Kizomba","o":["Kizomba","Samba","Highlife","Soukous"],"f":"Semba is the older Angolan dance; kizomba is slower and danced in a close embrace. Samba is Brazilian, highlife is West African, and soukous comes from the Congo basin."},{"q":"The giant sable, an antelope with long curved horns, lives wild only in this country. After the civil war it was feared lost. What was later found?","a":"Surviving herds","o":["Surviving herds","That it was a kind of zebra","That hunters had moved it to Kenya","That the animal had been a hoax"],"f":"Its scientific name is Hippotragus niger variani. Biologists who returned to the Cangandala and Luando reserves in the 2000s photographed living herds."}]},{"id":"ALB","name":"Albania","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇦🇱","latlng":[41,20],"q":[{"q":"Which novelist, born in Gjirokaster, wrote The General of the Dead Army and chronicled life under dictatorship?","a":"Ismail Kadare","o":["Ismail Kadare","Ivo Andric","Orhan Pamuk","Naguib Mahfouz"],"f":"Several of his books use fable and Ottoman history to talk about modern power. He later split his time between Albania and France."},{"q":"Gjergj Kastrioti, Albania's national hero, is better known by which name from his resistance to the Ottomans?","a":"Skanderbeg","o":["Skanderbeg","Suleiman","Mehmed the Conqueror","Barbarossa"],"f":"In the 1440s he left Ottoman service and held the mountains of Albania for about 25 years. A fortress museum in Kruje tells his story."},{"q":"From the 1960s to the 1980s the government poured concrete into hundreds of thousands of small domes. What were they?","a":"Military bunkers","o":["Military bunkers","Bread ovens","Beehives","Well covers"],"f":"Enver Hoxha's government expected invasion from every side. Most bunkers were never used in war, and many still dot fields and beaches."},{"q":"UNESCO lists the iso-polyphony of southern Albania, in which two or three voices hold a drone under a melody. It is performed mainly as what?","a":"A cappella singing","o":["A cappella singing","A brass-band march","A solo violin recital","A drum circle"],"f":"Groups sing at weddings and funerals. The Lab and Tosk styles of the south are the ones on the heritage list."},{"q":"Butrint, a UNESCO site in the south, stacks Greek, Roman, Byzantine, and Venetian ruins because the city was what for so long?","a":"A port on a strategic channel","o":["A port on a strategic channel","A desert caravan stop","An Arctic whaling station","A Himalayan monastery"],"f":"It sits by the channel to Lake Butrint, opposite Corfu. Virgil's Aeneid even gives it a legendary link to Troy."}]},{"id":"AND","name":"Andorra","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇦🇩","latlng":[42.5,1.5],"q":[{"q":"Since a 1278 agreement, Andorra has been ruled jointly by the Bishop of Urgell and which French officeholder?","a":"The president of France","o":["The president of France","The king of Spain","The mayor of Toulouse","The pope"],"f":"The French role passed from the count of Foix to the French head of state. The two co-princes remain the system even though Andorra adopted a constitution in 1993."},{"q":"Laws and public schooling in Andorra are written mainly in which language, shared with Catalonia?","a":"Catalan","o":["Catalan","Basque","Occitan","Galician"],"f":"French and Spanish are widely heard in shops, but the language of law and school is Catalan."},{"q":"The Madriu-Perafita-Claror valley, a UNESCO site, is protected as a cultural landscape of high pastures. Shepherds traditionally moved animals up in summer. That seasonal movement is called what?","a":"Transhumance","o":["Transhumance","Terrace flooding","Slash and burn","Fjord fishing"],"f":"Stone huts, terraces, and tracks in the valley record centuries of this mountain farming. The whole parish landscape was listed in 2004."},{"q":"Andorra has no airport and no railway. Most visitors arrive by what?","a":"Road through the Pyrenees","o":["Road through the Pyrenees","A canal barge","A glacier train","A ferry"],"f":"The country is a high valley between France and Spain. Ski slopes and duty-free shopping, not a harbor, drive the modern economy."},{"q":"Trinxat, a mountain dish, fries cabbage and potato together with what salty meat?","a":"Bacon or pork","o":["Bacon or pork","Salt cod only","Smoked reindeer","Dried squid"],"f":"It is cold-weather food from the Pyrenees. Some cooks crack an egg on top once the cake of potato and cabbage is crisp."}]},{"id":"ARE","name":"United Arab Emirates","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇦🇪","latlng":[24,54],"q":[{"q":"When the Burj Khalifa opened in 2010 it became, and long remained, the world's tallest what?","a":"Building","o":["Building","Bridge","Radio mast","Dam"],"f":"It stands a little over 828 meters. The design uses a stepping Y-shaped plan, partly to reduce the force of the wind."},{"q":"Before oil, the coastal towns lived from trade, fishing, and diving for what?","a":"Pearls","o":["Pearls","Sponges","Coral for lime","Ambergris only"],"f":"Divers went down on a single breath with a nose clip and a stone weight. The pearl economy collapsed in the 1930s after Japan began culturing pearls."},{"q":"Seven emirates formed a federation in 1971. Which emirate's ruler became the first president, by custom the post held by that emirate?","a":"Abu Dhabi","o":["Abu Dhabi","Dubai","Sharjah","Ras Al Khaimah"],"f":"Sheikh Zayed bin Sultan Al Nahyan led the union. The vice presidency is customarily held by the ruler of Dubai."},{"q":"Falconry, practiced across the Arabian Peninsula, was inscribed by UNESCO as heritage shared by several countries. The bird is trained to hunt what?","a":"Other birds and small game","o":["Other birds and small game","Fish at sea","Wild camels","Snakes only"],"f":"Houbara bustards were the classic quarry. Today many falcons are bred and tracked with radio, but the old partnership of hunter and bird remains."},{"q":"Liwa and the Empty Quarter south of the emirates are famous for huge dunes and for oasis groves of which fruit?","a":"Dates","o":["Dates","Olives","Coconuts","Figs"],"f":"Date palms can grow with their roots in brackish water. Forts at the oases once guarded the routes toward Oman and Saudi Arabia."}]},{"id":"ARG","name":"Argentina","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇦🇷","latlng":[-34,-64],"q":[{"q":"Which writer, born in Buenos Aires, filled books such as Ficciones with labyrinths, mirrors, and infinite libraries?","a":"Jorge Luis Borges","o":["Jorge Luis Borges","Gabriel Garcia Marquez","Pablo Neruda","Mario Vargas Llosa"],"f":"Borges later went blind and composed by dictation. He became the best-known figure of Argentine literature worldwide."},{"q":"Which Andean summit in Argentina is the highest mountain in the Americas by elevation above sea level?","a":"Aconcagua","o":["Aconcagua","Chimborazo","Huascaran","Cotopaxi"],"f":"Aconcagua rises about 6,961 meters. Ecuador's Chimborazo is lower, yet its peak sits farther from Earth's center because the planet bulges at the equator."},{"q":"The oldest stenciled hands at Cueva de las Manos in Patagonia are roughly how old?","a":"About 9,000 years","o":["About 9,000 years","About 500 years","About 2,000 years","About 200 years"],"f":"Hunters blew pigment around their hands onto the cliff. The UNESCO site records life in Patagonia long before Europeans arrived."},{"q":"Which dance that grew up in Buenos Aires port neighborhoods is typically accompanied by the bandoneon?","a":"Tango","o":["Tango","Samba","Cumbia","Marinera"],"f":"The bandoneon is a button accordion brought by European immigrants. The neighboring city of Montevideo also shares in tango's early history."},{"q":"Ushuaia, on Tierra del Fuego, faces which channel, named for the ship that carried Charles Darwin?","a":"The Beagle Channel","o":["The Beagle Channel","The Strait of Magellan","The Drake Passage","The Panama Canal"],"f":"HMS Beagle surveyed these waters in the 1830s. Darwin's notes from that voyage later shaped his ideas about how species change."}]},{"id":"ARM","name":"Armenia","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇦🇲","latlng":[40,45],"q":[{"q":"Around 405, Mesrop Mashtots designed a new alphabet so holy texts could be written in the local language. How many letters did that original alphabet have?","a":"36","o":["36","22","24","26"],"f":"Two further letters were added in later centuries. The script made a literature of its own possible. Georgian uses a different alphabet from about the same era, and 22, 24, and 26 are the counts of Phoenician, Greek, and Latin."},{"q":"Armenia is often described as the first state to adopt Christianity as its official religion. Tradition dates that decision to about which year?","a":"301","o":["301","33","800","1453"],"f":"The story credits King Tiridates III and Gregory the Illuminator. The exact year is traditional, but the conversion in the early 4th century is not in serious doubt."},{"q":"In 451, Vardan Mamikonian led a defeat against Sassanian Persia in defense of the Christian faith. What is that battle called?","a":"The Battle of Avarayr","o":["The Battle of Avarayr","The Battle of Manzikert","The Battle of Carrhae","The Battle of Nineveh"],"f":"Persia won the field, but the church survived. Manzikert, in 1071, was a later battle between Byzantines and Seljuks. Carrhae was a Roman defeat by the Parthians, centuries earlier."},{"q":"The duduk, a UNESCO-listed soft double-reed instrument, is carved from which wood?","a":"Apricot wood","o":["Apricot wood","Ebony","Bamboo","Pine"],"f":"A second player holds a steady drone while the first plays the melody. Film composers often use the duduk to suggest a mournful landscape."},{"q":"Geghard Monastery, a UNESCO site, is partly carved into the cliff. Its name refers to a relic said to have been kept there. What was the relic?","a":"The spear that pierced Jesus","o":["The spear that pierced Jesus","The Ark of the Covenant","A fragment of the True Cross only","The Holy Grail"],"f":"Geghard means spear. The oldest cave chapel dates from the 7th century, and the main church was built in the 13th."}]},{"id":"ATG","name":"Antigua and Barbuda","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇦🇬","latlng":[17.05,-61.8],"q":[{"q":"Which cricketer, born in St. John's, is widely ranked among the greatest batsmen and captained the West Indies?","a":"Vivian Richards","o":["Vivian Richards","Brian Lara","Garfield Sobers","Clive Lloyd"],"f":"Richards played from 1974 to 1991 and was known for attacking bowling without a helmet. Lara was born in Trinidad, Sobers in Barbados, and Lloyd in Guyana."},{"q":"Nelson's Dockyard at English Harbour is a restored Georgian yard built to repair warships. Why was this harbor so useful?","a":"High hills shielded it from hurricanes","o":["High hills shielded it from hurricanes","It never experienced a low tide","It was a freshwater lake","It froze solid each winter"],"f":"The UNESCO site served the British fleet in the 18th century. Horatio Nelson was stationed here as a young captain and reportedly disliked the post."},{"q":"Antigua and Barbuda became an independent state in which year?","a":"1981","o":["1981","1962","1974","1991"],"f":"Britain had held the islands since the 17th century, after earlier Spanish and French claims. Independence came as a constitutional monarchy in the Commonwealth."},{"q":"Codrington Lagoon on Barbuda is famous for one of the Caribbean's largest nesting colonies of which seabird?","a":"The magnificent frigatebird","o":["The magnificent frigatebird","The emperor penguin","The Atlantic puffin","The snowy owl"],"f":"Males inflate a red throat pouch to attract a mate. The lagoon is a shallow coral enclosure on Barbuda's west side."},{"q":"Unlike volcanic Antigua, Barbuda is mostly a flat island built of what?","a":"Coral limestone","o":["Coral limestone","Glacial moraine","River silt","Active lava"],"f":"That geology gives Barbuda its low profile and pale beaches. A separate small rock, Redonda, is the country's uninhabited third island."}]},{"id":"AUS","name":"Australia","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇦🇺","latlng":[-27,133],"q":[{"q":"Which Australian scientist, born in Adelaide, led the work that turned penicillin into a medicine that could be mass-produced?","a":"Howard Florey","o":["Howard Florey","Alexander Fleming","Louis Pasteur","Marie Curie"],"f":"Fleming noticed penicillin in 1928. Florey and Ernst Chain showed it could cure infections, and all three shared the 1945 Nobel Prize."},{"q":"Uluru is a sacred sandstone monolith for which Aboriginal people of central Australia?","a":"The Anangu","o":["The Anangu","The Yolngu","The Maori","The Noongar"],"f":"The Anangu ask visitors not to climb Uluru. The climb was closed in 2019 out of respect for that tradition."},{"q":"The First Fleet anchored in Botany Bay in 1788, but the penal colony was founded days later at which cove?","a":"Sydney Cove","o":["Sydney Cove","Botany Bay","Hobart","Darwin"],"f":"Captain Arthur Phillip found Botany Bay a poor harbor and moved the ships north to Port Jackson, landing at Sydney Cove."},{"q":"Which of these Australian mammals lays eggs rather than giving birth to live young?","a":"The platypus","o":["The platypus","The kangaroo","The koala","The wombat"],"f":"The platypus and the echidnas are monotremes, the only living egg-laying mammals. Kangaroos and koalas are marsupials."},{"q":"Vegemite, the salty spread created in Melbourne in the 1920s, is made mainly from leftover what?","a":"Brewer's yeast","o":["Brewer's yeast","Cocoa beans","Coconut milk","Chili peppers"],"f":"Chemist Cyril Callister developed it when imports of British Marmite slowed. The argument over how thick to spread it is a national pastime."}]},{"id":"AUT","name":"Austria","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇦🇹","latlng":[47.33333333,13.33333333],"q":[{"q":"Which physicist, born in Vienna, imagined a cat both alive and dead to question quantum measurement?","a":"Erwin Schrodinger","o":["Erwin Schrodinger","Niels Bohr","Werner Heisenberg","Max Planck"],"f":"He published the cat thought experiment in 1935. He shared the 1933 Nobel Prize for the wave equation that carries his name."},{"q":"Hallstatt and the Dachstein mountains sit above Bronze Age salt mines. Salt preserved food, so the mineral was valuable enough to be called what?","a":"White gold","o":["White gold","Black iron","Red ochre","Blue clay"],"f":"The town's name is tied to an ancient Celtic culture, the Hallstatt culture. Its lake setting is now a UNESCO cultural landscape."},{"q":"The Congress of Vienna, which redrew Europe after Napoleon, met in 1814 and 1815 under which Austrian statesman?","a":"Klemens von Metternich","o":["Klemens von Metternich","Otto von Bismarck","Talleyrand alone","Giuseppe Garibaldi"],"f":"Talleyrand represented France, but Metternich hosted and steered the talks. The settlement included recognizing Switzerland's permanent neutrality."},{"q":"Wiener schnitzel is a thin cutlet, fried in breadcrumbs, traditionally made from which meat?","a":"Veal","o":["Veal","Salmon","Venison","Duck"],"f":"Pork versions are common and cheaper, and honest menus label them as such. A lemon wedge is the classic finish."},{"q":"UNESCO lists the Viennese coffeehouse as intangible heritage. A Melange, the classic order, is coffee with what?","a":"Hot milk","o":["Hot milk","Whipped cream only","Melted chocolate","Sparkling wine"],"f":"Guests could sit for hours over one cup and read newspapers provided by the house. That slow visit, not fast takeaway, is what the listing protects."}]},{"id":"AZE","name":"Azerbaijan","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇦🇿","latlng":[40.5,47.5],"q":[{"q":"Which mathematician, born in Baku in 1921, founded fuzzy logic, a way of reasoning with degrees of truth?","a":"Lotfi A. Zadeh","o":["Lotfi A. Zadeh","Al-Khwarizmi","Omar Khayyam","Maryam Mirzakhani"],"f":"He later taught at the University of California, Berkeley. Fuzzy logic is used in everything from washing machines to train brakes."},{"q":"Azerbaijan has more mud volcanoes than any other country. A mud volcano erupts what, rather than lava?","a":"Mud, water, and gas","o":["Mud, water, and gas","Molten basalt","Ash and pumice","Liquid sulfur only"],"f":"Gobustan and the Absheron Peninsula are dotted with them. Some cold eruptions throw mud tens of meters into the air."},{"q":"Yanar Dag, a hillside near Baku, has flames that burn continuously because natural gas seeps from the rock. That sight helped earn the country which nickname?","a":"The Land of Fire","o":["The Land of Fire","The Land of Winds","The Land of Gold","The Land of Cedar"],"f":"Zoroastrian fire temples once stood nearby, including the Ateshgah at Surakhani, later used by Hindu and Sikh pilgrims as well."},{"q":"In the 1870s Baku became the center of the world's first great boom in which industry?","a":"Oil","o":["Oil","Cotton","Diamonds","Whaling"],"f":"The Nobel brothers and the Rothschilds invested in the fields. By 1900 the Baku region produced about half of the world's oil."},{"q":"Azerbaijani carpets, listed by UNESCO, are traditionally woven with which kind of knot and a rich red from local dyes?","a":"A symmetrical knot","o":["A symmetrical knot","A macramé fringe only","A knitted loop","A felted roll"],"f":"Regions such as Shirvan, Karabakh, and Quba have their own patterns. Girls historically learned the designs as part of a dowry."}]},{"id":"BDI","name":"Burundi","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇧🇮","latlng":[-3.5,30],"q":[{"q":"Which prince led the push for independence and was assassinated in October 1961, months before independence day?","a":"Louis Rwagasore","o":["Louis Rwagasore","Patrice Lumumba","Julius Nyerere","Jomo Kenyatta"],"f":"Independence from Belgium came on 1 July 1962, so he never governed the new state. Lumumba was killed in the Congo that same era. Nyerere and Kenyatta led Tanzania and Kenya."},{"q":"The country touches the northeastern shore of the deepest lake in Africa. Which lake is it?","a":"Lake Tanganyika","o":["Lake Tanganyika","Lake Victoria","Lake Kivu","Lake Malawi"],"f":"Only Siberia's Lake Baikal is deeper among freshwater lakes. Tanganyika is shared with Tanzania, DR Congo, and Zambia. This country holds only a short piece of the shore."},{"q":"Independence in 1962 kept a king, the mwami. A coup ended the monarchy in which year?","a":"1966","o":["1966","1962","1972","1993"],"f":"Michel Micombero then became the first president. The later crises of 1972 and 1993 are a different history from the end of the crown."},{"q":"A traditional drink is made by mashing ripe bananas and letting the mash ferment. What is that drink?","a":"Banana beer","o":["Banana beer","Honey wine","Palm wine","Grape wine"],"f":"Honey wine is the tej of Ethiopia. Palm wine is sap tapped from a tree. Grape wine needs vineyards this climate does not support."},{"q":"UNESCO inscribed a dance in which performers carry a huge instrument once reserved for the king. What is the instrument?","a":"The royal drum","o":["The royal drum","A royal harp","A bronze gong","A talking flute"],"f":"The 2014 inscription is the ritual dance of the royal drum. Dancers move with the drums strapped on. The dynastic drum of the old court was the karyenda."}]},{"id":"BEL","name":"Belgium","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇧🇪","latlng":[50.83333333,4],"q":[{"q":"Which instrument maker, born in Dinant, invented the saxophone in the 1840s?","a":"Adolphe Sax","o":["Adolphe Sax","Antonio Stradivari","Bartolomeo Cristofori","Theobald Boehm"],"f":"He was working in Paris and wanted a woodwind with the power of a brass instrument. The saxophone later found its most famous home in jazz."},{"q":"The Battle of Waterloo in 1815, which ended Napoleon's return from exile, was fought a short ride from which city?","a":"Brussels","o":["Brussels","Paris","Amsterdam","Luxembourg City"],"f":"Wellington and Blucher defeated Napoleon on 18 June. The lion mound on the battlefield was built afterward by the Dutch king."},{"q":"The filled chocolate called a praline was popularized in 1912 by the Neuhaus shop in which city?","a":"Brussels","o":["Brussels","Zurich","Vienna","Lyon"],"f":"Jean Neuhaus Jr. wrapped ganache in a chocolate shell. Belgium was already a cocoa port, which made the experiment possible."},{"q":"Belgium's three official languages are Dutch, French, and which third?","a":"German","o":["German","English","Spanish","Latin"],"f":"Dutch is the language of Flanders, French of Wallonia, and German of a small eastern community. Brussels is officially bilingual in French and Dutch."},{"q":"Georges Remi, born in Etterbeek, drew a boy reporter who travels with a dog named Snowy. What pen name did Remi use?","a":"Herge","o":["Herge","Peyo","Morris","Uderzo"],"f":"Herge comes from his initials reversed, R.G. Peyo drew the Smurfs, also Belgian, and Morris drew Lucky Luke."}]},{"id":"BEN","name":"Benin","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇧🇯","latlng":[9.5,2.25],"q":[{"q":"Which singer, born in Ouidah in 1960, brought Fon and Yoruba songs to audiences far beyond West Africa?","a":"Angélique Kidjo","o":["Angélique Kidjo","Miriam Makeba","Cesária Évora","Oumou Sangaré"],"f":"She left for Paris in the 1980s. Makeba was South African, Évora was Cape Verdean, and Sangaré is Malian. Ouidah was also a major port of the Atlantic slave trade."},{"q":"The Kingdom of Dahomey kept an all-woman regiment that Europeans called Amazons. Locally those soldiers were called what?","a":"The Mino","o":["The Mino","The Janissaries","The Impi","The Askari"],"f":"Mino means our mothers. They were a trained unit of the king, especially under Ghezo in the 19th century, and they fought the French in the 1890s. Janissaries, impi, and askari were soldiers of other places."},{"q":"A memorial arch on the beach at Ouidah marks the last point where many captives saw home before the Atlantic crossing. What is it called?","a":"The Door of No Return","o":["The Door of No Return","Elmina Castle","Bunce Island","Cape Coast Castle"],"f":"The road from the town to the beach is still walked as a memorial. Historians argue about exact numbers shipped from any one door, so the lesson is the trade itself, not a tally. Elmina and Cape Coast are in Ghana, and Bunce Island is in Sierra Leone."},{"q":"Ganvie, a town of houses on stilts in Lake Nokoue, was begun by people fleeing what?","a":"Slave raids from Dahomey","o":["Slave raids from Dahomey","A volcanic eruption","A Saharan sandstorm","A tsunami"],"f":"The Tofinu built the village over the water, where cavalry could not follow. It is still lived in, and pirogues are the streets. The lake is a lagoon, not a crater and not a dune sea."},{"q":"The famous Benin Bronzes were cast for a kingdom whose city is in which present-day country, not this one?","a":"Nigeria","o":["Nigeria","Ghana","Togo","Cameroon"],"f":"That kingdom had its court at Benin City. This country took the name Benin in 1975, from the Bight of Benin, when it dropped the colonial name Dahomey. The bronzes were not made here."}]},{"id":"BFA","name":"Burkina Faso","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇧🇫","latlng":[13,-2],"q":[{"q":"In 1984 Thomas Sankara replaced the colonial name Upper Volta. The new name means roughly what?","a":"Land of upright people","o":["Land of upright people","Land of the long river","Land of gold","Land beside the dunes"],"f":"Burkina is Moore and Faso is Dioula, so the name joins two local languages. Sankara was killed in 1987. The old name had described only the upper courses of the Volta rivers."},{"q":"The UNESCO ruins of Loropéni are stone enclosures linked by archaeologists to which old trade?","a":"The gold trade","o":["The gold trade","The Baltic amber trade","The silk trade of China","The spice trade of Malacca"],"f":"The walls stand in the southwest. Their exact century is still debated, so the question does not ask for a date. Gold from the West African savanna, not Chinese silk, is the trade they are tied to."},{"q":"A major pan-African film festival, founded in 1969, is held in Ouagadougou. What is it called?","a":"FESPACO","o":["FESPACO","Cannes","Carthage Film Festival","TIFF"],"f":"The full name is the Pan-African Film and Television Festival of Ouagadougou. The Carthage Film Festival is the other great African film meeting, held in Tunisia. Cannes and TIFF are European and Canadian."},{"q":"Kassena houses at Tiébélé are repainted with geometric patterns after the rains. The paint is made from what?","a":"Mud and natural pigments","o":["Mud and natural pigments","Glazed tiles","Marble dust","Oil paint from tubes"],"f":"Women maintain the walls of a living village, not a museum set. The patterns are decoration and a kind of local record. Nothing about them is industrial paint."},{"q":"The Grand Mosque of Bobo-Dioulasso, with wooden stakes poking from earthen towers, belongs to which building tradition?","a":"Sudano-Sahelian mud architecture","o":["Sudano-Sahelian mud architecture","Gothic stone vaults","Ottoman leaded domes","Greek marble temples"],"f":"The stakes are a ladder for workers who renew the mud plaster after each rainy season. The same family of mosques is famous in Mali. This one dates from the late 19th century."}]},{"id":"BGD","name":"Bangladesh","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇧🇩","latlng":[24,90],"q":[{"q":"Which artist, born in Kishoreganj, drew the starving figures of the 1943 Bengal famine and later led the country's modern art?","a":"Zainul Abedin","o":["Zainul Abedin","Jamini Roy","Rabindranath Tagore","Abanindranath Tagore"],"f":"His famine sketches used stark black ink. He also founded the art institute in Dhaka. The Tagores were giants of Bengali culture, but they were based in Kolkata."},{"q":"The Sundarbans, shared with India, is the world's largest forest of which trees, and it shelters Bengal tigers?","a":"Mangroves","o":["Mangroves","Teak","Baobabs","Pines"],"f":"The name means beautiful forest. Tigers there swim between islands, and the roots of the mangroves slow the storm surges of the Bay of Bengal."},{"q":"On 21 February 1952 students in Dhaka were killed demanding that Bengali be recognized alongside Urdu. That date is now marked worldwide as what?","a":"International Mother Language Day","o":["International Mother Language Day","World Book Day","Human Rights Day","Press Freedom Day"],"f":"UNESCO chose 21 February because of those martyrs. The movement showed that language, not only religion, would define the later break with Pakistan."},{"q":"The independence war of 1971 ended with a surrender on 16 December. The new state broke away from which country?","a":"Pakistan","o":["Pakistan","India","Myanmar","Britain"],"f":"The war followed a refusal to honor an election won by the Awami League. India intervened in December, and victory is marked on 16 December."},{"q":"The combined delta of the Ganges, Brahmaputra, and Meghna is the largest what on Earth?","a":"River delta","o":["River delta","Desert","Coral reef","Ice sheet"],"f":"New land is still built from Himalayan silt, and old channels shift. That fertility feeds one of the most densely populated regions in the world."}]},{"id":"BGR","name":"Bulgaria","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇧🇬","latlng":[43,25],"q":[{"q":"In 1905 Stamen Grigorov, a Bulgarian scientist, first described the bacterium later named for the country's famous food. Which food?","a":"Yogurt","o":["Yogurt","Feta cheese","Sourdough bread","Cured ham"],"f":"Lactobacillus bulgaricus is part of traditional yogurt starters. Grigorov identified it while studying in Geneva."},{"q":"The oldest worked gold yet found comes from a cemetery near which Black Sea city, dating to about 4500 BCE?","a":"Varna","o":["Varna","Sofia","Plovdiv","Burgas"],"f":"The Varna graves held hundreds of gold beads and ornaments. They show that social rank was already marked by metal long before the pyramids."},{"q":"Cyril and Methodius devised Glagolitic. Their disciples in the First Bulgarian Empire then developed which alphabet still used today?","a":"Cyrillic","o":["Cyrillic","Greek","Armenian","Georgian"],"f":"The Preslav and Ohrid schools spread the new letters. Many Slavic languages still use scripts descended from that work."},{"q":"The Valley of Roses, around Kazanlak, is famous for oil distilled from which flower?","a":"The damask rose","o":["The damask rose","The sunflower","Lavender","The tulip"],"f":"It takes a huge mass of petals to make a small bottle of rose oil. The harvest before dawn, while the flowers are cool, is an annual festival."},{"q":"Rila Monastery, founded in the 10th century by the hermit Ivan of Rila, is most famous today for its brightly painted what?","a":"Courtyard arcades","o":["Courtyard arcades","Glass pyramids","Minarets","Wooden stave church"],"f":"The present buildings are mostly 19th century, rebuilt after a fire, in a striking black, white, and red pattern. It is a UNESCO site."}]},{"id":"BHR","name":"Bahrain","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇧🇭","latlng":[26,50.55],"q":[{"q":"Dilmun traders linked Mesopotamia to the Indus Valley and to Magan. Magan is thought to lie mainly in which modern country?","a":"Oman","o":["Oman","Yemen","India","Egypt"],"f":"Dilmun itself, praised for sweet water, left thousands of burial mounds on the main island, now a UNESCO site."},{"q":"Before oil, the island's wealth came from the best natural pearls in the Gulf. Divers descended how?","a":"On a single held breath","o":["On a single held breath","In copper helmets from the 1400s","From glass submarines","With scuba gear invented locally"],"f":"A stone weight pulled them down and a rope pulled them up. The trade faded after cultured pearls appeared in the 1930s."},{"q":"Oil was first found in the Gulf states in Bahrain, in which year?","a":"1932","o":["1932","1908","1951","1971"],"f":"The well was at Jabal al-Dukhan, the mountain of smoke. Iran had oil earlier, at Masjed Soleyman in 1908, but Bahrain's strike was the first on the Arab side of the Gulf."},{"q":"A lone mesquite known as the Tree of Life has grown for centuries in the middle of the desert. It survives because its roots reach what?","a":"Deep groundwater","o":["Deep groundwater","A buried glacier","A leak from a pipeline","Nightly dew only"],"f":"There is no visible spring. Similar trees show that the island's limestone holds pockets of fresh water, the same gift ancient Dilmun was praised for."},{"q":"The King Fahd Causeway, opened in 1986, ties Bahrain by road to which neighbor?","a":"Saudi Arabia","o":["Saudi Arabia","Qatar","Iran","Kuwait"],"f":"It is about 25 kilometers long and includes a border island in the middle. Qatar is nearby but has no such road link."}]},{"id":"BHS","name":"Bahamas","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇧🇸","latlng":[24.25,-76],"q":[{"q":"Which sprinter, born in Nassau, won Olympic 400-meter gold in 2016 and again in 2021?","a":"Shaunae Miller-Uibo","o":["Shaunae Miller-Uibo","Allyson Felix","Shelly-Ann Fraser-Pryce","Christine Ohuruogu"],"f":"She dove across the line to win in Rio. Felix is American, Fraser-Pryce is Jamaican, and Ohuruogu is British."},{"q":"Andros Island is known for an unusual number of blue holes. A blue hole is mainly what?","a":"A flooded limestone sinkhole","o":["A flooded limestone sinkhole","A volcanic crater lake","A coral atoll lagoon","A glacial kettle"],"f":"Some open into cave systems below sea level. Divers explore them, and their still water can look a deep blue from above."},{"q":"Most historians place Columbus's first 1492 landfall in the Americas on which Bahamian island, the one he called San Salvador?","a":"Guanahani, today's San Salvador","o":["Guanahani, today's San Salvador","Hispaniola","Cuba","Trinidad"],"f":"A few researchers have argued for other islands, but San Salvador in the eastern Bahamas is the standard identification of Guanahani."},{"q":"Junkanoo, the national street parade, is famous for costumes and music built around which loud instrument?","a":"Goatskin drums","o":["Goatskin drums","Alpine horns","Steel guitars","Bagpipes"],"f":"Parades fill Bay Street in the early hours of Boxing Day and New Year's Day. Cowbells and whistles ride over the drum rhythm."},{"q":"Because the islands are porous limestone, the Bahamas have no natural what?","a":"Rivers","o":["Rivers","Beaches","Coral reefs","Hurricanes"],"f":"Rain sinks quickly into the rock. Fresh water traditionally came from wells and collected rainfall, not from flowing rivers."}]},{"id":"BIH","name":"Bosnia and Herzegovina","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇧🇦","latlng":[44,18],"q":[{"q":"Which writer, born near Travnik, won the 1961 Nobel Prize and set The Bridge on the Drina in Visegrad?","a":"Ivo Andric","o":["Ivo Andric","Ismail Kadare","Mesa Selimovic","Danilo Kis"],"f":"The novel follows a bridge across centuries of Ottoman and Austro-Hungarian rule. Selimovic and Kis are major Yugoslav writers who did not win that prize."},{"q":"The old bridge of Mostar, destroyed in 1993 during the war, was rebuilt and reopened in which year?","a":"2004","o":["2004","1995","2010","1984"],"f":"The original 16th-century Ottoman arch was the work of Mimar Hayruddin. Divers still jump from the rebuilt stone into the Neretva."},{"q":"Sarajevo hosted the Winter Olympics in which year, a decade before the city was besieged?","a":"1984","o":["1984","1976","1992","1968"],"f":"The bobsled track and the Olympic mountains are still above the city. The games were the first Winter Olympics in a socialist country."},{"q":"Stecci are medieval limestone tombstones scattered across Bosnia and Herzegovina. Many are carved with what?","a":"People, animals, and symbols","o":["People, animals, and symbols","Latin Bible verses only","Pharaoh portraits","Viking runes"],"f":"UNESCO lists the stecci cemeteries. They were raised between the 12th and 16th centuries and are not fully explained by one religion."},{"q":"Sevdalinka, or sevdah, is the traditional urban song of Bosnia. It is best known as a slow song about what?","a":"Love and longing","o":["Love and longing","Whale hunting","Harvest boasting","Court trumpet fanfares"],"f":"The word is related to the Turkish sevda, a passionate love. A single singer and a small ensemble are the classic setting."}]},{"id":"BLR","name":"Belarus","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇧🇾","latlng":[53,28],"q":[{"q":"Which painter, born near Vitebsk in 1887, floated fiddlers and goats over village roofs?","a":"Marc Chagall","o":["Marc Chagall","Wassily Kandinsky","Kazimir Malevich","Amedeo Modigliani"],"f":"He left for Paris but kept painting the wooden town of his childhood. Kandinsky and Malevich were different Russian avant-garde painters."},{"q":"Mir Castle, a UNESCO site, was built in the 16th century as a fortress that later became what?","a":"A noble residence","o":["A noble residence","A Buddhist monastery","A naval dockyard","A salt mine"],"f":"It combines Gothic, Renaissance, and Baroque work. It stands in a small town southwest of Minsk and is paired with the Nesvizh palace on the heritage list."},{"q":"Belovezhskaya Pushcha, shared with Poland, sheltered the European bison when the species was nearly gone. The bison was brought back mainly by what method?","a":"Breeding from zoo animals","o":["Breeding from zoo animals","Importing American buffalo","Cloning from fossils","Crossing with cattle"],"f":"The last wild European bison were killed in the early 20th century. A few captive animals founded the herds that were later released into the forest."},{"q":"Draniki, the best-known home dish, are pan-fried cakes of grated what?","a":"Potato","o":["Potato","Buckwheat","Beet","Rye"],"f":"They are served with sour cream. Potatoes became the staple of this cool climate after they spread from the Americas."},{"q":"World War II ruined most of Minsk, so the broad avenues of the center were laid out mainly in which period?","a":"The years after 1945","o":["The years after 1945","The 1600s","The 18th century","The years before 1914"],"f":"About four fifths of the buildings were destroyed. The postwar plan gave the city the wide streets that still define its center."}]},{"id":"BLZ","name":"Belize","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇧🇿","latlng":[17.25,-88.75],"q":[{"q":"Which Garifuna musician, born in Barranco, led a revival of Garifuna music and won a WOMEX award?","a":"Andy Palacio","o":["Andy Palacio","Bob Marley","Ruben Blades","Celia Cruz"],"f":"His album Watina, released in 2007, brought Garifuna songs to listeners worldwide. He died in 2008."},{"q":"The Great Blue Hole, a giant circular sinkhole in the reef off Belize, is roughly how wide?","a":"About 300 meters","o":["About 300 meters","About 30 meters","About 3 kilometers","About 30 kilometers"],"f":"Jacques Cousteau popularized it in 1971. It is a collapsed cave in the limestone, now open to the sea."},{"q":"Caracol was a major Maya city in Belize whose monuments show it once defeated which famous rival?","a":"Tikal","o":["Tikal","Teotihuacan","Cuzco","Tenochtitlan"],"f":"An altar from the 6th century records a victory over Tikal. Caracol's causeways and tallest temple still rise above the forest."},{"q":"The Belize Barrier Reef is part of the Mesoamerican Reef, usually described as the world's second-longest barrier reef after which one?","a":"Australia's Great Barrier Reef","o":["Australia's Great Barrier Reef","The Red Sea reef","The Maldives chain","The Hawaiian chain"],"f":"It runs roughly 300 kilometers and includes atolls and cays. UNESCO lists the reef system, which also faces coral bleaching."},{"q":"In 1797 the British exiled the Garifuna from St. Vincent. Many of their descendants live along the coast of Belize. The Garifuna heritage mixes West African traditions with which Indigenous Caribbean people?","a":"The Kalinago, or Island Carib","o":["The Kalinago, or Island Carib","The Inca","The Maori","The Sami"],"f":"UNESCO recognizes Garifuna language, dance, and music. A signature dish is hudut, fish cooked in coconut broth with mashed plantain."}]},{"id":"BOL","name":"Bolivia","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇧🇴","latlng":[-17,-65],"q":[{"q":"Which Aymara leader, born near Lake Titicaca, besieged La Paz in 1781 during a major uprising against Spanish rule?","a":"Tupac Katari","o":["Tupac Katari","Atahualpa","Lautaro","Moctezuma"],"f":"He took a name that honored earlier rebels Tupac Amaru and Tomas Katari. Bolivia remembers him as a founder of Indigenous resistance."},{"q":"The Salar de Uyuni is the world's largest example of which landscape?","a":"A salt flat","o":["A salt flat","A field of sand dunes","A coral reef","An ice cap"],"f":"After rain, a thin sheet of water turns the surface into a mirror. In many places the salt crust is thick enough to drive on."},{"q":"Tiwanaku, near Lake Titicaca, was a great ceremonial city that flourished centuries before which empire?","a":"The Inca Empire","o":["The Inca Empire","The Mali Empire","The Roman Empire","The Mongol Empire"],"f":"Its Gate of the Sun and tightly fitted stones still stand. The culture was at its height from roughly 500 to 1000 CE."},{"q":"At the Oruro carnival, dancers in horned masks perform which dance about the struggle of good and evil?","a":"The diablada","o":["The diablada","The samba","The hula","The flamenco"],"f":"UNESCO lists the carnival as intangible heritage. The costume mixes Andean mountain spirits with stories from colonial Christian plays."},{"q":"After losing its seacoast in the War of the Pacific, Bolivia kept a navy that trains mainly where?","a":"On Lake Titicaca","o":["On Lake Titicaca","On the Panama Canal","On the Amazon delta","On the Strait of Magellan"],"f":"The war of 1879 to 1884 left the coast in Chilean hands. The navy now serves on lakes and rivers and stands for the lost shoreline."}]},{"id":"BRA","name":"Brazil","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇧🇷","latlng":[-10,-55],"q":[{"q":"Which aviation pioneer, born in Minas Gerais, flew a steerable airship around the Eiffel Tower in 1901?","a":"Alberto Santos-Dumont","o":["Alberto Santos-Dumont","Orville Wright","Louis Bleriot","Ferdinand von Zeppelin"],"f":"In 1906 his 14-bis made a public powered airplane flight in Paris. Brazilians celebrate him as a father of flight."},{"q":"In the rainy season, the white dunes of Lencois Maranhenses fill with thousands of what?","a":"Freshwater lagoons","o":["Freshwater lagoons","Lava pools","Oil seeps","Salt pans"],"f":"Rain sits on a waterproof layer beneath the sand. Fish eggs can survive in the sand and hatch when the pools return."},{"q":"Fleeing Napoleon in 1808, the Portuguese royal court ruled the empire from which Brazilian city?","a":"Rio de Janeiro","o":["Rio de Janeiro","Salvador","Sao Paulo","Recife"],"f":"Salvador had been the older colonial capital, which makes it a tempting wrong answer. Rio stayed Brazil's capital until Brasilia was inaugurated in 1960."},{"q":"Capoeira, created by enslaved Africans in Brazil, mixes fighting, dance, and the rhythm of which instrument?","a":"The berimbau","o":["The berimbau","The bagpipe","The sitar","The didgeridoo"],"f":"The berimbau is a single-string musical bow. The swaying steps can hide powerful kicks."},{"q":"Near Manaus, the dark Rio Negro runs beside the muddy Amazon for kilometers without mixing. They differ in speed, temperature, and what else?","a":"The amount of sediment","o":["The amount of sediment","Their saltiness, like seawater","Their magnetic charge","A cover of ice"],"f":"Travelers call it the meeting of the waters. Farther downstream the two rivers finally blend into one Amazon."}]},{"id":"BRB","name":"Barbados","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇧🇧","latlng":[13.16666666,-59.53333333],"q":[{"q":"Which singer, born Robyn Fenty in Saint Michael, became one of the world's best-known pop artists?","a":"Rihanna","o":["Rihanna","Beyonce","Nicki Minaj","Alicia Keys"],"f":"She was born in 1988 and grew up in Barbados before her career took off in the United States. Beyonce, Minaj, and Keys were born in the United States."},{"q":"In 1751 a 19-year-old George Washington sailed to Barbados, which is notable because it was his only trip where?","a":"Outside mainland North America","o":["Outside mainland North America","Across the Pacific","To Antarctica","Around Africa"],"f":"He came with his ill brother Lawrence and caught smallpox, which later protected him during the Revolutionary War. He never left the mainland again."},{"q":"Mount Gay, on Barbados, traces its rum-making history to which year, making it one of the oldest commercial rum distilleries?","a":"1703","o":["1703","1492","1867","1966"],"f":"Sugar and molasses from the plantations fed the stills. Rum became the island's signature export."},{"q":"Crop Over began as a festival marking the end of which harvest?","a":"The sugar cane harvest","o":["The sugar cane harvest","The grape harvest","The coffee harvest","The apple harvest"],"f":"Plantation workers once celebrated the last canes with singing and a parade. The modern festival revived that tradition with calypso and costume bands."},{"q":"Ships sailing west from Africa often met Barbados first because it is the easternmost island of which arc?","a":"The Caribbean","o":["The Caribbean","The Aleutians","The Philippines","The Azores"],"f":"Trade winds and ocean currents push vessels toward it. That position made Bridgetown a key port in the age of sail."}]},{"id":"BRN","name":"Brunei","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇧🇳","latlng":[4.5,114.66666666],"q":[{"q":"Kampong Ayer, on the Brunei River, is a cluster of houses, schools, and mosques built on what?","a":"Stilts over the water","o":["Stilts over the water","Cliff ledges","Houseboats that sail","Reclaimed desert"],"f":"Europeans called it the Venice of the East. Many families have moved ashore, but thousands of people still live in the water village."},{"q":"Ambuyat, the national dish, is a sticky starch made from the trunk of which plant?","a":"The sago palm","o":["The sago palm","The coconut palm","Cassava","Rice"],"f":"Diners twirl it onto a bamboo fork and dip it in a sour sauce. It has almost no taste of its own, so the sauce does the work."},{"q":"The proboscis monkey, found only on Borneo, is named for the male's large what?","a":"Nose","o":["Nose","Tail fan","Thumb","Ear tufts"],"f":"The nose may amplify calls through the mangrove. The monkey is an excellent swimmer, unusual for a primate, and Brunei's forests are one of its refuges."},{"q":"Brunei regained full independence from Britain in which year?","a":"1984","o":["1984","1957","1963","1971"],"f":"It had been a British protectorate since 1888 and chose not to join Malaysia in 1963. The sultanate itself is centuries older than the protectorate."},{"q":"Since the 20th century the state's wealth has come mainly from oil and which other underground resource?","a":"Natural gas","o":["Natural gas","Coal","Diamonds","Uranium"],"f":"The liquefied gas is shipped mainly to East Asia. That income is why citizens receive extensive public services, including free schooling."}]},{"id":"BTN","name":"Bhutan","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇧🇹","latlng":[27.5,90.5],"q":[{"q":"Taktsang, or the Tiger's Nest, is a monastery clinging to a cliff above Paro. Tradition says a saint flew there on the back of what?","a":"A tigress","o":["A tigress","A snow lion","A dragon","A garuda"],"f":"The saint is Guru Rinpoche, Padmasambhava, who brought Buddhism to the region in the 8th century. The buildings were rebuilt after a fire in 1998."},{"q":"In the 1970s the king promoted a development goal he ranked above income alone. What did he call it?","a":"Gross National Happiness","o":["Gross National Happiness","Gross National Product","The Middle Way economy","Five-year steel plans"],"f":"The idea is that policy should guard culture, environment, and well-being, not only cash. How to measure it is debated, but the phrase is now the country's signature."},{"q":"The national sport is not football. In village contests, teams shoot at small targets with what?","a":"Bows and arrows","o":["Bows and arrows","Blowpipes","Javelins","Slingshots"],"f":"Archery matches are loud, with singing and teasing of the opposing archer. The targets can be more than 100 meters away."},{"q":"Ema datshi, eaten almost daily, is chilies stewed with which other ingredient?","a":"Cheese","o":["Cheese","Yak butter only","Fish sauce","Coconut milk"],"f":"Ema means chili and datshi means cheese. The heat is serious, and the dish is the taste of home for people from every valley."},{"q":"Television and the internet were both banned until a single year near the end of the 20th century. Which year were they allowed?","a":"1999","o":["1999","1972","1986","2008"],"f":"The government feared a sudden flood of foreign images. When the ban lifted, both television and the internet arrived in the same year."}]},{"id":"BWA","name":"Botswana","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇧🇼","latlng":[-22,24],"q":[{"q":"Who married Ruth Williams, was exiled by the British in the 1950s, and became the first president in 1966?","a":"Seretse Khama","o":["Seretse Khama","Jomo Kenyatta","Julius Nyerere","Kenneth Kaunda"],"f":"London disliked the interracial marriage and kept him out for years. He returned to lead independence. Kenyatta, Nyerere, and Kaunda were the independence leaders of Kenya, Tanzania, and Zambia."},{"q":"Water from the Angolan highlands spreads into the Okavango and then stops. Unlike the Zambezi, this inland delta never reaches what?","a":"The ocean","o":["The ocean","The Kalahari","Lake Ngami","A wetland"],"f":"The flood arrives in the dry season and turns the channels green. The delta is a UNESCO site inside the Kalahari, and some water pools at Lake Ngami. There is no outlet to the sea."},{"q":"The Tsodilo Hills, sacred to the San and the Hambukushu, are covered with what?","a":"Thousands of rock paintings","o":["Thousands of rock paintings","Roman mosaics","Soapstone birds","Bronze portrait heads"],"f":"UNESCO lists the hills. There are no captions on the paintings, so meanings stay partly open. Soapstone birds belong to Great Zimbabwe, and the bronze heads to Benin City in Nigeria."},{"q":"Beef boiled until soft, then pounded or shredded, and eaten with maize porridge, is called what?","a":"Seswaa","o":["Seswaa","Biltong","Boerewors","Bobotie"],"f":"The meat is salted and cooked a long time. Biltong is dried meat, boerewors is a sausage, and bobotie is a spiced bake. All three are foods of South Africa, not this dish."},{"q":"Diamonds were found at Orapa in 1967, a year after independence. They come up from the deep Earth in which rock?","a":"Kimberlite","o":["Kimberlite","Sandstone","Marble","Granite"],"f":"A kimberlite pipe is the throat of an ancient volcano. The stones ride up from the mantle. Sandstone, marble, and granite do not carry that cargo."}]},{"id":"CAF","name":"Central African Republic","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇨🇫","latlng":[7,21],"q":[{"q":"UNESCO inscribed, in 2003, the polyphonic singing of which forest people of this country?","a":"The Aka","o":["The Aka","The Maasai","The Dogon","The Himba"],"f":"Several melodies run at once, a rare way of building a song. The same communities live by the Dzanga-Sangha forest. Maasai, Dogon, and Himba are peoples of East Africa, Mali, and Namibia."},{"q":"Which priest and politician, killed in a plane crash in 1959, is remembered as the father of independence?","a":"Barthélemy Boganda","o":["Barthélemy Boganda","Félix Houphouët-Boigny","Léopold Sédar Senghor","Ahmed Sékou Touré"],"f":"Independence from France came in 1960, the year after his death. Houphouët-Boigny, Senghor, and Sékou Touré led Ivory Coast, Senegal, and Guinea."},{"q":"Dzanga Bai, a mineral clearing in the rainforest, draws large gatherings of which animal?","a":"Forest elephants","o":["Forest elephants","Mountain gorillas","Wildebeest","Giraffes"],"f":"Visitors watch from a platform while the elephants dig for salts. Mountain gorillas live on the Virunga volcanoes, far to the east. Wildebeest and giraffes are animals of open savanna."},{"q":"Much of the southern border is a great river that later joins the Congo. What is that river called?","a":"The Ubangi","o":["The Ubangi","The Kasai","The Niger","The Zambezi"],"f":"Barges still move goods on it. The Kasai is another Congo tributary, farther south. The Niger and the Zambezi drain entirely different parts of the continent."},{"q":"The everyday dough of the forest towns is made from a root that must be soaked or fermented before it is safe. Which root?","a":"Cassava","o":["Cassava","Yam","Plantain","Sorghum"],"f":"Bitter cassava contains a toxin that processing removes. The plant came from South America and became the starch of the Congo basin. Yams, plantains, and sorghum do not need that same detox step."}]},{"id":"CAN","name":"Canada","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇨🇦","latlng":[60,-95],"q":[{"q":"Which physician, born in Alliston, Ontario, shared the 1923 Nobel Prize for the discovery of insulin?","a":"Frederick Banting","o":["Frederick Banting","Alexander Fleming","Louis Pasteur","Jonas Salk"],"f":"In 1921 Banting and Charles Best isolated insulin at the University of Toronto. Banting sold the patent rights for one dollar so treatment could spread."},{"q":"The Bay of Fundy, between New Brunswick and Nova Scotia, is famous for the world's highest what?","a":"Tides","o":["Tides","Waterfalls","Sand dunes","Icebergs"],"f":"The difference between high and low tide can exceed 15 meters. At low tide, people walk on harbor floors that ships float over hours later."},{"q":"Which 1867 law of the British Parliament united Ontario, Quebec, Nova Scotia, and New Brunswick into Canada?","a":"The British North America Act","o":["The British North America Act","The Stamp Act","The Quebec Act","The Magna Carta"],"f":"It is now also called the Constitution Act, 1867. Canada later patriated the constitution in 1982, gaining the power to amend it at home."},{"q":"Poutine, a Quebec dish that spread across Canada, is fries and gravy topped with what?","a":"Cheese curds","o":["Cheese curds","Maple candy","Smoked salmon","Wild rice"],"f":"It took shape in rural Quebec in the 1950s. The curds should squeak when fresh, which is how diners judge a good batch."},{"q":"Mainly because of its Arctic archipelago, Canada has the world's longest what?","a":"Coastline","o":["Coastline","Mountain chain","Desert","Coral reef"],"f":"Counting the islands, the shore stretches farther than any other country's. Much of it is icebound for part of the year."}]},{"id":"CHE","name":"Switzerland","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇨🇭","latlng":[47,8],"q":[{"q":"Which mathematician, born in Basel in 1707, is honored in the number e and in a formula linking exponentials and circles?","a":"Leonhard Euler","o":["Leonhard Euler","Carl Friedrich Gauss","Pierre de Fermat","Bernhard Riemann"],"f":"Euler worked in St. Petersburg and Berlin as well as Switzerland. Even after he went blind he kept producing papers."},{"q":"The Matterhorn, the pyramid-shaped peak above Zermatt, was first climbed in 1865 by a party led by whom?","a":"Edward Whymper","o":["Edward Whymper","Edmund Hillary","Reinhold Messner","George Mallory"],"f":"Four men died on the descent. The tragedy made the mountain famous and began the age of guided Alpine climbing."},{"q":"Henry Dunant, shocked by the wounded at Solferino, helped found the Red Cross in Geneva in which decade?","a":"The 1860s","o":["The 1860s","The 1810s","The 1910s","The 1940s"],"f":"The first Geneva Convention was signed in 1864. Dunant shared the first Nobel Peace Prize in 1901."},{"q":"Daniel Peter of Vevey created the first successful milk chocolate bar in the 1870s by mixing cocoa with whose condensed milk?","a":"Henri Nestle","o":["Henri Nestle","Milton Hershey","John Cadbury","Rodolphe Lindt"],"f":"Cocoa butter and milk had been hard to combine without spoiling. Nestle was Peter's neighbor in Vevey, and the two ideas met there."},{"q":"The Congress of Vienna in 1815 recognized Switzerland's permanent what, which the country still claims?","a":"Neutrality","o":["Neutrality","Colonial empire","Papal rule","Union with France"],"f":"Switzerland stays outside military alliances and did not join the European Union. It does host many international organizations in Geneva."}]},{"id":"CHL","name":"Chile","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇨🇱","latlng":[-30,-71],"q":[{"q":"Which poet, born in Vicuna, in 1945 became the first Latin American to win the Nobel Prize in Literature?","a":"Gabriela Mistral","o":["Gabriela Mistral","Pablo Neruda","Gabriel Garcia Marquez","Octavio Paz"],"f":"She had been a rural schoolteacher and diplomat. Another Chilean, Pablo Neruda, won the same prize later, in 1971."},{"q":"The giant stone figures carved on Rapa Nui, a Chilean island in the Pacific, are called what?","a":"Moai","o":["Moai","Tiki","Totem poles","Obelisks"],"f":"Most were cut from volcanic tuff at Rano Raraku. Many stand on platforms called ahu, usually facing inland over a village."},{"q":"The strongest earthquake recorded with modern instruments struck in 1960 near which Chilean city?","a":"Valdivia","o":["Valdivia","Santiago","Antofagasta","Punta Arenas"],"f":"The quake is measured at about magnitude 9.5. The tsunami crossed the Pacific and caused deaths as far away as Japan."},{"q":"Which red-wine grape, thought lost in Europe after the phylloxera plague, was found growing in Chile in 1994 under another name?","a":"Carmenere","o":["Carmenere","Malbec","Pinotage","Tempranillo"],"f":"Growers had long labeled those vines Merlot. A French ampelographer noticed the leaves were actually Carmenere, an old Bordeaux variety."},{"q":"The Atacama, in northern Chile, is famous among non-polar deserts for being the most extreme in which way?","a":"It is the driest","o":["It is the driest","It is the coldest","It is the largest","It has the tallest dunes"],"f":"Some weather stations there have gone years with no rain at all. The clear air is also why astronomers built major observatories nearby."}]},{"id":"CHN","name":"China","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇨🇳","latlng":[35,105],"q":[{"q":"Which scientist, born in Ningbo, won the 2015 Nobel Prize for a malaria drug drawn from sweet wormwood?","a":"Tu Youyou","o":["Tu Youyou","Jane Goodall","Marie Curie","Dorothy Hodgkin"],"f":"She led a secret project in the 1970s that isolated artemisinin from a plant named in an old medical text. The drug is now a standard treatment worldwide."},{"q":"The Terracotta Army, buried near Xi'an, was made to guard the tomb of which ruler who died in 210 BCE?","a":"Qin Shi Huang","o":["Qin Shi Huang","Kublai Khan","Wu Zetian","Confucius"],"f":"He was the first emperor of a unified China. Farmers drilling a well in 1974 found the pits, which hold thousands of life-size soldiers, no two faces exactly alike."},{"q":"Court records credit Cai Lun, around 105 CE, with improving a cheap writing material made from bark, hemp, and rags. What was it?","a":"Paper","o":["Paper","Parchment","Papyrus","Silk only"],"f":"Earlier scraps of paper have been found, so he may have standardized a craft rather than inventing it from nothing. The new sheets spread along the Silk Road centuries later."},{"q":"Confucius, whose teaching shaped schooling and government for centuries, was born in the 6th century BCE in which state?","a":"Lu, near modern Qufu","o":["Lu, near modern Qufu","Qin, near modern Xi'an","Chu, in the south","Qi, on the Shandong coast"],"f":"The Analects were compiled by later students. His idea of rule by moral example, ren, became the backbone of the imperial exam system."},{"q":"The Grand Canal, begun in sections more than a thousand years before it was finished under the Sui and later dynasties, linked the rice of the south to which city in the north?","a":"Beijing","o":["Beijing","Shanghai","Guangzhou","Lhasa"],"f":"It is the longest canal ever built. Grain barges used it to feed the capital, and parts of it still carry water and boats."}]},{"id":"CIV","name":"Ivory Coast","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇨🇮","latlng":[8,-5],"q":[{"q":"Which footballer, born in Abidjan in 1978, captained Chelsea through the club's most successful years?","a":"Didier Drogba","o":["Didier Drogba","Samuel Eto'o","George Weah","Roger Milla"],"f":"In 2005 he and his teammates publicly asked the sides in a civil war to stop fighting, a moment many people here still remember. Eto'o and Milla are Cameroonian, and Weah is Liberian."},{"q":"Grand-Bassam, a UNESCO quarter of verandas beside a lagoon, was the first seat of which administration?","a":"The French colony","o":["The French colony","A Portuguese feitoria","The British Gold Coast","The Belgian Congo"],"f":"Yellow fever pushed the administrators to Bingerville and later to Abidjan. The old French grid is still there. The Gold Coast was Ghana, and the Belgian Congo was the vast colony to the east."},{"q":"The first president, who died in 1993, sponsored a huge basilica in Yamoussoukro modeled on St. Peter's. Who was he?","a":"Félix Houphouët-Boigny","o":["Félix Houphouët-Boigny","Kwame Nkrumah","Léopold Sédar Senghor","Gnassingbé Eyadéma"],"f":"He led the country from independence in 1960 until his death. Claims that the basilica is the world's largest church are disputed, so that ranking is not the question. Nkrumah, Senghor, and Eyadéma led Ghana, Senegal, and Togo."},{"q":"Fermented cassava, steamed into grains like a couscous and served with grilled fish, is called what?","a":"Attiéké","o":["Attiéké","Fufu","Gari","Ugali"],"f":"Lagoon communities, especially the Ebrié and Adioukrou, made it a staple. Gari is also cassava, but toasted and coarser. Fufu is a pounded dough, and ugali is a maize porridge."},{"q":"Akan peoples, including the Baoulé, cast small brass figures used to weigh gold dust. Those figures are called what?","a":"Goldweights","o":["Goldweights","Benin plaques","Royal stools","Dogon ladders"],"f":"Each weight could stand for a proverb as well as a mass. The Ashanti of Ghana used the same system. A stool is a sign of chiefly office, and the Benin plaques are bronze reliefs from Nigeria."}]},{"id":"CMR","name":"Cameroon","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇨🇲","latlng":[6,12],"q":[{"q":"Which striker, born in 1981, became famous at Barcelona and then at Inter Milan?","a":"Samuel Eto'o","o":["Samuel Eto'o","Didier Drogba","George Weah","Roger Milla"],"f":"Milla, born in 1952, was the earlier star from this country, the hero of the 1990 World Cup, not this player. Drogba is Ivorian and Weah is Liberian."},{"q":"Which saxophonist, born in Douala in 1933, recorded Soul Makossa, later heard in the opening of a Michael Jackson hit?","a":"Manu Dibango","o":["Manu Dibango","Fela Kuti","Youssou N'Dour","Hugh Masekela"],"f":"Makossa is a dance music of Douala. Jackson's label later settled with Dibango over the chant in Wanna Be Startin' Somethin'. Fela created Afrobeat in Nigeria, and Masekela was South African."},{"q":"Mount Cameroon, also called Fako, is the highest peak in West Africa. It is also what?","a":"An active volcano","o":["An active volcano","A coral atoll","A peak with permanent glaciers","A meteor crater"],"f":"Eruptions have been recorded into the 21st century. Published heights disagree by tens of meters, which is why there is no number in the question. The summit is far too warm for glaciers."},{"q":"French-ruled Cameroun became independent in 1960. The southern part of the neighboring British territory joined it in which year?","a":"1961","o":["1961","1957","1966","1972"],"f":"The northern part of that British territory voted to join Nigeria instead. A federation later became a unitary state in 1972. Both French and English remain in public life because of that double colonial history."},{"q":"A stew of bitter leaves, peanuts, and meat or shrimp, associated with the Douala coast, is called what?","a":"Ndolé","o":["Ndolé","Jollof rice","Thieboudienne","Injera"],"f":"The leaves are soaked to cut the bitterness. Jollof is a tomato rice shared across West Africa, thieboudienne is the fish rice of Senegal, and injera is the sour flatbread of Ethiopia and Eritrea."}]},{"id":"COD","name":"DR Congo","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇨🇩","latlng":[0,25],"q":[{"q":"Which independence leader, born in Katako-Kombe in 1925, was killed in 1961 months after becoming prime minister?","a":"Patrice Lumumba","o":["Patrice Lumumba","Mobutu Sese Seko","Joseph Kasa-Vubu","Laurent-Désiré Kabila"],"f":"His speech in June 1960 angered the departing Belgians. Mobutu later seized power and ruled for decades. Kasa-Vubu was president and a rival. Kabila came to power only in 1997."},{"q":"A park in the east, home to mountain gorillas and the volcano Nyiragongo, was created in 1925 as Albert National Park. What is it called now?","a":"Virunga National Park","o":["Virunga National Park","Kahuzi-Biega","Garamba","Salonga"],"f":"It is often called Africa's first national park, though older game reserves existed in southern Africa. The other three names are also parks in this country, set aside later."},{"q":"The Congo is not the world's longest river. Among rivers it is the deepest, and it ranks second in what?","a":"Fresh water emptied into the sea","o":["Fresh water emptied into the sea","Number of large dams","Length of its glaciers","Height of its tides"],"f":"Only the Amazon sends more water to the ocean. Depths pass 220 meters in places. The Nile is longer. Glaciers and ocean tides are not this river's claim."},{"q":"A short-necked relative of the giraffe, described by European science only in 1901, lives wild only in the Ituri forest. What is it?","a":"The okapi","o":["The okapi","The bongo","The quagga","The zebu"],"f":"Forest peoples knew it long before that description. The quagga was a southern zebra, now extinct. A bongo is a different striped antelope, and a zebu is a humped cow."},{"q":"Which ape, a separate species from the chimpanzee, lives wild only south of the Congo River and only in this country?","a":"The bonobo","o":["The bonobo","The mountain gorilla","The gibbon","The orangutan"],"f":"Bonobo groups are noted for strong female alliances. Gorillas live in this forest too, but also across the river and in neighboring countries. Gibbons and orangutans are Asian apes."}]},{"id":"COG","name":"Congo","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇨🇬","latlng":[-1,15],"q":[{"q":"Which novelist, born in Pointe-Noire in 1966, wrote Broken Glass and Memoirs of a Porcupine?","a":"Alain Mabanckou","o":["Alain Mabanckou","Sony Labou Tansi","Emmanuel Dongala","Henri Lopes"],"f":"He writes in French and has taught for years in the United States. The other three are major writers of this country too: Tansi wrote Life and a Half, Dongala wrote Johnny Mad Dog, and Lopes wrote Tribaliks."},{"q":"La Sape is a club of elegant dress in Brazzaville. The short name comes from a French phrase meaning what?","a":"Society of ambiance-makers and elegant people","o":["Society of ambiance-makers and elegant people","Society of port engineers","Union of river pilots","Guild of cocoa planters"],"f":"The same style of sharp suits exists in Kinshasa, across the river. It grew in the late colonial decades as a way to claim dignity in public. It is not a trade union."},{"q":"From 1940, Brazzaville was the African center of which wartime movement led by Charles de Gaulle?","a":"Free France","o":["Free France","Vichy France","The Belgian government in exile","The Commonwealth"],"f":"The Brazzaville Conference of 1944 debated the future of the French colonies. Vichy was the regime that collaborated with Germany. This city sided with de Gaulle."},{"q":"Brazzaville looks straight across the Congo River at which city?","a":"Kinshasa","o":["Kinshasa","Luanda","Libreville","Bangui"],"f":"A boat crosses in minutes. Kinshasa, formerly Leopoldville, is in DR Congo. Luanda, Libreville, and Bangui are the main cities of Angola, Gabon, and the Central African Republic."},{"q":"A guitar dance music inscribed by UNESCO in 2021 grew from Cuban son records mixed with local rhythms. What do the bands call it?","a":"Rumba","o":["Rumba","Highlife","Juju","Makossa"],"f":"The full name is Congolese rumba, and the inscription is shared with the country across the river. Soukous is a later, faster child of it. Highlife is Ghanaian, juju is Nigerian, and makossa is from Douala."}]},{"id":"COL","name":"Colombia","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇨🇴","latlng":[4,-72],"q":[{"q":"Which novelist, born in Aracataca, wrote One Hundred Years of Solitude and won the 1982 Nobel Prize in Literature?","a":"Gabriel Garcia Marquez","o":["Gabriel Garcia Marquez","Jorge Luis Borges","Pablo Neruda","Julio Cortazar"],"f":"He set the fictional town of Macondo in a world like the Caribbean coast where he grew up. Readers call the style magical realism."},{"q":"Teyuna, called the Lost City, was built in the Sierra Nevada de Santa Marta by which people?","a":"The Tairona","o":["The Tairona","The Inca","The Mapuche","The Aztec"],"f":"Stone stairways and terraces climb the forested mountains. The city was already centuries old when the Spanish arrived on the coast."},{"q":"Cumbia, now danced across Latin America, began on Colombia's Caribbean coast as a mix of Indigenous, European, and which other tradition?","a":"African drumming and dance","o":["African drumming and dance","Japanese court music","Alpine yodeling","Inuit throat singing"],"f":"Enslaved Africans brought rhythms that met Indigenous flutes and later European instruments. The gaita flute is a classic cumbia sound."},{"q":"For centuries the mines of Muzo and Chivor have been famous for producing fine green what?","a":"Emeralds","o":["Emeralds","Diamonds","Opals","Sapphires"],"f":"Colombia has long been the world's best-known source of top emeralds. Spanish colonists forced Indigenous miners to work the same mountains."},{"q":"El Dorado was not a golden city. The rumor began with a Muisca ritual at Lake Guatavita in which a new ruler was covered in what?","a":"Gold dust","o":["Gold dust","Emerald powder","Salt crystals","Cacao paste"],"f":"He rafted to the middle of the lake and offered gold and emeralds to the gods. Spanish retellings turned the ceremony into a hunt for a city of gold."}]},{"id":"COM","name":"Comoros","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇰🇲","latlng":[-12.16666666,44.25],"q":[{"q":"These islands are a leading source of perfume oil distilled from the flowers of which tree?","a":"Ylang-ylang","o":["Ylang-ylang","Jasmine","Lavender","Damask rose"],"f":"The yellow-green flowers are picked by hand and distilled quickly. The oil sits inside many famous perfumes. Jasmine, lavender, and rose oils are made mainly in other regions."},{"q":"A coelacanth, a fish once thought dead since the age of dinosaurs, was caught off these islands in 1952. Where had the first living one been found, in 1938?","a":"South Africa","o":["South Africa","Madagascar","Mozambique","The Seychelles"],"f":"Marjorie Courtenay-Latimer spotted that 1938 fish in a trawler's catch at East London. The Comoros finds showed there was a real population. Coelacanths are lobe-finned, closer to land animals than most fish."},{"q":"Mount Karthala, which has erupted in living memory and dominates Grande Comore, is what kind of volcano?","a":"A shield volcano","o":["A shield volcano","A coral atoll","A sand dune","A glaciated alp"],"f":"Lava from its wide crater has threatened villages. The islands are volcanic, which is why the slopes can grow perfume trees. There is no glacier and no dune sea here."},{"q":"The islands declared independence from France in which year?","a":"1975","o":["1975","1960","1977","1993"],"f":"Ahmed Abdallah was the central figure of that break. The island of Mayotte stayed under French administration, a dispute this question does not ask you to settle. 1977 was Djibouti's independence, not this one."},{"q":"On Grande Comore, a person traditionally joins the class of notables only after paying for a huge wedding feast. What is that custom called?","a":"The grand marriage","o":["The grand marriage","The gerewol","Famadihana","The reed dance"],"f":"Families can save for years to host it. The gerewol is a Wodaabe festival in Niger, famadihana is a Malagasy ceremony for the dead, and the reed dance is in Eswatini."}]},{"id":"CPV","name":"Cape Verde","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇨🇻","latlng":[16,-24],"q":[{"q":"Which singer, born in Mindelo in 1941, was called the Barefoot Diva and carried morna to the world?","a":"Cesária Évora","o":["Cesária Évora","Miriam Makeba","Angélique Kidjo","Mariza"],"f":"She sang in Cape Verdean Creole, often with no shoes. Morna is a slow, guitar-led song, and UNESCO inscribed the practice in 2019. Makeba was South African, Kidjo is Beninese, and Mariza is a fado singer raised in Portugal."},{"q":"Pico do Fogo erupted in 2014 and buried houses in lava inside its own crater. The mountain is what?","a":"An active volcano","o":["An active volcano","An extinct coral stack","A meteor crater","A glacier-carved peak"],"f":"People of Chã das Caldeiras farm the black soil of the old crater and had to flee that flow. Every island in the country is volcanic."},{"q":"A slow stew of hominy corn, beans, and fish or meat, often fried again the next morning, is called what?","a":"Cachupa","o":["Cachupa","Feijoada","Jollof","Thieboudienne"],"f":"Cachupa rica adds meat; cachupa pobre is the plain pot. Feijoada is the bean stew of Brazil and Portugal, a cousin, not this recipe. Jollof and thieboudienne are tomato rice dishes of the mainland."},{"q":"Amílcar Cabral, the liberation leader often linked to these islands, was in fact born in which country?","a":"Guinea-Bissau","o":["Guinea-Bissau","Portugal","Angola","São Tomé and Príncipe"],"f":"His parents were from these islands, but he was born in Bafatá in 1924. He led the movement for both territories and was assassinated in 1973. Guinea-Bissau's independence was recognized in 1974, and these islands became independent in 1975."},{"q":"When Portuguese sailors reached the archipelago in the 15th century, what did they find?","a":"No one living there","o":["No one living there","A colony of the Mali Empire","Songhai garrisons","Sugar plantations already in production"],"f":"Settlement began soon after, with sugar and, later, a society built on enslaved labor from the mainland. The islands had no earlier population to conquer."}]},{"id":"CRI","name":"Costa Rica","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇨🇷","latlng":[10,-84],"q":[{"q":"Which president, born in Heredia, won the 1987 Nobel Peace Prize for a regional peace plan?","a":"Oscar Arias","o":["Oscar Arias","Rigoberta Menchu","Nelson Mandela","Desmond Tutu"],"f":"His plan helped end civil wars in neighboring countries. He later served a second term as president, long after the prize."},{"q":"After a brief civil war, Costa Rica's 1949 constitution did something almost unique. It abolished what?","a":"The standing army","o":["The standing army","All elections","The court system","Public schools"],"f":"Article 12 forbids a permanent army. The old military budget was shifted toward education and health, a point Costa Ricans still emphasize."},{"q":"Monteverde is a famous reserve of which ecosystem, where trees drip with moisture blown off the Caribbean?","a":"Cloud forest","o":["Cloud forest","Mangrove swamp","Alpine tundra","Hot desert"],"f":"Orchids, quetzals, and howler monkeys live in the mist. The reserve was expanded with help from local Quaker settlers and biologists."},{"q":"Gallo pinto, eaten almost daily, is rice fried with seasoned beans of which two colors in the classic plate?","a":"Black or red beans with white rice","o":["Black or red beans with white rice","Green lentils with barley","Chickpeas with pasta","Soybeans with millet"],"f":"The name means spotted rooster, after the speckled look. Lizano sauce, a tamarind-based condiment, is the local finish."},{"q":"Despite its small size, Costa Rica is often credited with holding about what share of the world's known species?","a":"Around 5 percent","o":["Around 5 percent","Around half","Less than 0.01 percent","Around 40 percent"],"f":"National parks and reserves cover a large part of the country. That variety, from dry Pacific forest to Caribbean rainforest, made it a pioneer of ecotourism."}]},{"id":"CUB","name":"Cuba","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇨🇺","latlng":[21.5,-80],"q":[{"q":"Which poet and organizer, born in Havana in 1853, became the leading writer of Cuba's independence struggle?","a":"Jose Marti","o":["Jose Marti","Simon Bolivar","Ruben Dario","Pablo Neruda"],"f":"He founded the Cuban Revolutionary Party in exile and died in battle in 1895. His verses are still memorized in Cuban classrooms."},{"q":"The Vinales Valley is a landscape of tobacco farms and steep limestone hills that Cubans call what?","a":"Mogotes","o":["Mogotes","Tepuis","Inselbergs","Drumlins"],"f":"The rounded towers are the remains of a limestone plateau. UNESCO listed the valley for both the scenery and the traditional farming."},{"q":"In 1959 a revolution overthrew Fulgencio Batista. Which lawyer, born in Biran, became the new government's central leader?","a":"Fidel Castro","o":["Fidel Castro","Che Guevara","Jose Marti","Fulgencio Batista"],"f":"Guevara, born in Argentina, was a key commander but not the head of state. Castro led Cuba until 2008 and died in 2016."},{"q":"Son cubano, the style that later fed salsa, blends Spanish guitar with African rhythms. A classic son group features bongos, clave, and which guitar-like instrument?","a":"The tres","o":["The tres","The banjo","The sitar","The kora"],"f":"The tres has three pairs of strings. From son came mambo and salsa, which Cuban musicians carried to New York and beyond."},{"q":"The bee hummingbird, found only in Cuba, holds which record among living birds?","a":"It is the smallest","o":["It is the smallest","It is the fastest flier","It is the only flightless one","It is the deepest diver"],"f":"A male can weigh less than two grams, lighter than a coin. Females build a nest about the size of a thimble."}]},{"id":"CYP","name":"Cyprus","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇨🇾","latlng":[35,33],"q":[{"q":"Zeno of Citium, born in what is now Larnaca, founded which school of philosophy in Athens?","a":"Stoicism","o":["Stoicism","Epicureanism","Cynicism","Skepticism"],"f":"He taught in the Stoa Poikile, the painted porch, which gave the school its name. Stoics aimed at virtue and calm in the face of what they could not control."},{"q":"The Latin word for copper, cuprum, comes from the Roman name for Cyprus. The island was famous in antiquity for what?","a":"Copper mines","o":["Copper mines","Tin mines","Diamond pipes","Marble only"],"f":"Aes Cyprium meant metal of Cyprus. Ingots shaped like oxhides were shipped across the Mediterranean in the Bronze Age."},{"q":"Commandaria, a sweet wine from sun-dried grapes, is often called the oldest named wine still made. Crusaders met it on the island. It is named for a region once held by whom?","a":"The Knights Templar and Hospitallers","o":["The Knights Templar and Hospitallers","The Vikings","The Aztecs","The Han dynasty"],"f":"Richard the Lionheart sold Cyprus to the Templars in 1192, and the Hospitallers later held the wine country. The name Commandaria recalls their commandery."},{"q":"Halloumi is a semi-hard cheese traditionally made from sheep and goat milk. Cooks like it because heating makes it do what?","a":"Soften without fully melting","o":["Soften without fully melting","Turn into a liquid sauce","Become sweeter than honey","Double in size"],"f":"That high melting point lets people grill it. Mint is often mixed into the curd, a Cypriot touch."},{"q":"Kourion, on a cliff above the south coast, is famous for a Greco-Roman theater and for mosaics destroyed suddenly in which kind of disaster?","a":"An earthquake","o":["An earthquake","A volcanic eruption","A tsunami from Japan","A sandstorm"],"f":"A great earthquake in 365 CE toppled the city. The cliff-edge theater and the mosaics of later houses are what visitors see today."}]},{"id":"CZE","name":"Czechia","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇨🇿","latlng":[49.75,15.5],"q":[{"q":"Which monk, born in what is now Vrazne, founded genetics by breeding pea plants in a monastery garden?","a":"Gregor Mendel","o":["Gregor Mendel","Charles Darwin","Louis Pasteur","Carl Linnaeus"],"f":"He tracked traits such as tall and short through generations and found they came in predictable ratios. His 1866 paper was ignored until about 1900."},{"q":"The word robot was introduced in a 1920 Prague play, R.U.R. Who wrote that play?","a":"Karel Capek","o":["Karel Capek","Franz Kafka","Isaac Asimov","H. G. Wells"],"f":"His brother Josef suggested robot, from robota, the old word for forced labor. Asimov later wrote the famous laws, but he did not coin the word."},{"q":"Franz Kafka, born in Prague in 1883, wrote in German about baffling authority. Which unfinished novel begins with Josef K. being arrested and never told the charge?","a":"The Trial","o":["The Trial","The Metamorphosis","The Castle","Amerika"],"f":"The Metamorphosis is the story of a man who wakes as an insect, and it was finished. Kafka asked a friend to burn the manuscripts, and the friend published them instead."},{"q":"In November 1989, protests ended one-party rule in a few weeks without a civil war. That change is called what?","a":"The Velvet Revolution","o":["The Velvet Revolution","The Prague Spring","The Singing Revolution","The Orange Revolution"],"f":"The Prague Spring was the 1968 reform movement crushed by invasion. Vaclav Havel, a playwright, became president after 1989."},{"q":"Cesky Krumlov, a UNESCO town, bends around a castle above which river?","a":"The Vltava","o":["The Vltava","The Danube","The Elbe","The Rhine"],"f":"The same river later runs through Prague. The castle's baroque theater still has its original stage machinery."}]},{"id":"DEU","name":"Germany","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇩🇪","latlng":[51,9],"q":[{"q":"Which composer, born in Eisenach in 1685, wrote the Brandenburg Concertos and the St. Matthew Passion?","a":"Johann Sebastian Bach","o":["Johann Sebastian Bach","George Frideric Handel","Ludwig van Beethoven","Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart"],"f":"Handel was born the same year, in Halle, but made his career in London. Beethoven came later and was born in Bonn."},{"q":"Around 1440 in Mainz, Johannes Gutenberg combined movable metal type with a screw press adapted from what machine?","a":"A wine press","o":["A wine press","A waterwheel","A spinning jenny","A steam engine"],"f":"Oil-based ink helped the type print cleanly on paper. Movable type already existed in Asia; his workshop made it practical for the Latin alphabet."},{"q":"In 1886 Karl Benz of Mannheim patented a three-wheeled vehicle recognized as the first practical what?","a":"Automobile","o":["Automobile","Steam locomotive","Airplane","Safety bicycle"],"f":"His wife Bertha drove one from Mannheim to Pforzheim to prove it could travel. Gottlieb Daimler was building high-speed engines nearby at the same time."},{"q":"On 9 November 1989 crowds crossed a barrier that had split a city since 1961. Which barrier?","a":"The Berlin Wall","o":["The Berlin Wall","The Maginot Line","Hadrian's Wall","The Great Wall"],"f":"A muddled announcement that travel rules were changing sent people to the checkpoints that night. Germany was formally reunited in October 1990."},{"q":"Neuschwanstein, the hilltop palace that influenced later fairy-tale castles, was built for which king?","a":"Ludwig II of Bavaria","o":["Ludwig II of Bavaria","Frederick the Great","Charlemagne","Wilhelm II"],"f":"He spent a private fortune on it and slept there only a handful of nights before his death in 1886. The medieval look is 19th-century romance, not a castle that ever fought a siege."}]},{"id":"DJI","name":"Djibouti","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇩🇯","latlng":[11.5,43],"q":[{"q":"In the northern winter, large spotted fish gather to feed in the Gulf of Tadjoura. They strain food from the water and do not hunt people. What are they?","a":"Whale sharks","o":["Whale sharks","Great white sharks","Nile perch","Atlantic salmon"],"f":"The gulf is one of the few places swimmers regularly see them. Great whites are not this gathering. Nile perch are a freshwater fish of the African lakes, and salmon do not run here."},{"q":"Lake Assal, in the Afar depression, is the lowest point in Africa. Its surface sits about how far below sea level?","a":"About 155 meters","o":["About 155 meters","About 15 meters","About 1,550 meters","Level with the sea"],"f":"Salt is cut from the shore in slabs. The lake lies where plates are pulling apart. The Dead Sea is lower, but it is not on this continent."},{"q":"The territory became independent from France in which year?","a":"1977","o":["1977","1960","1967","1993"],"f":"Hassan Gouled Aptidon became the first president. France had called the colony French Somaliland, and later the French Territory of the Afars and the Issas."},{"q":"Ships between the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden pass a narrow strait beside this country. What is the strait called?","a":"Bab el-Mandeb","o":["Bab el-Mandeb","The Strait of Hormuz","The Strait of Gibraltar","The Mozambique Channel"],"f":"The Arabic name means the gate of tears. A large share of world trade squeezes through. Hormuz is the mouth of the Persian Gulf, and Gibraltar is the entrance to the Mediterranean."},{"q":"Geologists study this country because three tectonic plates meet and pull apart. That meeting is called what?","a":"A triple junction","o":["A triple junction","A single transform fault","A passive continental shelf","A meteor scar"],"f":"The African, Arabian, and Somali plates meet in the Afar. That is why the land is splitting, why Lake Assal is so low, and why lava can reach the surface. Iceland is another famous place where plates meet."}]},{"id":"DMA","name":"Dominica","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇩🇲","latlng":[15.41666666,-61.33333333],"q":[{"q":"Which novelist, born in Roseau, retold Jane Eyre from the Caribbean in Wide Sargasso Sea?","a":"Jean Rhys","o":["Jean Rhys","Derek Walcott","Jamaica Kincaid","V. S. Naipaul"],"f":"She left Dominica as a teenager and wrote the novel decades later, in 1966. It gives a history and a voice to Mr. Rochester's first wife."},{"q":"High in Morne Trois Pitons National Park, Dominica's Boiling Lake is a flooded volcanic vent famous for water that does what?","a":"It steams and churns from volcanic heat","o":["It steams and churns from volcanic heat","It freezes in midsummer","It glows from fireflies","It turns solid salt"],"f":"The lake sits in a volcanic crater and is one of the largest boiling lakes on Earth. Landslides have emptied and refilled it more than once."},{"q":"The sisserou, or imperial amazon, is a large parrot found in the wild only in Dominica. What is unusual about its home range?","a":"It is endemic to this one island","o":["It is endemic to this one island","It migrates to Antarctica","It lives only in deserts","It nests on glaciers"],"f":"Habitat loss and hurricanes have made it endangered. It appears beside the flag's design, but the bird itself is the national symbol."},{"q":"A district on Dominica's east coast is reserved for the island's Indigenous community, the largest of its kind in the eastern Caribbean. Which people live there?","a":"The Kalinago","o":["The Kalinago","The Taino of Puerto Rico","The Maori","The Inuit"],"f":"They were long called Caribs, a name many now set aside. The district has its own chief and council."},{"q":"Dominica became independent from Britain in which year?","a":"1978","o":["1978","1962","1981","1995"],"f":"France and Britain traded the island several times before Britain kept it. Dominica chose a president as head of state rather than the British monarch."}]},{"id":"DNK","name":"Denmark","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇩🇰","latlng":[56,10],"q":[{"q":"Which physicist, born in Copenhagen, pictured electrons in set orbits around the nucleus?","a":"Niels Bohr","o":["Niels Bohr","Ernest Rutherford","Marie Curie","Enrico Fermi"],"f":"His 1913 model built on Rutherford's discovery of the nucleus. He later helped scientists flee the Nazi occupation and escaped to Sweden himself."},{"q":"A Billund carpenter coined a company name in 1934 from words meaning play well. What is the company?","a":"LEGO","o":["LEGO","BRIO","Playmobil","Duplo"],"f":"Ole Kirk Christiansen started with wooden toys in 1932. The familiar brick that locks onto the one below was patented in 1958. Duplo is a LEGO product, not a rival firm."},{"q":"Which writer, born in Odense, published The Little Mermaid and The Emperor's New Clothes?","a":"Hans Christian Andersen","o":["Hans Christian Andersen","The Brothers Grimm","Charles Perrault","Soren Kierkegaard"],"f":"The Grimms collected German folk tales, and Perrault wrote earlier French ones. Kierkegaard, another Dane, wrote philosophy, not fairy tales."},{"q":"Kronborg, at Helsingor, is the real castle Shakespeare used as the setting for which play, though he never saw it?","a":"Hamlet","o":["Hamlet","Macbeth","King Lear","Othello"],"f":"In Shakespeare's time the English knew it as Elsinore, a castle that controlled the toll on ships entering the Baltic."},{"q":"Smorrebrod is an open-faced sandwich built on which bread?","a":"Dense rye bread","o":["Dense rye bread","A sweet brioche","A rice cracker","A corn tortilla"],"f":"Butter, fish or meat, and carefully placed toppings make a single slice a full course. The name means buttered bread."}]},{"id":"DOM","name":"Dominican Republic","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇩🇴","latlng":[19,-70.66666666],"q":[{"q":"Which of the country's founders, born in Santo Domingo, led the 1844 movement that separated the nation from Haitian rule?","a":"Juan Pablo Duarte","o":["Juan Pablo Duarte","Simon Bolivar","Jose Marti","Toussaint Louverture"],"f":"Duarte, Francisco del Rosario Sanchez, and Matias Ramon Mella are remembered as the trinity of independence. Duarte spent much of his later life in exile."},{"q":"Pico Duarte, in the Cordillera Central, holds which geographic title in the Caribbean?","a":"The highest peak","o":["The highest peak","The deepest cave","The longest river","The largest desert"],"f":"It rises about 3,098 meters. Older maps called it Pico Trujillo, after a dictator, before the name Duarte was restored."},{"q":"Santo Domingo's colonial zone contains the oldest cathedral and the oldest university in which region?","a":"The Americas","o":["The Americas","Polynesia","Scandinavia","The Sahara"],"f":"The Spanish founded the city in 1496, the first lasting European settlement in the Americas. The Cathedral of Santa Maria la Menor was begun in 1512."},{"q":"Merengue, a national music and dance, is typically driven by which scraping drum?","a":"The guira","o":["The guira","The bodhran","The djembe","The taiko"],"f":"An accordion and a tambora drum join the metal guira. UNESCO lists merengue as intangible heritage, and bachata is the other great local style."},{"q":"The country shares the island of Hispaniola with which neighbor to the west?","a":"Haiti","o":["Haiti","Cuba","Jamaica","Puerto Rico"],"f":"A treaty in 1929, with later adjustments, fixed most of the border. The two countries have very different languages, French Creole and Spanish."}]},{"id":"DZA","name":"Algeria","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇩🇿","latlng":[28,3],"q":[{"q":"Which bishop and philosopher, author of the Confessions, was born in 354 at Thagaste, now Souk Ahras?","a":"Augustine of Hippo","o":["Augustine of Hippo","Tertullian","Cyprian of Carthage","Athanasius"],"f":"He later served as bishop of Hippo, now Annaba, on this same coast. Tertullian and Cyprian belonged to Carthage, in present-day Tunisia. Athanasius was an Egyptian bishop."},{"q":"The Roman grid city of Timgad, a UNESCO site, was founded around the year 100 under which emperor?","a":"Trajan","o":["Trajan","Augustus","Constantine","Justinian"],"f":"Its formal name was Colonia Marciana Traiana Thamugadi. The arch, forum, and library can still be read in the plan. Constantine and Justinian lived centuries later."},{"q":"A popular music of Oran, mixing older local song with Spanish and cabaret influences, is called what?","a":"Raï","o":["Raï","Gnawa","Chaabi","Andalusian nuba"],"f":"Cheikha Rimitti was a pioneer, and Khaled carried the style worldwide late in the 20th century. Gnawa is a Moroccan trance music. Chaabi and the Andalusian nuba are older urban traditions of the Maghreb."},{"q":"Among the countries of Africa, this one ranks first in which measure?","a":"Land area","o":["Land area","Number of people","Length of coastline","Number of islands"],"f":"Most of that area is Sahara. Nigeria has long been the most populous country in Africa, so area and population do not match. The ranking by land does not change from year to year."},{"q":"A war with France, begun in 1954, ended with independence in which year?","a":"1962","o":["1962","1830","1954","1988"],"f":"France had invaded in 1830. The fighting began in 1954, and the Evian agreements brought independence in 1962."}]},{"id":"ECU","name":"Ecuador","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇪🇨","latlng":[-2,-77.5],"q":[{"q":"Which painter, born in Quito, became famous for anguished murals of Indigenous and mestizo suffering?","a":"Oswaldo Guayasamin","o":["Oswaldo Guayasamin","Diego Rivera","Frida Kahlo","Fernando Botero"],"f":"His series The Age of Anger responded to violence in the Americas. Botero, a tempting wrong answer, was Colombian and painted rounded figures."},{"q":"Darwin's observations of finches and tortoises in which Ecuadorian island group later supported his theory of evolution?","a":"The Galapagos Islands","o":["The Galapagos Islands","The Juan Fernandez Islands","The Falkland Islands","The Canary Islands"],"f":"He visited in 1835 aboard HMS Beagle. The islands' isolation had produced species found nowhere else."},{"q":"The straw hats that traders misnamed Panama hats are woven in towns such as Montecristi from the leaves of which plant?","a":"The toquilla palm","o":["The toquilla palm","The coconut palm","Esparto grass","The papyrus reed"],"f":"Merchants shipped the hats through Panama to gold-rush travelers and canal workers, so the wrong country's name stuck."},{"q":"Chimborazo's summit is special because, thanks to Earth's equatorial bulge, it is the farthest point from what?","a":"The center of the Earth","o":["The center of the Earth","The South Pole","The Moon","The International Date Line"],"f":"Aconcagua is still higher above sea level. Measured from the planet's center, Chimborazo wins because the equator sticks out."},{"q":"The best-known Inca ruin in Ecuador, with a temple that may have tracked the sun, is called what?","a":"Ingapirca","o":["Ingapirca","Machu Picchu","Tiwanaku","Chan Chan"],"f":"The Canari people lived here before the Inca expanded north. The fitted green stones of the sun temple are the site's landmark."}]},{"id":"EGY","name":"Egypt","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇪🇬","latlng":[27,30],"q":[{"q":"Which novelist, born in Cairo, won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1988?","a":"Naguib Mahfouz","o":["Naguib Mahfouz","Taha Hussein","Nawal El Saadawi","Tawfiq al-Hakim"],"f":"The Cairo Trilogy follows one family through the old city. Hussein, El Saadawi, and al-Hakim are major writers from this country who did not receive that prize."},{"q":"The temples of Abu Simbel were cut apart and rebuilt on higher ground in the 1960s to escape the lake of which dam?","a":"The Aswan High Dam","o":["The Aswan High Dam","The Hoover Dam","The Three Gorges Dam","The Itaipu Dam"],"f":"UNESCO led an international rescue. Ramses II had the temples carved in the 13th century BCE. The lake behind the dam is Lake Nasser."},{"q":"A stone found at Rashid in 1799 repeated one decree in three scripts and unlocked hieroglyphs. What is it called?","a":"The Rosetta Stone","o":["The Rosetta Stone","The Behistun Inscription","The Mesha Stele","The Palermo Stone"],"f":"Champollion announced the decipherment in 1822. Behistun, in Iran, did the same job for cuneiform. The stone is named for Rashid's European name, Rosetta."},{"q":"The Great Pyramid of Khufu at Giza stayed the world's tallest human-made structure for about how long?","a":"About 3,800 years","o":["About 3,800 years","About 380 years","About 800 years","About 38 years"],"f":"It held that rank from around 2560 BCE until a cathedral spire in Lincoln passed it in the 14th century. The pyramid beside it belongs to Khafre, not to Khufu."},{"q":"A street plate of rice, lentils, pasta, and chickpeas under a tomato sauce is called what?","a":"Koshari","o":["Koshari","Ful medames","Molokhia","Falafel"],"f":"Vendors layer it from separate pots. Ful, stewed fava beans, is the older breakfast. Molokhia is a green soup. Falafel is the fried patty, not the mixed plate."}]},{"id":"ERI","name":"Eritrea","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇪🇷","latlng":[15,39],"q":[{"q":"Asmara's center, a UNESCO site, is preserved as a showcase of which 20th-century building style?","a":"Italian modernism","o":["Italian modernism","Ottoman wooden houses","Mughal marble tombs","Mud-brick mosques of the Sahel"],"f":"Italy occupied the highlands from the 1890s and built the city as a display, nicknamed little Rome. Cinemas, villas, and a Fiat garage are part of the listing."},{"q":"After a long war, a referendum confirmed independence from Ethiopia in which year?","a":"1993","o":["1993","1941","1974","2018"],"f":"Fighters had already taken Asmara in 1991. The 1993 vote made independence formal. 1941 ended Italy's short occupation of Ethiopia, a different date."},{"q":"The country's name comes from the Greek name for which sea on its coast?","a":"The Red Sea","o":["The Red Sea","The Black Sea","The Dead Sea","The Caspian"],"f":"Erythra means red. Italian mapmakers reused the old Greek name for this shore. The Dead Sea and the Caspian are basins far from here."},{"q":"The Dahlak Archipelago, offshore, has long been known to divers and once to pearl divers for what?","a":"Coral and clear salt water","o":["Coral and clear salt water","River dolphins","Alpine lakes","Mangrove tigers"],"f":"The islands sit in water salty enough to sting. Pearl diving is an old trade of the Red Sea, and the reefs are the modern draw."},{"q":"A sour spongy flatbread of teff, shared with Ethiopia and eaten with stews, is called what?","a":"Injera","o":["Injera","Kisra","Chapati","Lahooh"],"f":"Teff is a tiny highland grain. Kisra, in Sudan, is a thinner sorghum bread. The dish crosses the border, so it is not the invention of one modern state. Lahooh is the Somali cousin."}]},{"id":"ESP","name":"Spain","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇪🇸","latlng":[40,-4],"q":[{"q":"Which doctor, born in Petilla de Aragon, shared the 1906 Nobel Prize for showing that the nervous system is made of separate cells?","a":"Santiago Ramon y Cajal","o":["Santiago Ramon y Cajal","Ivan Pavlov","Camillo Golgi","Sigmund Freud"],"f":"He improved Golgi's silver stain and drew neurons by hand. Golgi, who shared the prize, still argued that nerves formed a continuous net."},{"q":"The Alhambra in Granada was the palace of the last Muslim dynasty in Spain. Which dynasty?","a":"The Nasrids","o":["The Nasrids","The Umayyads of Cordoba","The Almohads","The Abbasids"],"f":"Its courtyards, especially the Court of the Lions, use water and carved stucco rather than huge statues. Granada fell to Ferdinand and Isabella in 1492."},{"q":"The cave of Altamira in Cantabria is famous for Ice Age paintings, especially of which animals?","a":"Bison","o":["Bison","Horses only","Whales","Camels"],"f":"When the paintings were found in the 1870s, many experts thought they were too fine to be prehistoric. They are now dated to roughly 14,000 years ago or earlier."},{"q":"Flamenco grew in Andalusia from Romani, Moorish, and local folk music. Its intense sung form is called what?","a":"Cante jondo","o":["Cante jondo","Fado","Sevdah","Rebetiko"],"f":"Cante jondo means deep song. Guitar, dance, and rhythmic clapping, or palmas, join the voice, and UNESCO lists flamenco as heritage."},{"q":"Paella was born as a country dish in which region, cooked over a wood fire in a wide shallow pan?","a":"Valencia","o":["Valencia","Galicia","Andalusia","Catalonia"],"f":"The classic countryside version uses chicken, rabbit, and beans, not a pile of seafood. Saffron turns the rice gold."}]},{"id":"EST","name":"Estonia","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇪🇪","latlng":[59,26],"q":[{"q":"Which composer, born in Paide, wrote spare sacred music such as Spiegel im Spiegel and Tabula Rasa?","a":"Arvo Part","o":["Arvo Part","Jean Sibelius","Edvard Grieg","Henryk Gorecki"],"f":"He calls his later style tintinnabuli, like the sound of a bell. Soviet officials had distrusted his earlier experimental work."},{"q":"The first version of Skype was programmed largely in Tallinn. It was software for what?","a":"Voice and video calls over the internet","o":["Voice and video calls over the internet","A search engine","A mobile operating system","A social photo feed"],"f":"Estonian engineers including Ahti Heinla, Priit Kasesalu, and Jaan Tallinn built it with Swedish and Danish founders. The name was a shortening of sky peer-to-peer."},{"q":"On 23 August 1989 about two million people held hands across Estonia, Latvia, and Lithuania. What was that protest called?","a":"The Baltic Way","o":["The Baltic Way","The Velvet Revolution","The Orange Revolution","The Singing Revolution"],"f":"The date marked the 1939 pact that had assigned the Baltic states to the Soviet sphere. The Singing Revolution is the wider movement of song festivals and protests around it."},{"q":"Tallinn's old town, a UNESCO site, keeps a medieval core of merchants' houses and walls. It prospered as a member of which trading league?","a":"The Hanseatic League","o":["The Hanseatic League","The Delian League","The Hanseatic League of Italy","The East India Company"],"f":"German-speaking merchants dominated the lower town. Toompea hill above it was the seat of bishops and later of Russian governors."},{"q":"The Kaali crater on the island of Saaremaa was blasted out by what?","a":"A meteorite","o":["A meteorite","A volcano","An artillery test","A collapsed salt dome"],"f":"The impact happened a few thousand years ago, recent enough that stories of a burning sky may have entered local legend. Nine smaller craters surround the main lake."}]},{"id":"ETH","name":"Ethiopia","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇪🇹","latlng":[8,38],"q":[{"q":"Which runner won the Olympic marathon at Rome in 1960 while running barefoot?","a":"Abebe Bikila","o":["Abebe Bikila","Haile Gebrselassie","Kenenisa Bekele","Mamo Wolde"],"f":"He won again in Tokyo in 1964, that time in shoes, weeks after an appendix operation. Gebrselassie and Bekele were later stars. Wolde won the marathon in 1968."},{"q":"The churches of Lalibela were not built up from blocks. How were they made?","a":"Carved downward out of solid rock","o":["Carved downward out of solid rock","Cast in concrete","Assembled from imported marble","Woven from reeds"],"f":"King Lalibela, in the 12th or 13th century, is credited with the eleven churches. A stream named Jordan runs among them. Pilgrims still use them."},{"q":"In 1896 an army led by Menelik II and Taytu defeated Italy and stopped a colonial conquest. Which battle was it?","a":"The Battle of Adwa","o":["The Battle of Adwa","The Battle of Magdala","The Battle of Isandlwana","The Battle of Omdurman"],"f":"Italy did occupy the country later, from 1936 to 1941, but 1896 kept it independent through the Scramble. Isandlwana was a Zulu victory, and Omdurman was fought in Sudan."},{"q":"Arabica, the coffee species behind most of the world's cups, still grows wild in which landscape here?","a":"The highland forests","o":["The highland forests","The Danakil salt flats","The coastal mangroves","The open Sahara"],"f":"A story says a goatherd named Kaldi saw his goats frisk after eating the berries. Yemen later made the drink a world trade. The plant's home is these highlands."},{"q":"For much of the year the local church calendar sits how far behind the Gregorian calendar?","a":"Seven or eight years","o":["Seven or eight years","One year","Exactly 100 years","Exactly 550 years"],"f":"New Year falls in September, so the gap switches between seven and eight. The count follows an old reckoning of the Annunciation, not a different length of day."}]},{"id":"FIN","name":"Finland","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇫🇮","latlng":[64,26],"q":[{"q":"Which illustrator and writer, born in Helsinki, created the Moomins?","a":"Tove Jansson","o":["Tove Jansson","Astrid Lindgren","Hans Christian Andersen","Elsa Beskow"],"f":"The first Moomin book appeared in 1945. Jansson later wrote darker fiction for adults, including The Summer Book."},{"q":"Linus Torvalds, born in Helsinki, released the first version of Linux in 1991. Linux is what?","a":"An operating-system kernel","o":["An operating-system kernel","A web browser","A programming language","A search engine"],"f":"He posted it for free and invited others to improve it. It now runs most of the world's supercomputers and a huge share of phones, through Android."},{"q":"The word sauna is Finnish. A traditional smoke sauna is heated by a stove full of stones and has what unusual feature?","a":"No chimney, so smoke fills the room","o":["No chimney, so smoke fills the room","A floor of ice","A pool of seawater inside","Electric lights only"],"f":"Bathers wait until the smoke is vented, then throw water on the hot stones. Public and apartment saunas are everywhere, and the ritual is serious enough to be intangible heritage."},{"q":"Finland declared independence from Russia in December of which year, during the revolution?","a":"1917","o":["1917","1905","1945","1991"],"f":"A bitter civil war followed in 1918. The country stayed independent through the Second World War, though it lost territory to the Soviet Union."},{"q":"Jokamiehenoikeus, the everyman's right, lets people walk, ski, and pick wild berries on land that is what?","a":"Privately owned","o":["Privately owned","Only inside national parks","Only on the coast","Closed in winter"],"f":"You may not disturb residents or damage crops, and camping rules are stricter near houses. Similar rights exist in Norway and Sweden."}]},{"id":"FJI","name":"Fiji","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇫🇯","latlng":[-18,175],"q":[{"q":"Which golfer, born in Lautoka, reached world number one in 2004?","a":"Vijay Singh","o":["Vijay Singh","Tiger Woods","Ernie Els","Rory McIlroy"],"f":"Singh won three major championships, including the Masters in 2000. He was the first Fijian golfer to top the world ranking."},{"q":"On the island of Beqa, a ceremony of the Sawau people involves crossing a pit of heated stones. What is it?","a":"Firewalking","o":["Firewalking","Land diving","Pearl diving","Stick-chart sailing"],"f":"Tradition says a spirit gave the Sawau power over fire. The walk is still performed for special occasions."},{"q":"From 1879 to 1916, Britain brought indentured sugar workers to Fiji mainly from which country?","a":"India","o":["India","China","Portugal","Japan"],"f":"Descendants call that era girmit, from the English word agreement. Indo-Fijians remain one of Fiji's major communities."},{"q":"Which non-alcoholic drink, pounded from a pepper-plant root, is shared in Fiji's formal yaqona ceremony?","a":"Kava","o":["Kava","Mate","Sake","Arrack"],"f":"Kava numbs the lips slightly and is offered to guests as a sign of respect across much of the Pacific."},{"q":"Fiji won the first Olympic gold medal in men's rugby sevens in which year?","a":"2016","o":["2016","2000","2008","1996"],"f":"Rugby sevens debuted at the Rio Olympics. Fiji beat Britain in the final, and the win was celebrated as a national holiday."}]},{"id":"FRA","name":"France","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇫🇷","latlng":[46,2],"q":[{"q":"Which scientist, born in Dole, showed that microbes spoil food and developed the heat treatment named after him?","a":"Louis Pasteur","o":["Louis Pasteur","Robert Koch","Alexander Fleming","Antoine Lavoisier"],"f":"Pasteurization heats wine, milk, or beer enough to kill many germs without boiling the liquid away. He also made vaccines against anthrax and rabies."},{"q":"The Lumiere brothers, born in Besancon, held the first paid public screening of motion pictures in Paris in which year?","a":"1895","o":["1895","1877","1905","1927"],"f":"Their short Workers Leaving the Factory was on the program of 28 December 1895. They had improved a camera and projector into one machine, the cinematograph."},{"q":"The cave of Lascaux in the Dordogne is famous for paintings from about 17,000 years ago, especially of which animals?","a":"Horses, bulls, and deer","o":["Horses, bulls, and deer","Dinosaurs","Whales","Camels"],"f":"Teenagers found the cave in 1940. Visitors' breath damaged the walls, so the original was closed and replica caves were built nearby."},{"q":"On 14 July 1789 a Paris crowd stormed a fortress-prison that held only a few inmates but a store of gunpowder. What was it called?","a":"The Bastille","o":["The Bastille","The Conciergerie","The Louvre","Vincennes"],"f":"The date became France's national day. The revolution that followed ended the absolute monarchy and, in 1793, executed Louis XVI."},{"q":"During the French Revolution, scientists defined a new unit of length as one ten-millionth of the distance from the equator to the pole. What unit?","a":"The meter","o":["The meter","The foot","The league","The cubit"],"f":"The metric system was meant to replace a tangle of local weights. A platinum bar later served as the standard meter until the definition moved to the speed of light."}]},{"id":"FSM","name":"Micronesia","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇫🇲","latlng":[6.91666666,158.25],"q":[{"q":"Nan Madol, off Pohnpei, is a ceremonial city of artificial islets walled with which stone?","a":"Basalt columns","o":["Basalt columns","Marble blocks","Fired brick","Sandstone slabs"],"f":"The Saudeleur dynasty built it between about 1200 and 1500. Some basalt pieces weigh many tons and were moved without metal tools or wheels."},{"q":"On Yap, traditional wealth included huge stone discs quarried in which other island group?","a":"Palau","o":["Palau","Hawaii","Tahiti","Fiji"],"f":"Some rai stones are wider than a person. Ownership can change by public agreement even if a stone sank and cannot be moved."},{"q":"Chuuk Lagoon is a famous dive site because of ships sunk in 1944 during which conflict?","a":"World War II","o":["World War II","The Korean War","World War I","The Vietnam War"],"f":"Operation Hailstone sank much of a Japanese fleet in the lagoon. Divers treat the wrecks as war graves."},{"q":"Sakau, served at welcomes on Pohnpei, is a local form of which Pacific drink?","a":"Kava","o":["Kava","Tea","Coffee","Cider"],"f":"Roots are pounded and strained, then served from a coconut cup. The first cup offered to a guest is a serious welcome."},{"q":"The Federated States of Micronesia are Yap, Chuuk, Pohnpei, and which fourth state?","a":"Kosrae","o":["Kosrae","Guam","Palau","Saipan"],"f":"Guam, the Northern Marianas, and Palau are separate. Kosrae is one high volcanic island, with older ruins at Leluh."}]},{"id":"GAB","name":"Gabon","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇬🇦","latlng":[-1,11.75],"q":[{"q":"At Loango, on the Atlantic, forest elephants and buffalo regularly walk where?","a":"On the beaches","o":["On the beaches","Across glaciers","Through city sewers","Inside a coral lagoon only"],"f":"The coastal park is one of the few places this happens in daylight. These are the smaller forest elephants, not the savanna species."},{"q":"Fang byeri figures were carved to sit on containers holding what?","a":"Bones of ancestors","o":["Bones of ancestors","Millet for the dry season","Gold dust","Copies of the Quran"],"f":"A head or a full figure guarded a bark box of relics. Collectors removed many in the colonial period. They are reliquary guardians, not grain gods."},{"q":"Kongou Falls drops across a broad front inside Ivindo National Park. The setting around the falls is what?","a":"Rainforest","o":["Rainforest","A sand sea","A Mediterranean scrub","A high ice field"],"f":"In 2002 the country announced 13 national parks in one sweep, including Ivindo and the coastal park at Loango. Most of the land is forest."},{"q":"Chicken in a sauce of palm-nut pulp, a dish of Sundays and celebrations, is called what?","a":"Nyembwe chicken","o":["Nyembwe chicken","Jollof rice","Bunny chow","Bobotie"],"f":"The palm pulp gives the sauce its orange color. Jollof is a West African rice, bunny chow is a Durban bread loaf, and bobotie is a South African bake."},{"q":"The country straddles the equator and is covered mostly by which kind of wild land?","a":"Tropical rainforest","o":["Tropical rainforest","Sahara dunes","Fynbos shrub","Afroalpine moor"],"f":"Forest elephants, gorillas, and mandrills live under that canopy. The equator is why the rain is heavy. Fynbos is the shrubland of the Cape, far to the south."}]},{"id":"GBR","name":"United Kingdom","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇬🇧","latlng":[54,-2],"q":[{"q":"Which mathematician, born in London in 1815, wrote notes on Babbage's Analytical Engine that describe what we now call a program?","a":"Ada Lovelace","o":["Ada Lovelace","Charles Babbage","Alan Turing","George Boole"],"f":"She was the daughter of Lord Byron and studied with tutors when few girls did. Babbage designed the machine; her notes saw that it could handle symbols, not only numbers."},{"q":"Stonehenge, on Salisbury Plain, is a ring of giant stones raised mainly in which period?","a":"The late Stone Age and early Bronze Age","o":["The late Stone Age and early Bronze Age","The Roman occupation","The Viking age","The Norman conquest"],"f":"The big sarsen circle went up around 2500 BCE. How the builders moved stones from Wales is still studied, and the site lines up with the midsummer sunrise."},{"q":"In 1215 rebellious barons forced King John to seal a charter limiting royal power. What is that charter called?","a":"Magna Carta","o":["Magna Carta","The Domesday Book","The Bill of Rights","The Act of Union"],"f":"Much of it dealt with feudal fees, but clauses about lawful judgment became a symbol of the rule of law. The original was agreed at Runnymede, by the Thames."},{"q":"Alexander Fleming, born in Darvel in Scotland, noticed in 1928 that a mold killed bacteria in a dish. Which medicine came from that mold?","a":"Penicillin","o":["Penicillin","Aspirin","Insulin","Quinine"],"f":"He published the observation, but the drug became usable only when Howard Florey's team in Oxford learned to purify it in the 1940s. Fleming, Florey, and Chain shared a Nobel Prize."},{"q":"An 1884 conference chose a line through an observatory in London as the world's prime meridian. Which observatory?","a":"Greenwich","o":["Greenwich","Edinburgh","Oxford","Cambridge"],"f":"Longitudes worldwide are counted east or west of that line. The observatory sits on a hill above the Thames, and visitors can stand with a foot in each hemisphere."}]},{"id":"GEO","name":"Georgia","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇬🇪","latlng":[42,43.5],"q":[{"q":"Which painter of animals and feasts, born in Mirzaani, worked as a self-taught sign painter in Tbilisi?","a":"Niko Pirosmani","o":["Niko Pirosmani","Marc Chagall","Ivan Aivazovsky","Kazimir Malevich"],"f":"He often painted on black oilcloth and sometimes took payment in food. His portraits of deer and of ordinary townspeople are now national icons."},{"q":"An 8,000-year-old method ferments wine in large clay jars buried in the ground. What are the jars called?","a":"Qvevri","o":["Qvevri","Amphorae","Pithoi","Dolia"],"f":"Sealed underground, the wine ferments on the skins, which is why many whites come out amber. UNESCO lists the method as intangible heritage."},{"q":"The medieval cave city of Vardzia was carved into a cliff under which queen, as a refuge from invaders?","a":"Tamar","o":["Tamar","Cleopatra","Elizabeth I","Catherine the Great"],"f":"An earthquake in 1283 sheared away the outer rock and exposed the rooms like a dollhouse. A monastery is still active in part of the complex."},{"q":"At a supra, a formal feast, a toastmaster guides dozens of toasts. What is that toastmaster called?","a":"A tamada","o":["A tamada","A khachapuri","A qvevri","A dzamakatsi"],"f":"Guests do not sip at random. Each toast has a theme, from peace to the dead, and draining the glass at the right moment is part of the etiquette."},{"q":"The Soviet leader Joseph Stalin was born in 1878 in which Georgian town?","a":"Gori","o":["Gori","Batumi","Kutaisi","Yerevan"],"f":"His birth name was Ioseb Jughashvili. A museum in Gori still stands on the street of his childhood house, and Georgians argue over how to present it."}]},{"id":"GHA","name":"Ghana","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇬🇭","latlng":[8,-2],"q":[{"q":"Which UN secretary-general, born in Kumasi in 1938, shared the 2001 Nobel Peace Prize?","a":"Kofi Annan","o":["Kofi Annan","Boutros Boutros-Ghali","U Thant","Dag Hammarskjöld"],"f":"He served from 1997 to 2006, and the prize was shared with the UN itself. Boutros-Ghali was his predecessor, from Egypt. U Thant was from Myanmar, and Hammarskjöld was Swedish."},{"q":"Elmina Castle, begun in 1482 and later a notorious slaving fort, was first built by whom?","a":"Portugal","o":["Portugal","Spain","Belgium","Italy"],"f":"It is among the oldest European buildings still standing south of the Sahara. The Dutch, then the British, held it later. Cape Coast Castle, nearby, is the great British fort. Both are memorials of the trade."},{"q":"Kente, bright strips sewn into a single cloth, was woven for the courts of which people?","a":"The Ashanti","o":["The Ashanti","The Yoruba","The Zulu","The Berbers"],"f":"Narrow looms make the strips. The cloth was once for royalty and great occasions. Yoruba, Zulu, and Berber textiles are real, and they are not kente."},{"q":"At independence the country took the name of a medieval gold empire. Where did that empire actually lie?","a":"Far northwest, in today's Mali and Mauritania","o":["Far northwest, in today's Mali and Mauritania","On this same coast","In the Nile valley","In southern Africa"],"f":"Medieval Ghana, or Wagadou, taxed the gold and salt trades of the western Sahel. Kwame Nkrumah chose the name in 1957 as a link to that fame. The old kingdom was not this territory."},{"q":"Independence from Britain, led by Kwame Nkrumah, came in which year?","a":"1957","o":["1957","1956","1960","1966"],"f":"Sudan had become independent in 1956, so this was not the first independence in Africa. Nkrumah was overthrown in 1966. 1960 was the year many French colonies left."}]},{"id":"GIN","name":"Guinea","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇬🇳","latlng":[11,-10],"q":[{"q":"In 1958 this was the only French territory to vote no to de Gaulle's new community. Who led that vote?","a":"Ahmed Sékou Touré","o":["Ahmed Sékou Touré","Léopold Sédar Senghor","Félix Houphouët-Boigny","Modibo Keïta"],"f":"He said people preferred freedom in poverty to comfort in chains. France pulled its staff out at once, and independence came that same year. The other three led neighbors that joined the community and left in 1960."},{"q":"The Fouta Djallon highlands are called a water tower because they feed the headwaters of which rivers?","a":"The Niger, the Senegal, and the Gambia","o":["The Niger, the Senegal, and the Gambia","The Nile, the Congo, and the Zambezi","The Orange and the Limpopo","The Congo and the Ubangi"],"f":"Rain on the plateau runs west and north and then east with the Niger's great bend. None of these streams is a source of the Nile."},{"q":"A goblet-shaped drum played with bare hands, shared with Mali and its neighbors, is called what?","a":"The djembe","o":["The djembe","The tabla","The bodhrán","The taiko"],"f":"It is a Mandé instrument, so it is not the property of one modern border. Tabla, the bodhrán, and taiko belong to India, Ireland, and Japan."},{"q":"The Baga people of the coast dance a tall shoulder mask of a guardian spirit. What is that mask called?","a":"Nimba","o":["Nimba","Chi wara","Ekpe","Gelede"],"f":"It is also called D'mba. Helpers guide the dancer, who can barely see. Chi wara is a Bamana antelope mask from Mali. Ekpe and Gelede are Nigerian masquerades."},{"q":"Mount Nimba, a reserve shared with Ivory Coast and Liberia, is famous among biologists for a toad that does what?","a":"Gives birth to live young","o":["Gives birth to live young","Flies south for the winter","Spits venom","Builds a nest of sticks"],"f":"The Nimba toad, Nimbaphrynoides, skips the free-swimming tadpole. Iron mining has threatened the mountain. The reserve sits on three borders."}]},{"id":"GMB","name":"Gambia","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇬🇲","latlng":[13.46666666,-16.56666666],"q":[{"q":"Kunta Kinteh Island, formerly James Island, is a UNESCO site remembering what?","a":"The Atlantic slave trade","o":["The Atlantic slave trade","A Phoenician copper port","A Viking shipyard","A Chinese treasure fleet"],"f":"Captives were held in the fort before the ocean crossing. Alex Haley set a scene here in Roots, but a novel is not proof of one family's paperwork. The island was renamed in 2011."},{"q":"Among mainland countries in Africa, this one is the smallest by what measure?","a":"Land area","o":["Land area","Number of people","Annual rainfall","Number of languages"],"f":"It is a ribbon on both banks of one river, inside Senegal, with only a short coast. Island countries such as Seychelles are smaller still. Area, unlike population, does not change each year."},{"q":"Britain and France drew the borders in 1889 as a strip about 10 miles to each side of the river. The strip was meant to follow what?","a":"The river upstream from the sea","o":["The river upstream from the sea","A line of latitude only","The crest of the Atlas","An old Roman road"],"f":"The shape is why the country looks like a finger pushed into Senegal. The 10-mile rule relaxed near the coast so the port could function. It is a diplomatic line, not a natural ridge."},{"q":"A peanut stew with tomato, served with rice, is called what?","a":"Domoda","o":["Domoda","Yassa","Egusi","Thieboudienne"],"f":"Groundnuts were the colonial cash crop and stayed in the pot. Senegal's mafé is a close cousin. Yassa is lemon and onion, and egusi is a melon-seed stew from Nigeria."},{"q":"A 21-string harp-lute of Mandinka griots, played here and in Mali, is called what?","a":"The kora","o":["The kora","The oud","The sitar","The banjo"],"f":"A gourd and a long neck carry the strings. Players such as Toumani Diabaté are Malian, so the instrument is regional. West African lutes influenced the banjo, but a banjo is not a kora."}]},{"id":"GNB","name":"Guinea-Bissau","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇬🇼","latlng":[12,-15],"q":[{"q":"Which liberation leader was born in Bafatá in 1924 and assassinated in 1973, before independence?","a":"Amílcar Cabral","o":["Amílcar Cabral","Samora Machel","Agostinho Neto","Eduardo Mondlane"],"f":"He founded the PAIGC and fought for both this country and Cape Verde. His parents' roots were in Cape Verde, but his birthplace was Bafatá. Machel, Neto, and Mondlane led the struggles in Mozambique and Angola."},{"q":"Portugal recognized independence in which year, after the revolution in Lisbon?","a":"1974","o":["1974","1963","1975","1994"],"f":"The PAIGC had declared independence in 1973, while Cabral was already dead. Cape Verde, the partner territory, became independent in 1975."},{"q":"The Bijagós islands are a biosphere reserve. A noted custom there is that important rights pass through whom?","a":"The maternal line","o":["The maternal line","The Portuguese governor","The eldest son only","Foreign trading companies"],"f":"Women hold a decisive place in inheritance and in ritual houses. It is too simple to call the islands the only matriarchy, but the maternal line is central. Mangroves and turtles are why the biosphere exists."},{"q":"Hippos on Orango, in the Bijagós, are unusual because they spend time in what?","a":"Salt water","o":["Salt water","Snowfields","Desert caves","The high canopy"],"f":"Most hippos stay in fresh water. These swim the channels between the islands. The archipelago is also a nesting ground for sea turtles."},{"q":"Besides the mainland, the country includes a large offshore group. What is that group called?","a":"The Bijagós","o":["The Bijagós","The Canaries","Cape Verde","Zanzibar"],"f":"Dozens of islands scatter through the channels, many of them inside the biosphere. The Canaries, Cape Verde, and Zanzibar are other island worlds, not this archipelago."}]},{"id":"GNQ","name":"Equatorial Guinea","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇬🇶","latlng":[2,10],"q":[{"q":"Schools and government here use a European language that no other African member of the United Nations uses in that role. Which language?","a":"Spanish","o":["Spanish","Portuguese","Italian","German"],"f":"Spain joined the island of Bioko to the mainland territory of Río Muni. Portuguese is the language of the former Portuguese colonies. French is the common language of the neighbors."},{"q":"The mainland is Río Muni. The large volcanic island that holds Malabo is called what?","a":"Bioko","o":["Bioko","Annobón","São Tomé","Príncipe"],"f":"Under Spanish rule Bioko was Fernando Poo. Annobón is a smaller, distant island that is also part of the country. São Tomé and Príncipe are a separate state."},{"q":"Independence from Spain came in which year?","a":"1968","o":["1968","1956","1975","1990"],"f":"Francisco Macías Nguema became the first president. The Portuguese colonies in Africa became independent around 1975, not in 1968."},{"q":"Under Spanish rule, plantations on Bioko were famous for which crop, grown with forced labor?","a":"Cocoa","o":["Cocoa","Cloves","Tea","Dates"],"f":"Workers were brought from the mainland and from abroad. Cocoa is still grown, but offshore oil later dominated the economy. Cloves were Zanzibar's crop."},{"q":"Bioko rises from the same volcanic line as Mount Cameroon, just offshore. The island itself is best described as what?","a":"A volcanic island","o":["A volcanic island","A coral atoll","A sandspit","A granite mesa"],"f":"The peak is an extinct or quiet cone in a chain that includes the active volcano on the mainland. Malabo sits on its northern side. It is not a flat atoll."}]},{"id":"GRC","name":"Greece","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇬🇷","latlng":[39,22],"q":[{"q":"In the 5th century BCE, Athens developed a system in which free male citizens voted directly on laws. What is that system called?","a":"Democracy","o":["Democracy","Oligarchy","Monarchy","Theocracy"],"f":"Women, enslaved people, and foreigners could not vote. Even so, the assembly on the Pnyx hill is the root of the word democracy, rule by the people."},{"q":"The Parthenon on the Acropolis was a temple dedicated chiefly to which goddess?","a":"Athena","o":["Athena","Hera","Artemis","Aphrodite"],"f":"It was built in the 440s and 430s BCE under Pericles. A huge gold and ivory statue of Athena once stood inside, and the outer sculptures are now partly in Athens and partly in London."},{"q":"The ancient Olympic Games were held every four years at Olympia in honor of which god?","a":"Zeus","o":["Zeus","Apollo","Poseidon","Ares"],"f":"Tradition dates the first games to 776 BCE. Wars were supposed to pause, and the prize was an olive wreath, not money."},{"q":"Tragedy, as a form of theater, developed in Athens from choral songs at festivals of which god?","a":"Dionysus","o":["Dionysus","Hermes","Hades","Hephaestus"],"f":"Aeschylus, Sophocles, and Euripides competed there in the 5th century BCE. Actors wore masks, and a chorus commented on the story."},{"q":"The modern marathon race honors a messenger tradition linked to which Greek victory over the Persians in 490 BCE?","a":"The Battle of Marathon","o":["The Battle of Marathon","The Battle of Thermopylae","The Battle of Salamis","The Battle of Plataea"],"f":"A later writer said a runner reached Athens with the news and then died. That dramatic ending is probably legend, but the battle itself was real, and the race keeps its name."}]},{"id":"GRD","name":"Grenada","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇬🇩","latlng":[12.11666666,-61.66666666],"q":[{"q":"Which sprinter, born in Gouyave, won Olympic 400-meter gold in 2012, the country's first Olympic title?","a":"Kirani James","o":["Kirani James","Usain Bolt","Shaunae Miller-Uibo","Michael Johnson"],"f":"He was 19 when he won in London. Bolt is Jamaican, Miller-Uibo is Bahamian, and Johnson is American."},{"q":"Nutmeg and mace, Grenada's signature spices, are two products of one fruit. Mace is which part?","a":"The red lacy covering of the seed","o":["The red lacy covering of the seed","The tree's root","The flower's pollen","The bark"],"f":"The seed itself is nutmeg. One fruit therefore yields two spices, which is why Grenada is nicknamed the Spice Isle."},{"q":"Oil down, often called the national dish, is a one-pot stew of salted meat, coconut milk, and which starchy fruit?","a":"Breadfruit","o":["Breadfruit","Plantain only","Mango","Ackee"],"f":"Everything cooks in one pot until the liquid is absorbed, or down. Dumplings and callaloo often go in too."},{"q":"Grenada gained independence from Britain in which year?","a":"1974","o":["1974","1962","1981","1958"],"f":"Eric Gairy became the first prime minister. In 1983 a violent split in the government led to a U.S.-led invasion, a still-debated episode."},{"q":"The island of Grenada is volcanic. Its highest point, Mount Saint Catherine, and the lake-filled crater of Grand Etang are evidence of what kind of past?","a":"Volcanic eruptions","o":["Volcanic eruptions","Glaciers scraping the rock","A meteor impact last century","Coral growing on a desert"],"f":"Grand Etang is a crater lake in the high forest. Hikers still use trails that cross the old volcanic peaks."}]},{"id":"GTM","name":"Guatemala","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇬🇹","latlng":[15.5,-90.25],"q":[{"q":"Which K'iche' activist, born in Chimel, won the 1992 Nobel Peace Prize for defending Indigenous rights?","a":"Rigoberta Menchu","o":["Rigoberta Menchu","Malala Yousafzai","Wangari Maathai","Oscar Arias"],"f":"Her prize drew attention to the civil war's violence against Maya communities. She has remained a public advocate since."},{"q":"Tikal's temples rise above the Peten forest. In the Classic period this Maya city was a rival of which other great Maya power, in Mexico?","a":"Calakmul","o":["Calakmul","Cuzco","Tenochtitlan","Chan Chan"],"f":"Tikal's tallest temple, Temple IV, is over 60 meters high. Its carved stelae record wars and accessions of kings."},{"q":"The resplendent quetzal, with long green tail plumes, was sacred to the Maya as a symbol of what?","a":"Freedom and preciousness","o":["Freedom and preciousness","The underworld ocean","Winter storms","Desert winds"],"f":"Tail feathers were used in royal costume and could not be bought cheaply. The bird still lives in cloud forests, and its name was given to the currency."},{"q":"Pepian, a festive stew, is built on toasted seeds and chilies. Which seeds are the usual base?","a":"Pumpkin and sesame seeds","o":["Pumpkin and sesame seeds","Pine nuts and almonds","Sunflower seeds only","Coffee beans"],"f":"Every family has a version, often with chicken or beef. The sauce is thick, not soupy, and served with rice or tamales."},{"q":"Semuc Champey is a series of turquoise pools sitting on a natural limestone bridge over which river?","a":"The Cahabon River","o":["The Cahabon River","The Amazon","The Mississippi","The Rio Grande"],"f":"The river itself dives underground, and the stepped pools on the bridge are fed by smaller springs. It is one of the country's best-known natural sights."}]},{"id":"GUY","name":"Guyana","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇬🇾","latlng":[5,-59],"q":[{"q":"Which writer, born in Georgetown, told of teaching in a tough London school in the memoir To Sir, With Love?","a":"E. R. Braithwaite","o":["E. R. Braithwaite","V. S. Naipaul","Derek Walcott","Chinua Achebe"],"f":"He later served as a diplomat. The book became a well-known film, but the classroom story began with his own life."},{"q":"Kaieteur Falls, on the Potaro River, is famous for a single enormous drop surrounded by what kind of forest?","a":"Rainforest","o":["Rainforest","Mangrove swamp only","Pine taiga","Date-palm oasis"],"f":"The Potaro drops about 226 meters in one plunge. Swifts nest behind the sheet of water."},{"q":"Schools and courts in Guyana work mainly in which language, unlike in Brazil or Suriname?","a":"English","o":["English","Dutch","Portuguese","French"],"f":"The country was British Guiana until independence in 1966. Suriname uses Dutch, and French Guiana is part of France, not a separate country."},{"q":"Pepperpot, a Christmas classic, keeps meat in a dark stew made from boiled-down juice of which root?","a":"Cassava","o":["Cassava","Potato","Ginger","Sugar beet"],"f":"The boiled juice is called cassareep. Families traditionally kept a pot going, adding meat and reheating it through the holidays."},{"q":"The country's name comes from an Indigenous word usually translated as land of many what?","a":"Waters","o":["Waters","Mountains","Gold mines","Volcanoes"],"f":"Rivers such as the Essequibo, Demerara, and Berbice drain a land that is mostly rainforest. Demerara also gave its name to a type of brown sugar."}]},{"id":"HND","name":"Honduras","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇭🇳","latlng":[15,-86.5],"q":[{"q":"Which 19th-century leader, born in Tegucigalpa, tried to reunite the countries of Central America and is a national hero?","a":"Francisco Morazan","o":["Francisco Morazan","Simon Bolivar","Jose de San Martin","Benito Juarez"],"f":"He was the last president of the Federal Republic of Central America and was executed in Costa Rica in 1842. His birthday is a holiday."},{"q":"Copan, a Maya city near the Guatemalan border, is especially famous for sculpture and for a staircase covered in what?","a":"Hieroglyphs","o":["Hieroglyphs","Gold leaf","Roman mosaics","Chinese porcelain"],"f":"The Hieroglyphic Stairway has the longest known Maya inscription. Copan's kings also raised finely carved stelae of themselves."},{"q":"The Bay Islands, including Roatan and Utila, sit on which reef system?","a":"The Mesoamerican Reef","o":["The Mesoamerican Reef","The Great Barrier Reef","The Red Sea reef","The Maldives atolls"],"f":"English is widely spoken on the islands because of British settlement and later English-speaking islanders. Utila is a well-known scuba stop."},{"q":"A baleada, everyday street food, is a wheat-flour tortilla folded around mashed beans, cream, and often what else?","a":"Cheese and eggs","o":["Cheese and eggs","Raw fish and lime","Maple syrup","Seaweed"],"f":"The name may come from a woman nicknamed Baleada who sold them. They are Honduras's closest thing to a national snack."},{"q":"In 1998 Hurricane Mitch stalled over Honduras and became one of the most destructive Atlantic hurricanes on record. It was especially deadly because of what on the steep slopes?","a":"Floods and landslides","o":["Floods and landslides","Lava flows","Blizzards","Sand dunes burying cities"],"f":"Whole neighborhoods of Tegucigalpa were swept away along the Choluteca River. Recovery and the map of some towns changed for good."}]},{"id":"HRV","name":"Croatia","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇭🇷","latlng":[45.16666666,15.5],"q":[{"q":"Which inventor, born in Smiljan in 1856, pioneered alternating-current electricity and the induction motor?","a":"Nikola Tesla","o":["Nikola Tesla","Thomas Edison","Guglielmo Marconi","Michael Faraday"],"f":"He was an ethnic Serb born in the Austrian Empire, in the village that is now in Croatia, and later worked in the United States. His ashes are kept in Belgrade."},{"q":"The modern necktie traces back to 17th-century soldiers from this region serving in France. The French word cravate comes from their name. What did they wear?","a":"A knotted neckerchief","o":["A knotted neckerchief","A feathered helmet","A tartan kilt","A metal gorget only"],"f":"Louis XIV's court copied the style. The soldiers were called Croats, and cravate is the French reshaping of that word."},{"q":"Dubrovnik's stone walls, a UNESCO site, enclosed a republic that rivaled Venice in the Adriatic. For most of its golden age that republic was called what?","a":"Ragusa","o":["Ragusa","Spalato","Zadar","Kotor"],"f":"Ragusa paid tribute at times but kept its own senate and ships. The old name survives in the word argosy, a rich merchant vessel, from Ragusa."},{"q":"Plitvice Lakes are a chain of turquoise pools dropped one into another by barriers of what?","a":"Travertine, a limestone deposited by the water","o":["Travertine, a limestone deposited by the water","Beaver dams","Coral reefs","Glacial moraines"],"f":"Moss and minerals build the barriers, so the waterfalls slowly change shape. The park is a UNESCO natural site."},{"q":"The Dalmatian dog takes its name from Dalmatia, a coastal region of Croatia. The breed was long used in Europe as what?","a":"A carriage dog","o":["A carriage dog","A sled dog","A herding dog only","A temple guardian"],"f":"Spotted dogs ran beside horse-drawn carriages, which is why firehouses later kept them. The breed standard, not a Croatian law, fixed the spots."}]},{"id":"HTI","name":"Haiti","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇭🇹","latlng":[19,-72.41666666],"q":[{"q":"Which leader of the Haitian Revolution, born enslaved on Saint-Domingue, died in a French prison in 1803?","a":"Toussaint Louverture","o":["Toussaint Louverture","Henri Christophe","Jean-Jacques Dessalines","Simon Bolivar"],"f":"He had turned the slave uprising into a disciplined army and forced France to abolish slavery on the island. Napoleon had him seized during negotiations."},{"q":"The Citadelle Laferriere, a huge hilltop fort and UNESCO site, was built under which early king?","a":"Henri Christophe","o":["Henri Christophe","Louis XIV","Henry VIII","Kamehameha I"],"f":"It was meant to resist a French attempt to restore slavery. Tens of thousands of workers hauled cannon and stone up the mountain after independence."},{"q":"On 1 January 1804 Haiti declared independence. It is remembered as the first nation born from which kind of successful uprising?","a":"A revolution of enslaved people","o":["A revolution of enslaved people","A gold rush","A peasant revolt in Europe","A sailors' mutiny"],"f":"The army defeated French forces sent by Napoleon. The new state was the first Black-led republic in the modern world."},{"q":"Soup joumou, pumpkin soup eaten on Independence Day, is significant because during slavery it was what?","a":"Reserved for the French colonists","o":["Reserved for the French colonists","Eaten only at sea","Forbidden as too spicy","A drink, not a soup"],"f":"After 1804, formerly enslaved people cooked it to mark freedom. It is now the taste of New Year's morning."},{"q":"Haitian Vodou developed on the plantations from West and Central African religions meeting which other tradition?","a":"Catholicism","o":["Catholicism","Shinto","Lutheran pietism","Sikhism"],"f":"Spirits called lwa are honored with drumming, songs, and offerings. In 2003 the state recognized Vodou as a religion alongside others."}]},{"id":"HUN","name":"Hungary","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇭🇺","latlng":[47,20],"q":[{"q":"Which designer, born in Budapest, invented a twisting cube puzzle in 1974?","a":"Erno Rubik","o":["Erno Rubik","Laszlo Biro","Harry Houdini","John von Neumann"],"f":"He was a professor of architecture and built the cube to teach three-dimensional movement. It became a worldwide craze after 1980. Houdini and von Neumann were also born in Budapest."},{"q":"Laszlo Biro, born in Budapest, patented a pen that writes with a tiny ball. What does the ball do?","a":"It rolls ink onto the paper","o":["It rolls ink onto the paper","It erases graphite","It punches Braille","It sprays perfume"],"f":"He and his brother developed it in the 1930s after noticing newspaper ink dried fast. Production later took off in Argentina, where Biro had fled the war."},{"q":"Goulash, the national stew, is flavored above all by which ground red spice?","a":"Paprika","o":["Paprika","Saffron","Turmeric","Cinnamon"],"f":"Paprika is dried pepper, brought from the Americas and later grown on the Hungarian plain. A proper goulash is soupier than many foreign versions."},{"q":"In October 1956 a popular uprising in Budapest demanded reform and was crushed by which army?","a":"The Soviet army","o":["The Soviet army","The German army","The Yugoslav army","The Romanian army"],"f":"Imre Nagy, the reform prime minister, was later executed. The revolt failed, but it showed how fragile Soviet control of Eastern Europe could be."},{"q":"Budapest's famous baths, such as Szechenyi, are fed by natural hot water. The city sits on a large supply of what?","a":"Thermal springs","o":["Thermal springs","Glacial meltwater","Artesian oil","Desalinated seawater"],"f":"Romans already bathed at Aquincum, the ancient city under Obuda. Buda and Pest, on opposite banks of the Danube, were united as one city in 1873."}]},{"id":"IDN","name":"Indonesia","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇮🇩","latlng":[-5,120],"q":[{"q":"Borobudur, a huge 9th-century monument in Java, is a stepped pyramid of which religion?","a":"Buddhism","o":["Buddhism","Hinduism","Islam","Christianity"],"f":"It was buried under volcanic ash and jungle until the 19th century. Pilgrims walk clockwise up the terraces toward a central stupa."},{"q":"The Komodo dragon, the largest living lizard, lives wild only on a few islands here. Besides teeth, its bite delivers what?","a":"Venom","o":["Venom","A constricting tail","Electric shock","A spray of acid"],"f":"For years the dangerous bite was blamed only on bacteria in the saliva. Researchers later found venom glands. A dragon can take down deer and water buffalo."},{"q":"In 1883 a volcano in the Sunda Strait exploded, darkened skies worldwide, and killed tens of thousands. What was it called?","a":"Krakatoa","o":["Krakatoa","Tambora","Merapi","Fuji"],"f":"Most deaths came from the tsunamis, not the ash. Tambora, in 1815, was an even larger eruption on a different island."},{"q":"Batik, listed by UNESCO, makes patterns on cloth by drawing or stamping with what before dyeing?","a":"Hot wax","o":["Hot wax","Cold mud","Gold leaf","Raw silk thread"],"f":"The wax resists the dye, and boiling later removes it. Courts in Java developed extremely fine styles that once only nobles could wear."},{"q":"A gamelan is an orchestra built mostly around which family of instruments?","a":"Bronze gongs and metallophones","o":["Bronze gongs and metallophones","Bowed violins","Bagpipes","Steel drums"],"f":"Players think of the whole set as one instrument, not a collection of soloists. Different islands keep different tuning systems."}]},{"id":"IND","name":"India","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇮🇳","latlng":[20,77],"q":[{"q":"Which physicist, born in Tiruchirappalli, won the 1930 Nobel Prize for showing how light changes color when it scatters?","a":"C. V. Raman","o":["C. V. Raman","Satyendra Nath Bose","Homi Bhabha","Abdus Salam"],"f":"The color of the sea and the sky helped lead him to the effect now called Raman scattering. Bose, of Bose-Einstein statistics, never received that prize."},{"q":"The Taj Mahal was built in the 17th century by Shah Jahan as a tomb for whom?","a":"His wife Mumtaz Mahal","o":["His wife Mumtaz Mahal","His mother Nur Jahan","A Hindu saint","A Persian poet"],"f":"It took about 20 years and white marble from Makrana. The four minarets lean slightly outward so that in an earthquake they would fall away from the dome."},{"q":"The game of chess developed from an Indian strategy game played with four armies. What was that game called?","a":"Chaturanga","o":["Chaturanga","Go","Mancala","Pachisi"],"f":"Chaturanga means four limbs, matching the pieces of an ancient army: infantry, cavalry, elephants, and chariots. It passed through Persia, where it became shatranj."},{"q":"In 1930 Mohandas Gandhi, born in Porbandar, marched to the sea to make salt and break a tax. That march is named for which town on the coast?","a":"Dandi","o":["Dandi","Goa","Pondicherry","Kochi"],"f":"The British salt monopoly made it illegal to gather salt. The 24-day march became a model of nonviolent mass protest."},{"q":"Indian mathematicians, including Brahmagupta in the 7th century, treated zero as a number you can calculate with, not only as a blank. That idea later traveled west through which scholars?","a":"Arabic-writing mathematicians","o":["Arabic-writing mathematicians","The ancient Greeks","The Maya alone","Roman engineers"],"f":"The Maya also used a zero, independently. The numeral system used almost everywhere today is the one that passed from India through Baghdad to Europe."}]},{"id":"IRL","name":"Ireland","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇮🇪","latlng":[53,-8],"q":[{"q":"Which scientist, born at Lismore Castle in 1627, showed that the pressure of a gas rises as its volume shrinks?","a":"Robert Boyle","o":["Robert Boyle","Isaac Newton","Robert Hooke","Antoine Lavoisier"],"f":"Boyle's law is that relationship, when temperature stays the same. He also helped found the Royal Society in London and argued for careful experiments."},{"q":"Newgrange, a passage tomb in the Boyne Valley, is older than the pyramids at Giza. About when was it built?","a":"Around 3200 BCE","o":["Around 3200 BCE","Around 1200 BCE","Around 500 BCE","Around 800 CE"],"f":"At midwinter sunrise a beam of light enters the passage and lights the chamber. The carvings of spirals on the kerbstones are older than any writing in Ireland."},{"q":"From 1845 to about 1852 a blight destroyed the crop that poor families depended on, and a million people died. Which crop?","a":"The potato","o":["The potato","Wheat","Oats","Flax"],"f":"The blight was a microorganism, Phytophthora infestans. Death and emigration cut the island's population sharply, and the politics of the famine are still debated."},{"q":"Which novelist, born in Dublin, set Ulysses on a single day, 16 June 1904?","a":"James Joyce","o":["James Joyce","W. B. Yeats","Samuel Beckett","Oscar Wilde"],"f":"Fans call that date Bloomsday, after Leopold Bloom. Yeats, Beckett, and Wilde were also Irish writers, but not the author of Ulysses."},{"q":"Samhain, an old festival marking the start of winter, is the ancestor of which modern holiday?","a":"Halloween","o":["Halloween","Christmas","Easter","May Day"],"f":"It fell around 1 November, when the boundary with the otherworld was thought to thin. Later Christian feasts of All Saints and All Souls landed on the same days."}]},{"id":"IRN","name":"Iran","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇮🇷","latlng":[32,53],"q":[{"q":"Which mathematician, born in Tehran, in 2014 became the first woman to win the Fields Medal?","a":"Maryam Mirzakhani","o":["Maryam Mirzakhani","Emmy Noether","Hypatia","Tu Youyou"],"f":"She worked on the geometry of surfaces and died in 2017 at 40. The Fields Medal is often described as the highest prize in mathematics."},{"q":"Persepolis, burned after Alexander's invasion, was the ceremonial seat of which empire?","a":"The Achaemenid Empire","o":["The Achaemenid Empire","The Sassanian Empire","The Ottoman Empire","The Parthian Empire"],"f":"Darius I began the great terrace in the 6th century BCE. Reliefs of gift-bearers from many lands line the stairways."},{"q":"Nowruz, the new year marked at the spring equinox, is kept with a table of seven items whose names begin with the letter S. The festival is older than which religion that later adopted it?","a":"Islam","o":["Islam","Zoroastrianism","Buddhism","Christianity"],"f":"It was already a major feast under the Achaemenids and is linked to Zoroastrian tradition. Families still grow sabzeh, sprouted greens, for the table."},{"q":"A qanat is an underground channel that brings water from a hillside aquifer to a dry plain. The shafts you see on the surface were dug for what?","a":"Construction and ventilation","o":["Construction and ventilation","Storing grain","Mining copper","Trapping birds"],"f":"Some Iranian qanats are more than 2,000 years old and still flow. UNESCO lists a group of them as a way of sharing scarce water without pumps."},{"q":"Ferdowsi, born near Tus, spent decades writing the Shahnameh. That book is an epic of what?","a":"Persian kings and heroes","o":["Persian kings and heroes","The life of the Buddha","A voyage to India","The laws of Hammurabi"],"f":"He wrote it in Persian at a time when Arabic dominated scholarship, which helped keep the language literary. The hero Rostam is its most famous figure."}]},{"id":"IRQ","name":"Iraq","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇮🇶","latlng":[33,44],"q":[{"q":"Which architect, born in Baghdad in 1950, became famous for sweeping concrete curves such as the Guangzhou Opera House?","a":"Zaha Hadid","o":["Zaha Hadid","Ieoh Ming Pei","Tadao Ando","Frank Gehry"],"f":"She was the first woman to win the Pritzker Prize, in 2004. She died in 2016, and many of her buildings opened only after her death."},{"q":"In southern Mesopotamia, Sumerian scribes pressed signs into wet clay. That writing system is called what?","a":"Cuneiform","o":["Cuneiform","Hieroglyphs","Linear B","Devanagari"],"f":"The name means wedge-shaped, from the mark of a reed stylus. It began as pictures for trade around 3400 BCE and later recorded laws and poems."},{"q":"The Ziggurat of Ur, partly restored, was a stepped temple platform in the city of the moon god. Which god?","a":"Nanna, or Sin","o":["Nanna, or Sin","Marduk","Enlil","Ishtar"],"f":"Ur was a Sumerian capital on the Euphrates. The brick core is ancient, and the lower facing was rebuilt in the 20th century."},{"q":"The Epic of Gilgamesh, known from clay tablets in Mesopotamia, follows a king who seeks what after his friend dies?","a":"Immortality","o":["Immortality","The golden fleece","A route to India","The philosopher's stone"],"f":"A flood story in the epic is older than the one in Genesis and resembles it. The most complete tablets come from the library of Ashurbanipal in Nineveh."},{"q":"The Ma'dan, or Marsh Arabs, lived among reed houses in the wetlands where the Tigris and Euphrates meet. Their meeting halls were built of what?","a":"Giant reeds","o":["Giant reeds","Fired brick","Mud brick only","Palm trunks"],"f":"The marshes shrank sharply in the late 20th century when rivers were diverted, then partly reflooded. UNESCO now lists the wetland landscape."}]},{"id":"ISL","name":"Iceland","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇮🇸","latlng":[65,-18],"q":[{"q":"Which novelist, born in Reykjavik, won the 1955 Nobel Prize and wrote Independent People?","a":"Halldor Laxness","o":["Halldor Laxness","Sigrid Undset","Knut Hamsun","Sjón"],"f":"Independent People follows a stubborn sheep farmer. Laxness wrote in Icelandic and argued that a small language could still hold a world literature."},{"q":"The Althing, founded around 930 at Thingvellir, is one of the oldest surviving what?","a":"Parliaments","o":["Parliaments","Universities","Stock exchanges","Navies"],"f":"Chiefs met in the open rift valley, where the Eurasian and North American plates pull apart. The modern parliament in Reykjavik still uses the old name."},{"q":"The island of Surtsey rose from the sea in eruptions that began in which year?","a":"1963","o":["1963","1947","1973","1996"],"f":"Scientists kept the new island almost free of visitors so they could watch life arrive on bare lava. Moss and birds colonized it within a few years."},{"q":"Iceland has a coast guard but, unusually for a country, it has no standing what?","a":"Army","o":["Army","Police","Court system","Fishing fleet"],"f":"Defense agreements with NATO and the United States filled the gap during the Cold War. The coast guard is famous instead for the cod disputes with Britain."},{"q":"The medieval sagas, written mainly in the 13th century, tell in plain prose of feuds among which earlier settlers?","a":"Viking-age farmers","o":["Viking-age farmers","Roman legionaries","Celtic druids","Hanseatic merchants"],"f":"Njals Saga and Egil's Saga read almost like novels. They were composed long after the events, so historians treat them as literature first and archives second."}]},{"id":"ISR","name":"Israel","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇮🇱","latlng":[31.47,35.13],"q":[{"q":"Which violinist, born in Tel Aviv in 1945, became a celebrated soloist after a childhood bout of polio?","a":"Itzhak Perlman","o":["Itzhak Perlman","Jascha Heifetz","Pinchas Zukerman","Yehudi Menuhin"],"f":"He uses crutches or a scooter to reach the chair and then plays seated. Heifetz and Menuhin were born in the Russian Empire and in New York."},{"q":"The Dead Sea shore is the lowest dry land on Earth. Its water is famous for being so salty that a swimmer does what?","a":"Floats with little effort","o":["Floats with little effort","Sinks faster than in the ocean","Can drink it safely","Sees it freeze in summer"],"f":"The salt is several times that of the ocean, so the water is dense. The surface has been falling as rivers are diverted and minerals are extracted."},{"q":"In 73 or 74 CE, after a Roman siege, the last defenders of a desert fortress killed themselves rather than be enslaved. Which fortress?","a":"Masada","o":["Masada","Herodium","Megiddo","Beersheba"],"f":"The story comes mainly from the historian Josephus. Archaeologists found the Roman ramp still leaning against the cliff."},{"q":"Hebrew had survived as a language of prayer and books. In the late 19th century a scholar pushed it back into kitchens and streets. Who was he?","a":"Eliezer Ben-Yehuda","o":["Eliezer Ben-Yehuda","Theodor Herzl","Maimonides","Rashi"],"f":"He raised his son to hear only Hebrew at home. Modern Hebrew's everyday vocabulary, from ice cream to newspaper, owes a great deal to that experiment."},{"q":"In 1947 shepherds found jars of ancient manuscripts in caves near the Dead Sea. Those texts are known as what?","a":"The Dead Sea Scrolls","o":["The Dead Sea Scrolls","The Nag Hammadi library","The Cairo Geniza","The Rosetta Stone"],"f":"They include the oldest known copies of many Hebrew Bible books, hidden around the 1st century CE. The Nag Hammadi books are Christian texts found in Egypt."}]},{"id":"ITA","name":"Italy","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇮🇹","latlng":[42.83333333,12.83333333],"q":[{"q":"Which scientist, born in Pisa in 1564, used a telescope to see Jupiter's moons and defended the idea that Earth moves around the Sun?","a":"Galileo Galilei","o":["Galileo Galilei","Nicolaus Copernicus","Johannes Kepler","Giordano Bruno"],"f":"Copernicus was Polish and Kepler German; Galileo brought the new astronomy into public view in Italian. The Inquisition forced him to recant in 1633, and he spent his last years under house arrest."},{"q":"In 79 CE Mount Vesuvius buried Pompeii under a surge of hot gas and stone. The city was a Roman town near which modern city?","a":"Naples","o":["Naples","Rome","Florence","Venice"],"f":"Ash preserved bakeries, graffiti, and the holes where bodies had been. Plaster casts made from those holes show people as they fell."},{"q":"Modern Italy became a single kingdom in 1861, but Rome joined only in 1870. Before that, the peninsula was what?","a":"A patchwork of separate states","o":["A patchwork of separate states","One empire ruled from Constantinople","A French province","A single republic since ancient times"],"f":"Piedmont-Sardinia led the unification, with Garibaldi's volunteers in the south. The pope lost Rome and did not recognize the new state until the Lateran Treaty of 1929."},{"q":"Opera, as a sung drama with orchestra, was invented around 1600 in which city, by a group called the Camerata?","a":"Florence","o":["Florence","Milan","Venice","Naples"],"f":"They wanted to revive ancient Greek tragedy. Monteverdi, working soon after in Mantua and Venice, turned the experiment into a public art."},{"q":"Modern pizza, a flatbread with tomato, developed in the streets of which city?","a":"Naples","o":["Naples","Rome","Turin","Genoa"],"f":"Tomatoes, an American plant, were poor people's food before they met bread and cheese. A famous 1889 pie for Queen Margherita used tomato, mozzarella, and basil, the colors of the new flag."}]},{"id":"JAM","name":"Jamaica","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇯🇲","latlng":[18.25,-77.5],"q":[{"q":"Which organizer, born in St. Ann's Bay in 1887, founded the Universal Negro Improvement Association?","a":"Marcus Garvey","o":["Marcus Garvey","Martin Luther King Jr.","W. E. B. Du Bois","Nelson Mandela"],"f":"He urged people of African descent worldwide to take pride in their history and build their own institutions. Jamaica named him its first national hero."},{"q":"Blue Mountain coffee is grown on steep slopes in the east of the island. The range is named for the blue haze, and the beans are known for what?","a":"A mild flavor and high price","o":["A mild flavor and high price","Being naturally caffeine-free","Growing under snow","A bitter smoked taste"],"f":"Cool mist and rich soil slow the ripening of the cherries. Japan has long been one of the biggest buyers of the certified beans."},{"q":"In the 1700s communities of escaped enslaved people, the Maroons, forced Britain to sign peace treaties. Which Maroon leader is a national hero?","a":"Queen Nanny","o":["Queen Nanny","Nzinga","Harriet Tubman","Yaa Asantewaa"],"f":"Nanny Town, in the Blue Mountains, was a Maroon stronghold. The treaties gave the Maroons land and a degree of self-rule."},{"q":"Reggae grew in Kingston in the late 1960s out of ska and which slightly slower style?","a":"Rocksteady","o":["Rocksteady","Mambo","Tango","Highlife"],"f":"The offbeat guitar chop and a heavy bass became the signature. UNESCO added reggae to its intangible heritage list in 2018."},{"q":"The red-billed streamertail, a hummingbird found only in Jamaica, is locally nicknamed what?","a":"The doctor bird","o":["The doctor bird","The sisserou","The quetzal","The bee hummingbird"],"f":"Males trail long black tail feathers. One folk explanation of the nickname is that the forked tail looks like the old coat-tails of a doctor."}]},{"id":"JOR","name":"Jordan","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇯🇴","latlng":[31,36],"q":[{"q":"Petra's most famous facade, Al-Khazneh, was carved from the cliff by which ancient people?","a":"The Nabataeans","o":["The Nabataeans","The Romans","The Pharaohs","The Persians"],"f":"They grew rich taxing the incense caravans. The name Treasury is a later legend about a pharaoh's gold, not the building's real use."},{"q":"In 1812 a Swiss traveler entered Petra disguised as a pilgrim and became the first European in centuries to describe it. Who was he?","a":"Johann Ludwig Burckhardt","o":["Johann Ludwig Burckhardt","Richard Burton","Ibn Battuta","Gertrude Bell"],"f":"He had learned Arabic and traveled as Sheikh Ibrahim. His notes revealed the city to European scholars, though local people had never forgotten it."},{"q":"Wadi Rum is a desert of sandstone mountains. Film crews use it because the red sand and cliffs can stand in for what?","a":"The surface of Mars","o":["The surface of Mars","The Amazon rainforest","The Alps","A coral reef"],"f":"Scenes in The Martian and earlier films were shot there. The valley is also full of ancient rock carvings and Bedouin camps."},{"q":"Mansaf, the feast dish, is lamb cooked in a dried yogurt sauce and served on flatbread with rice. The dried yogurt is called what?","a":"Jameed","o":["Jameed","Labneh","Kishk","Ayran"],"f":"Jameed is yogurt salted and dried into hard balls, then reconstituted. Guests traditionally eat from the shared platter with the right hand."},{"q":"The Nabataeans farmed a desert by cutting channels and cisterns into the rock. Their skill was mainly in collecting what?","a":"Rare rainwater","o":["Rare rainwater","Nile floodwater","Melted snow","Water from deep oil wells"],"f":"Hidden dams slowed flash floods and filled cisterns. That water system, more than gold, made Petra possible."}]},{"id":"JPN","name":"Japan","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇯🇵","latlng":[36,138],"q":[{"q":"Which physicist, born in Tokyo, predicted a particle later called the meson and won the 1949 Nobel Prize?","a":"Hideki Yukawa","o":["Hideki Yukawa","Shinichiro Tomonaga","Leo Esaki","Richard Feynman"],"f":"He proposed the particle in 1935 to explain how the nucleus holds together. Tomonaga, also Japanese, shared a later Nobel for other work in physics."},{"q":"Mount Fuji, a cone visible from far across the Kanto plain, last erupted in which year?","a":"1707","o":["1707","1868","1923","1945"],"f":"The Hoei eruption threw ash as far as Edo, now Tokyo. The mountain is a sacred site in both Shinto and Buddhist tradition, and a UNESCO cultural site."},{"q":"In 1868 the Tokugawa shogunate fell and the emperor was restored to the center of politics. That change is called what?","a":"The Meiji Restoration","o":["The Meiji Restoration","The Taisho Democracy","The Genpei War","The Boshin War"],"f":"Meiji means enlightened rule. The Boshin War was the civil war fought during the change, not the name of the new order. Within decades the government built railways, a conscript army, and a constitution."},{"q":"The Tokaido Shinkansen, opened in 1964 for the Tokyo Olympics, was the world's first regular what?","a":"High-speed railway","o":["High-speed railway","Underground metro","Maglev line","Electric tram"],"f":"Trains ran at about 210 kilometers an hour, far beyond ordinary expresses of the time. The name means new trunk line."},{"q":"A formal tea gathering, chanoyu, uses a powdered green tea whisked with hot water. What is that tea called?","a":"Matcha","o":["Matcha","Sencha","Hojicha","Genmaicha"],"f":"Sencha is steeped leaf tea, the everyday drink. The tea ceremony grew from Zen monasteries and values quiet, season, and the handmade bowl."}]},{"id":"KAZ","name":"Kazakhstan","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇰🇿","latlng":[48,68],"q":[{"q":"Wild apple forests in the Tian Shan are the main ancestor of the apples sold in supermarkets. Botanists call that wild apple what?","a":"Malus sieversii","o":["Malus sieversii","Malus sylvestris","Pyrus communis","Prunus armeniaca"],"f":"Malus sieversii still grows in the mountains of the southeast. Genetic studies show that domestic apples descend chiefly from it. Malus sylvestris is the European crab apple, a lesser contributor."},{"q":"Yuri Gagarin's 1961 flight left Earth from a cosmodrome on the steppe that is leased to Russia. What is the site called?","a":"Baikonur","o":["Baikonur","Plesetsk","Cape Canaveral","Jiuquan"],"f":"The town of Baikonur was a decoy name; the pads are nearer the settlement once called Leninsk. The lease to Russia runs well into the 21st century."},{"q":"A burial found at Issyk in 1969 held a young warrior covered in thousands of gold pieces. The figure is known as what?","a":"The Golden Man","o":["The Golden Man","The Golden Fleece","The Amber Prince","The Jade Emperor"],"f":"The costume is Saka, from the Iron Age nomads of the steppe. A copy of the tall headdress became a national symbol."},{"q":"Beshbarmak, a feast dish, is boiled meat served over wide noodles. The name means what?","a":"Five fingers","o":["Five fingers","Three fires","White gold","Long road"],"f":"Guests traditionally ate it with their hands. The head of a sheep can be served with parts offered to guests by age and status."},{"q":"Among countries with no seacoast, this one is the largest by area. It stretches from the Caspian Sea, which is a lake, to which mountains?","a":"The Altai and the Tian Shan","o":["The Altai and the Tian Shan","The Andes","The Alps","The Atlas"],"f":"The Caspian is salty and has no outlet to the ocean, so it does not count as a seacoast. The steppe between the mountains was the highway of the Silk Road."}]},{"id":"KEN","name":"Kenya","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇰🇪","latlng":[1,38],"q":[{"q":"Which environmentalist, born in Nyeri in 1940, founded the Green Belt Movement and won the Nobel Peace Prize in 2004?","a":"Wangarĩ Maathai","o":["Wangarĩ Maathai","Rigoberta Menchú","Shirin Ebadi","Malala Yousafzai"],"f":"Rural women planted trees against erosion and defended public forests. Menchú, Ebadi, and Yousafzai are laureates from Guatemala, Iran, and Pakistan."},{"q":"The second-highest peak in Africa, after Kilimanjaro, stands in this country. It is an old what?","a":"Volcano","o":["Volcano","Sandstone fold","Meteor crater","Coral plateau"],"f":"The highest summit is Batian, on Mount Kenya. Glaciers there have been shrinking, so this question does not ask you to count them. Kilimanjaro is across the border in Tanzania."},{"q":"A 1950s revolt against British rule and land losses, strongest among Kikuyu fighters, is known as what?","a":"The Mau Mau uprising","o":["The Mau Mau uprising","The Maji Maji war","The Herero war","The Anglo-Zulu war"],"f":"The British held many people in camps. Independence came in 1963 under Jomo Kenyatta. Maji Maji was in German East Africa, and the Herero war was in German South West Africa."},{"q":"Many famous distance runners, including Kipchoge Keino, come from which highland community of the Rift Valley?","a":"The Kalenjin","o":["The Kalenjin","The Swahili of the coast","The Somali of the northeast","The Luo of the lake"],"f":"Keino's Olympic golds in 1968 and 1972 helped start a tradition of high-altitude training. Other communities produce athletes too. This is a pattern, not an exclusive rule."},{"q":"Swahili grew along this coast as a Bantu language heavily mixed with words from which other language?","a":"Arabic","o":["Arabic","Portuguese","Dutch","Chinese"],"f":"Traders of the Indian Ocean mixed the languages over centuries. The grammar stayed Bantu. Portuguese came later and left fewer everyday words."}]},{"id":"KGZ","name":"Kyrgyzstan","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇰🇬","latlng":[41,75],"q":[{"q":"Which novelist, born in Sheker, wrote Jamila and The Day Lasts More Than a Hundred Years?","a":"Chingiz Aitmatov","o":["Chingiz Aitmatov","Leo Tolstoy","Orhan Pamuk","Naguib Mahfouz"],"f":"He wrote in both Kyrgyz and Russian and brought nomadic life to readers abroad. Jamila, a love story set in a wartime village, was praised by Louis Aragon."},{"q":"The Manas epic, recited by specialist bards, tells of a hero who unites the tribes. Those bards are called what?","a":"Manaschi","o":["Manaschi","Akyns","Ashiks","Griots"],"f":"A full recitation can last many hours, and some versions run to hundreds of thousands of lines. UNESCO lists the epic as intangible heritage."},{"q":"Issyk-Kul is a huge mountain lake that rarely freezes. The main reason is that the water is what?","a":"Slightly salty","o":["Slightly salty","Heated by a volcano","Covered in oil","Stirred by ships"],"f":"The name means hot lake, though the water is cold. It has no outlet, so minerals stay behind as water evaporates."},{"q":"A traditional round tent of wooden lattice and felt, still used on high pastures, is called what?","a":"A yurt, or boz uy","o":["A yurt, or boz uy","A teepee","A lavvu","A chum"],"f":"Mongolian speakers call the same kind of tent a ger. The crown ring, the tunduk, is a national emblem. A yurt can be packed onto animals and raised again in an afternoon."},{"q":"The Tian Shan, or Celestial Mountains, form the backbone of the country. Their highest peak here is which one?","a":"Jengish Chokusu, or Victory Peak","o":["Jengish Chokusu, or Victory Peak","K2","Everest","Lenin Peak is lower"],"f":"Jengish Chokusu reaches about 7,439 meters on the border with China. It is also called Tomur by Chinese climbers."}]},{"id":"KHM","name":"Cambodia","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇰🇭","latlng":[13,105],"q":[{"q":"Angkor Wat was built in the 12th century as a temple to Vishnu and later became a Buddhist shrine. Which king began it?","a":"Suryavarman II","o":["Suryavarman II","Jayavarman VII","Norodom Sihanouk","Yasovarman I"],"f":"Jayavarman VII, a later king, built the nearby city of Angkor Thom and the faces of the Bayon. Angkor Wat itself remains the largest religious monument on Earth."},{"q":"Between 1975 and 1979 the Khmer Rouge emptied the cities and renamed the country Democratic Kampuchea. Their leader was who?","a":"Pol Pot","o":["Pol Pot","Norodom Sihanouk","Lon Nol","Hun Sen"],"f":"About a quarter of the population died from execution, hunger, and overwork. The Tuol Sleng prison and the killing fields are now memorials."},{"q":"Classical dancers bend the hands back and tell stories of spirits and kings. That court dance is called what?","a":"Apsara dance","o":["Apsara dance","Kecak","Kathak","Legong"],"f":"The poses copy carvings of heavenly dancers on the temples of Angkor. The tradition was nearly wiped out in the 1970s and later rebuilt by surviving teachers."},{"q":"The Tonle Sap River does something almost unique once a year, when the Mekong floods. What happens?","a":"It reverses and fills the lake","o":["It reverses and fills the lake","It freezes solid","It turns to salt","It disappears underground"],"f":"The lake can grow several times in area, and the flooded forest feeds one of the richest freshwater fisheries in Asia. When the rains end, the river runs back toward the sea."},{"q":"Khmer, the national language, is written in a script descended from which South Asian writing system?","a":"Brahmi, via southern Indian scripts","o":["Brahmi, via southern Indian scripts","Chinese characters","Arabic script","Cyrillic"],"f":"The script arrived with Hindu and Buddhist scholarship more than a thousand years ago. It has many more letters than it needs for modern sounds, a trace of that history."}]},{"id":"KIR","name":"Kiribati","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇰🇮","latlng":[1.41666666,173],"q":[{"q":"Kiribati is the only country whose own islands lie in all four of what?","a":"Hemispheres","o":["Hemispheres","Oceans","Climate zones","Continental plates"],"f":"The Gilbert, Phoenix, and Line Islands straddle both the equator and the 180th meridian."},{"q":"The country's name is the local pronunciation of which earlier European name?","a":"The Gilberts","o":["The Gilberts","The Solomons","The Marshalls","The Carolines"],"f":"It is pronounced Kiri-bass. Britain once joined these islands with the Ellice Islands, which later became Tuvalu."},{"q":"Village decisions in Kiribati are debated in an open meeting house called a what?","a":"Maneaba","o":["Maneaba","Bai","Haus tambaran","Langi"],"f":"Where you sit in a maneaba shows your family's role. Speaking out of turn is a serious breach of custom."},{"q":"Which Kiribati island chain keeps a clock so far ahead that it is among the first to start a new day?","a":"The Line Islands","o":["The Line Islands","The Phoenix Islands","The Gilbert Islands","The Caroline Islands"],"f":"Kiritimati, or Christmas Island, uses UTC+14. The new year arrives there before almost anywhere else."},{"q":"Kiribati became independent from the United Kingdom in which year?","a":"1979","o":["1979","1962","1970","1990"],"f":"The new state kept the Gilberts and also the Phoenix Islands and most of the Line Islands, spread across a vast ocean."}]},{"id":"KNA","name":"Saint Kitts and Nevis","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇰🇳","latlng":[17.33333333,-62.75],"q":[{"q":"Which singer and guitarist, born in Basseterre in 1950, moved to England as a child and wrote Love and Affection?","a":"Joan Armatrading","o":["Joan Armatrading","Tracy Chapman","Nina Simone","Miriam Makeba"],"f":"She was seven when the family settled in Birmingham. Her self-titled 1976 album made her an international songwriter."},{"q":"Brimstone Hill Fortress, a UNESCO site, was built by enslaved Africans to British designs. It guarded which island?","a":"St. Kitts","o":["St. Kitts","Barbados","Jamaica","Bermuda"],"f":"The citadel sits about 230 meters up a volcanic hill. France captured it once, in 1782, and Britain took it back the next year."},{"q":"St. Kitts, settled by the English in 1623, is often called the mother colony of which empire's Caribbean?","a":"The British Empire","o":["The British Empire","The Dutch Empire","The Portuguese Empire","The Swedish Empire"],"f":"From here, English settlers went on to other islands. France also colonized part of St. Kitts until the 1713 Treaty of Utrecht."},{"q":"For centuries sugar dominated the economy. The last sugar crop was harvested in which year, ending the industry?","a":"2005","o":["2005","1967","1983","2019"],"f":"The narrow-gauge sugar train had carried cane across St. Kitts. After the closure, tourism became the main earner."},{"q":"St. Kitts and Nevis is the smallest country in the Americas by both population and what else?","a":"Land area","o":["Land area","Number of islands","Length of paved roads","Height of its highest peak"],"f":"The two islands together cover about 261 square kilometers. Nevis has its own island assembly and a right, under the constitution, to seek secession."}]},{"id":"KOR","name":"South Korea","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇰🇷","latlng":[37,127.5],"q":[{"q":"King Sejong's scholars announced a simple phonetic alphabet in 1446 so that ordinary people could read. What is it called?","a":"Hangul","o":["Hangul","Hanja","Kana","Katakana"],"f":"Before Hangul, writing used Chinese characters, which took years to learn. The shapes of Hangul were designed from how the mouth makes each sound."},{"q":"In 1377 monks at Heungdeok Temple printed a Buddhist book with movable metal type. It is older than Gutenberg's Bible. What is the book called?","a":"Jikji","o":["Jikji","The Tripitaka Koreana","The Diamond Sutra of Dunhuang","The Analects"],"f":"The surviving volume is kept in Paris. The Tripitaka Koreana, carved on wooden blocks at Haeinsa, is a different and also famous project."},{"q":"Admiral Yi Sun-sin, born in Seoul in 1545, defended the coast with armored ships nicknamed what?","a":"Turtle ships","o":["Turtle ships","Dragon ships","Fire ships","Junks"],"f":"During the Japanese invasions of the 1590s his fleets won battles even when badly outnumbered. He was killed at the moment of victory in 1598."},{"q":"Kimchi is vegetables, usually napa cabbage, fermented with chili and garlic. The buried jars of the past kept it through which season?","a":"Winter","o":["Winter","The monsoon only","The grape harvest","The typhoon months"],"f":"Families still gather for kimjang, the communal making of winter kimchi. UNESCO lists that custom, not a single recipe."},{"q":"Traditional houses are warmed by smoke from the kitchen fire passing under the floor. That system is called what?","a":"Ondol","o":["Ondol","Tatami","Kang","Hypocaust"],"f":"The Roman hypocaust was a similar idea, reached independently. Ondol is why people sit and sleep on the floor, close to the heat."}]},{"id":"KWT","name":"Kuwait","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇰🇼","latlng":[29.5,45.75],"q":[{"q":"Bronze Age ruins and a later Greek settlement called Ikaros have been excavated on which island in the bay?","a":"Failaka","o":["Failaka","Bubiyan","Tarout","Sir Bani Yas"],"f":"Failaka sat on the old Dilmun trade routes. Alexander's successors left a small fort and temples there. Tarout is a Saudi island, and Sir Bani Yas is in Abu Dhabi."},{"q":"In August 1990 a neighboring army occupied the country and was driven out seven months later. Which state invaded?","a":"Iraq","o":["Iraq","Iran","Saudi Arabia","Oman"],"f":"The occupation lasted until February 1991, when a US-led coalition expelled the Iraqi army. Oil wells were set alight during the retreat."},{"q":"Before oil, divers went down on one breath for months each season to gather what?","a":"Pearls","o":["Pearls","Sponges","Ambergris","Coral for jewelry"],"f":"A stone weight pulled the diver down and a nose clip kept the water out. Japanese cultured pearls crashed the old market in the 1930s."},{"q":"A wooden lateen-sailed ship, built by eye for gulf trade and pearling, is called what?","a":"A dhow","o":["A dhow","A junk","A felucca","A longship"],"f":"The boom is one of the larger types. Builders shaped the planks without drawn plans. A felucca is the Nile boat, and a junk is the Chinese oceangoing ship."},{"q":"Spiced rice cooked with meat and dried lime is known in gulf kitchens as what?","a":"Machboos","o":["Machboos","Mansaf","Harees","Tharid"],"f":"Dried black lime, loomi, gives the rice its sour note. Neighbors cook closely related rice dishes under other names. Mansaf is the Jordanian lamb and yogurt dish."}]},{"id":"LAO","name":"Laos","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇱🇦","latlng":[18,105],"q":[{"q":"Thousands of giant stone vessels on the Xieng Khouang plateau, mostly Iron Age, are known as what?","a":"The Plain of Jars","o":["The Plain of Jars","The Plain of Temples","The Stone Forest","The Valley of Stupas"],"f":"Burials found nearby suggest the jars once held bodies or bones. Many sites are still dangerous because of unexploded bombs from the wars of the 1960s and 1970s."},{"q":"Luang Prabang's old town of monasteries sits where the Mekong meets which river?","a":"The Nam Khan","o":["The Nam Khan","The Nam Ou","The Mun","The Chao Phraya"],"f":"At dawn, monks walk the peninsula collecting alms. The whole old town is a UNESCO site. The Chao Phraya is the river of Bangkok, not of this confluence."},{"q":"From 1964 to 1973, while officially neutral, the country was heavily bombed in a covert conflict often called what?","a":"The Secret War","o":["The Secret War","The Tet Offensive","The Malayan Emergency","The Indonesian Confrontation"],"f":"Clearance teams still find cluster bomblets in fields. The Tet Offensive was fought in Vietnam. The Malayan Emergency and the Indonesian Confrontation were other Southeast Asian conflicts."},{"q":"The everyday grain, steamed and eaten with the fingers, is glutinous rather than the fluffy rice of most neighbors. What is it?","a":"Sticky rice","o":["Sticky rice","Jasmine rice","Basmati","Wild rice"],"f":"A basket called a tip khao keeps a ball of it warm. Laap, minced meat with lime, herbs, and roasted rice powder, is the classic dish beside it."},{"q":"Pha That Luang in Vientiane, the great gold stupa, is traditionally said to house what?","a":"A relic of the Buddha","o":["A relic of the Buddha","A meteorite","The Emerald Buddha","The ashes of a French governor"],"f":"The Emerald Buddha passed through Vientiane in history but is now in Bangkok. The present stupa was rebuilt in the 16th century and restored again later."}]},{"id":"LBN","name":"Lebanon","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇱🇧","latlng":[33.83333333,35.83333333],"q":[{"q":"Which poet and painter, born in Bsharri in 1883, wrote The Prophet?","a":"Khalil Gibran","o":["Khalil Gibran","Mahmoud Darwish","Nizar Qabbani","Adonis"],"f":"He emigrated to the United States as a boy and wrote much of his English work in New York. Darwish was Palestinian. Qabbani and Adonis were Syrian."},{"q":"Ancient shipbuilders prized the timber of which mountain tree, now rare and kept in highland groves?","a":"The cedar","o":["The cedar","The olive","The date palm","The carob"],"f":"Egyptian records ask for this timber from the coast. A stylized cedar is the national emblem. Most of the old forests were cut centuries ago."},{"q":"The Greek word for book comes from an ancient port here, because papyrus was shipped through it. Which port?","a":"Byblos","o":["Byblos","Tyre","Sidon","Ugarit"],"f":"Byblos has been lived in for thousands of years. Ugarit, where an early alphabet was found, is on the Syrian coast. Tyre and Sidon were the other great ports of this shore."},{"q":"Traders from Tyre, Sidon, and Byblos spread a script of about 22 consonant signs. That script is known as what?","a":"The Phoenician alphabet","o":["The Phoenician alphabet","Cuneiform","Hieroglyphs","Hangul"],"f":"Greeks later added signs for vowels, and Latin inherited the idea. Tyrian purple, a dye from sea snails, was another famous export of the same cities."},{"q":"Flatbread topped with oil and a mix of thyme, sesame, and sumac is called what?","a":"Man'oushe","o":["Man'oushe","Lahmacun","Injera","Naan"],"f":"The herb mix is za'atar, and bakers still cook the bread on a domed saj in the morning. Lahmacun is the thin spiced flatbread of the Turkish and Armenian table."}]},{"id":"LBR","name":"Liberia","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇱🇷","latlng":[6.5,-9.5],"q":[{"q":"Settlers who founded the republic, independent in 1847, were mainly who?","a":"Freed African Americans","o":["Freed African Americans","Portuguese convicts","Dutch farmers","British convicts"],"f":"The American Colonization Society began settlements in the 1820s. The state was not a colony of a European empire, which is why the date is so early. The main city's name honors U.S. president James Monroe."},{"q":"Who, elected in 2005 and sworn in in 2006, was the first woman elected head of state in Africa?","a":"Ellen Johnson Sirleaf","o":["Ellen Johnson Sirleaf","Wangarĩ Maathai","Winnie Madikizela-Mandela","Ruth Perry"],"f":"She shared the 2011 Nobel Peace Prize and served two terms. Ruth Perry had chaired a transitional council in the 1990s, but she was not chosen in a national election. Maathai and Madikizela-Mandela did not hold this office."},{"q":"In 1926 the Firestone company took a huge concession to plant which crop?","a":"Rubber","o":["Rubber","Cocoa","Cloves","Tea"],"f":"The deal lasted for most of the century and tied farms to American tire making. Cocoa built the economies of Ghana and Ivory Coast. Cloves built Zanzibar."},{"q":"Two civil wars, beginning in 1989 and 1999, finally ended in which year?","a":"2003","o":["2003","1847","1980","2014"],"f":"Charles Taylor left power in 2003. Ellen Johnson Sirleaf was elected two years later. 1847 was independence, not the peace."},{"q":"A pepper stew of palm-fruit pulp with greens and meat or fish, served with rice, is called what?","a":"Palm butter","o":["Palm butter","Egusi soup","Jollof rice","Fufu"],"f":"The oil palm gives the sauce its color and weight. Jollof is a tomato rice shared with neighbors. Fufu is a dough eaten beside a sauce, not the sauce itself."}]},{"id":"LBY","name":"Libya","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇱🇾","latlng":[25,17],"q":[{"q":"Who led resistance to the Italian occupation in Cyrenaica and was executed in 1931?","a":"Omar Mukhtar","o":["Omar Mukhtar","Abd el-Kader","Samori Touré","Muhammad Ahmad"],"f":"He was called the Lion of the Desert. Abd el-Kader fought France in Algeria, Samori Touré fought the French in West Africa, and Muhammad Ahmad was the Mahdi in Sudan."},{"q":"The Roman emperor Septimius Severus, who ruled from 193, was born in which city on this coast?","a":"Leptis Magna","o":["Leptis Magna","Carthage","Alexandria","Cyrene"],"f":"He adorned his hometown with a forum, a basilica, and arches that still stand. Carthage is in Tunisia and Alexandria in Egypt. Cyrene is a Greek city on this same coast, but it is not his birthplace."},{"q":"The Tadrart Acacus, a UNESCO landscape in the southwest, is famous for what on the rock?","a":"Prehistoric paintings of people and animals","o":["Prehistoric paintings of people and animals","Roman mosaics","Phoenician shipping logs","Medieval icons"],"f":"The pictures run from a wetter Sahara into the desert age and show cattle and giraffes where it is now extremely dry. They are paintings, not Roman floors."},{"q":"The Garamantes farmed the Fezzan by tapping ancient water with underground tunnels. Locally those tunnels are called what?","a":"Foggaras","o":["Foggaras","Arch aqueducts","Animal sakias","Hand shadufs"],"f":"The tunnels served Garama, modern Germa. The idea is cousin to the Persian qanat, under the Saharan name foggara. Arches, sakias, and shadufs move water in other ways."},{"q":"After Italian rule and a British-French administration, the country became an independent kingdom under Idris in which year?","a":"1951","o":["1951","1911","1931","1969"],"f":"Italy had invaded in 1911. Idris was overthrown in 1969. Omar Mukhtar had been executed in 1931, long before the kingdom."}]},{"id":"LCA","name":"Saint Lucia","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇱🇨","latlng":[13.88333333,-60.96666666],"q":[{"q":"Which poet, born in Castries, won the 1992 Nobel Prize in Literature?","a":"Derek Walcott","o":["Derek Walcott","V. S. Naipaul","Seamus Heaney","Wole Soyinka"],"f":"His long poem Omeros sets Homeric themes in the Caribbean. He also founded the Trinidad Theatre Workshop."},{"q":"The Pitons, a UNESCO site, are two volcanic plugs rising straight from the sea. What are their usual names?","a":"Gros Piton and Petit Piton","o":["Gros Piton and Petit Piton","Mount Pelée and Soufriere","Pico Duarte and La Selle","Blue Mountain and Catherine"],"f":"Gros Piton is the broader one, Petit Piton the steeper. Coral reefs at their feet are part of the same World Heritage area."},{"q":"France and Britain captured St. Lucia from each other more than a dozen times. That history earned it which nickname?","a":"The Helen of the West Indies","o":["The Helen of the West Indies","The Pearl of the Antilles","The Spice Isle","The Nature Isle"],"f":"Like Helen of Troy, the island was fought over for its beauty and its harbors. Britain finally kept it in 1814, and independence came in 1979."},{"q":"Jounen Kweyol, Creole Day, celebrates the French-based Creole language and food. A favorite is green fig, which is actually what?","a":"Unripe banana","o":["Unripe banana","A cactus fruit","A type of fig tree","Pickled mango"],"f":"Green figs are boiled and served with salt fish. Creole, Kweyol, is the language of most homes even though English is official."},{"q":"St. Lucia is the only sovereign country in the world named for a woman. Which saint is that?","a":"Saint Lucy of Syracuse","o":["Saint Lucy of Syracuse","Saint Helena","Saint Joan of Arc","Saint Brigid"],"f":"French sailors in the 16th century are credited with the name. Saint Helena is a British territory, not a sovereign country."}]},{"id":"LIE","name":"Liechtenstein","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇱🇮","latlng":[47.26666666,9.53333333],"q":[{"q":"In a 1984 referendum, Liechtenstein became the last country in Europe to grant women what?","a":"The vote in national elections","o":["The vote in national elections","The right to own land","Access to university","Passports of their own"],"f":"Only men voted, and the yes side won by 119 votes. Women first used the national vote in 1986, when Emma Eigenmann entered parliament."},{"q":"Only one other country is surrounded solely by countries that themselves have no seacoast. Which is it?","a":"Uzbekistan","o":["Uzbekistan","Switzerland","Austria","Luxembourg"],"f":"Switzerland and Austria border Liechtenstein, and neither touches the sea, so a traveler crosses two countries to reach the ocean. Geographers call that doubly landlocked."},{"q":"The principality takes its name from a noble family that, in 1719, bought land so they could have a seat in the empire. Where was that family originally based?","a":"Lower Austria, near Vienna","o":["Lower Austria, near Vienna","Brittany","Sicily","Bohemia only"],"f":"Emperor Charles VI united the county of Vaduz and the lordship of Schellenberg and raised them to a principality. The princes did not live there full time for many generations."},{"q":"Liechtenstein abolished its army in 1868 and has not restored it. Who provides for its defense in practice?","a":"It relies on neutrality and its neighbors","o":["It relies on neutrality and its neighbors","A Swiss-style navy on the Rhine","A compulsory foreign legion","United Nations peacekeepers stationed in Vaduz"],"f":"The last military action is remembered, a bit jokingly, as sending 80 men to the 1866 war with Austria, all of whom came home. Police handle security today."},{"q":"Vaduz Castle, on the cliff above the town, is still the home of whom?","a":"The reigning prince","o":["The reigning prince","A museum staff only","The bishop of Chur","A United Nations governor"],"f":"The medieval castle was rebuilt in the 16th and 20th centuries. Unlike many European palaces, it is a private home and is not open as a walk-through museum."}]},{"id":"LKA","name":"Sri Lanka","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇱🇰","latlng":[7,81],"q":[{"q":"Which novelist, born in Colombo in 1943, wrote The English Patient?","a":"Michael Ondaatje","o":["Michael Ondaatje","Salman Rushdie","Arundhati Roy","Kazuo Ishiguro"],"f":"He moved to Canada as a teenager. Rushdie was born in Bombay, Roy in Meghalaya, and Ishiguro in Nagasaki. Arthur C. Clarke lived on this island for years but was born in England."},{"q":"A 5th-century king built a palace on a sheer rock, with frescoes on the cliff below. What is the site called?","a":"Sigiriya","o":["Sigiriya","Polonnaruwa","Dambulla","Anuradhapura"],"f":"King Kashyapa ruled from the rock in the late 400s. Visitors scratched poems on the mirror wall under the paintings. The other three are famous cities or cave temples, but not this rock."},{"q":"After a fungus wiped out highland coffee in the 19th century, who planted the estate tea that replaced it?","a":"James Taylor, a British planter","o":["James Taylor, a British planter","A medieval Sinhalese king","Dutch cinnamon merchants","Chinese monks in the 8th century"],"f":"Taylor planted tea at Loolecondera in 1867. The drink sold as Ceylon tea is a crop of the colonial century, not an ancient one."},{"q":"Pilgrims to the temple in Kandy come for a relic the chronicles say arrived from India in the 4th century. What is that relic?","a":"A tooth of the Buddha","o":["A tooth of the Buddha","A footprint from Adam's Peak","A lock of a king's hair","The ashes of a British governor"],"f":"The temple's inner shrine is opened at set hours. A footprint on Adam's Peak is a different pilgrimage, not the relic kept in Kandy."},{"q":"Geoffrey Bawa, born in Colombo in 1919, designed buildings for monsoon light and open courtyards. That approach is often called what?","a":"Tropical modernism","o":["Tropical modernism","Gothic revival","Stalinist classicism","Bauhaus glass towers"],"f":"He studied law before training as an architect in London. Houses such as his own in Colombo let gardens run through the rooms. The style is modern, but it is not a glass tower sealed against the weather."}]},{"id":"LSO","name":"Lesotho","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇱🇸","latlng":[-29.5,28.5],"q":[{"q":"Which 19th-century leader gathered people at Thaba Bosiu and is remembered as the founder of the Basotho nation?","a":"Moshoeshoe I","o":["Moshoeshoe I","Shaka","Mzilikazi","Cetshwayo"],"f":"He mixed defense with diplomacy and invited missionaries. Shaka and Cetshwayo led the Zulu, and Mzilikazi led the Ndebele. None of them founded this kingdom."},{"q":"A conical woven hat, echoing a hill near Thaba Bosiu, is worn with a patterned blanket. What is the hat called?","a":"A mokorotlo","o":["A mokorotlo","A fez","A kufi","A keffiyeh"],"f":"The hill is Qiloane. The blanket became everyday dress in the 19th century and works as a coat in a cold highland winter. A fez, a kufi, and a keffiyeh are caps from other regions."},{"q":"This is the only independent country whose whole territory lies above which elevation?","a":"1,000 meters","o":["1,000 meters","100 meters","5,000 meters","Sea level"],"f":"The lowest point is about 1,400 meters, where rivers meet the border. The Maloti and the Drakensberg make the rest higher. No other independent state is entirely above 1,000 meters."},{"q":"The country is completely surrounded by which single neighbor?","a":"South Africa","o":["South Africa","Eswatini","Namibia","Botswana"],"f":"Eswatini touches Mozambique as well as South Africa. Vatican City and San Marino are the other independent states enclosed by one neighbor, both inside Italy. This is the only such state in Africa."},{"q":"Winter on the high peaks brings something rare at this latitude in Africa. What?","a":"Snow","o":["Snow","Typhoons","Mediterranean hurricanes","Monsoon floods from the Indian Ocean"],"f":"Villages sit high enough for frost, and the summits can carry snow. Herders use the blanket as a coat. Typhoons belong to other oceans."}]},{"id":"LTU","name":"Lithuania","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇱🇹","latlng":[56,24],"q":[{"q":"Which painter and composer, born in 1875, filled pictures with glowing mythical figures and wrote music for them?","a":"Mikalojus Konstantinas Ciurlionis","o":["Mikalojus Konstantinas Ciurlionis","Marc Chagall","Arvo Part","Jean Sibelius"],"f":"He died at 35, in 1911, leaving hundreds of paintings and many musical scores. A museum in Kaunas keeps the largest collection."},{"q":"The Hill of Crosses near Siauliai is a pilgrimage mound covered with tens of thousands of what?","a":"Crosses left by visitors","o":["Crosses left by visitors","Soviet statues","Wind turbines","Burial mounds of kings"],"f":"Authorities bulldozed it more than once in the Soviet period, and people rebuilt it. It became a symbol of quiet resistance and of prayer."},{"q":"At its greatest extent in the 15th century, the Grand Duchy of Lithuania stretched from the Baltic toward which sea?","a":"The Black Sea","o":["The Black Sea","The Caspian Sea","The North Sea","The Mediterranean"],"f":"It was a multiethnic state of Lithuanians, Ruthenians, and others, later joined with Poland. Its size is why Lithuanian is not a Slavic language even though the old realm covered Slavic lands."},{"q":"In 1992 the Olympic basketball team won bronze wearing warm-up suits donated by which American band?","a":"The Grateful Dead","o":["The Grateful Dead","The Beatles","ABBA","Nirvana"],"f":"After independence, the team had little money for uniforms. The tie-dyed suits became a symbol of the country's return to the Olympics on its own."},{"q":"Baltic amber, washed up on Lithuanian beaches, is fossilized what?","a":"Tree resin","o":["Tree resin","Coral","Whale bone","Volcanic glass"],"f":"Some pieces contain insects trapped 40 million years ago. The Amber Road carried it south to the Mediterranean in ancient times."}]},{"id":"LUX","name":"Luxembourg","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇱🇺","latlng":[49.75,6.16666666],"q":[{"q":"Which statesman, born in Luxembourg City in 1886, later became French foreign minister and proposed the coal and steel pool that led toward the European Union?","a":"Robert Schuman","o":["Robert Schuman","Jean Monnet","Konrad Adenauer","Alcide De Gasperi"],"f":"He was born a German subject in Luxembourg and became a French citizen. Monnet drafted much of the 1950 plan, but the public proposal is called the Schuman Declaration."},{"q":"In 1985 five countries signed an agreement in a Moselle wine village to drop passport checks. What was the village?","a":"Schengen","o":["Schengen","Echternach","Vianden","Clervaux"],"f":"The Schengen Area later grew to most of Europe. The name of a Luxembourg village became the word for border-free travel."},{"q":"The Bock casemates are kilometers of tunnels cut into the rock under Luxembourg City. They were built mainly as what?","a":"Military defenses","o":["Military defenses","Wine cellars","A Roman aqueduct","A salt mine"],"f":"The fortress was so strong that the city was called the Gibraltar of the North. The tunnels were partly destroyed after 1867, when the fortress was dismantled by treaty."},{"q":"The home language belongs historically to which West Germanic group, rather than to French?","a":"Moselle Franconian","o":["Moselle Franconian","Alemannic","Bavarian","Low Saxon"],"f":"Luxembourgish is a Moselle Franconian tongue. Laws are often written in French, and many people switch among Luxembourgish, French, and German in one conversation. Alemannic is the group of Swiss German."},{"q":"Luxembourg is the world's only remaining sovereign grand duchy. A grand duke ranks, in protocol, just below whom?","a":"A king","o":["A king","A baron","A count","A knight"],"f":"The title survived when most grand duchies were absorbed into larger countries. The current constitution still makes the grand duke the head of state."}]},{"id":"LVA","name":"Latvia","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇱🇻","latlng":[57,25],"q":[{"q":"Which painter, born in Daugavpils in 1903, later became famous in America for large fields of color?","a":"Mark Rothko","o":["Mark Rothko","Marc Chagall","Jackson Pollock","Wassily Kandinsky"],"f":"His family emigrated when he was ten, and he grew up as Marcus Rothkowitz. The city was then called Dvinsk, in the Russian Empire."},{"q":"Riga has one of Europe's richest collections of buildings in which decorative style of about 1900?","a":"Art Nouveau","o":["Art Nouveau","Gothic only","Bauhaus","Neoclassical temples"],"f":"Alberta Street and Elizabetes Street are lined with sculpted faces, peacocks, and flowing stone. The old town beside them is medieval and Hanseatic."},{"q":"Jani, the midsummer festival, is celebrated with bonfires, wreaths, and a search for a mythical fern flower. It marks the shortest what?","a":"Night of the year","o":["Night of the year","Day of the year","Lent","Harvest moon"],"f":"Herbal cheeses and songs go with the fires. The holiday is a public festival, not only a folk revival."},{"q":"Latvia joined the 1989 human chain from Tallinn to Vilnius. That chain protested a secret 1939 pact between which two powers?","a":"Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union","o":["Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union","Britain and France","Sweden and Poland","Austria and Prussia"],"f":"The Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact had placed Latvia in the Soviet sphere. The chain, the Baltic Way, was a demand to publish that history."},{"q":"Latvian song festivals gather huge choirs in a tradition shared with Estonia and Lithuania. UNESCO lists them as what kind of heritage?","a":"Intangible cultural heritage","o":["Intangible cultural heritage","A natural wonder","An industrial monument","A culinary city"],"f":"The first Latvian festival was in 1873, when the country was still part of the Russian Empire. Singing later carried the independence movement of the late 1980s."}]},{"id":"MAR","name":"Morocco","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇲🇦","latlng":[32,-5],"q":[{"q":"Which traveler, born in Tangier in 1304, dictated a famous account after nearly 30 years on the road?","a":"Ibn Battuta","o":["Ibn Battuta","Ibn Khaldun","Leo Africanus","Ibn Jubayr"],"f":"The Rihla describes journeys from West Africa to China. Ibn Khaldun, born in Tunis, wrote a theory of history. Leo Africanus grew up in Fez but was born in Granada. Ibn Jubayr was an earlier traveler from al-Andalus."},{"q":"Aït Benhaddou, a UNESCO village of earthen towers, stands on an old caravan road into which mountains?","a":"The Atlas","o":["The Atlas","The Alps","The Drakensberg","The Simien"],"f":"Caravans used it between the Sahara and Marrakech. The towers are rammed earth, repaired after rains. Film crews borrow the ksar because the shape reads clearly on camera."},{"q":"Volubilis, near Meknes, still shows mosaics from its time as a city of which empire?","a":"The Roman Empire","o":["The Roman Empire","The Ottoman Empire","The Songhai Empire","The Aksumite Empire"],"f":"It began as a town of Mauretanian kings and became a Roman center. A mosaic of the labors of Hercules is still there. Juba II, a local king educated in Rome, belongs to the same story."},{"q":"Argan oil, pressed in the southwest where the tree almost uniquely grows, comes from which part of the fruit?","a":"The kernels","o":["The kernels","The bark","The sap","The flowers"],"f":"Goats climb the trees to eat the fruit, but cooperatives, often of women, gather most of the crop. UNESCO lists the practices around the tree. It is not a rubber tree."},{"q":"This country is unusual in Africa for having coasts on which two seas?","a":"The Atlantic and the Mediterranean","o":["The Atlantic and the Mediterranean","The Red Sea and the Mediterranean","The Atlantic and the Indian Ocean","The Mediterranean and the Black Sea"],"f":"The Strait of Gibraltar is the hinge, and Tangier looks toward Spain. The Red Sea coast belongs to countries farther east."}]},{"id":"MCO","name":"Monaco","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇲🇨","latlng":[43.73333333,7.4],"q":[{"q":"In 1297 Francois Grimaldi seized the rock of Monaco disguised as what?","a":"A Franciscan friar","o":["A Franciscan friar","A Venetian admiral","A Turkish merchant","A papal legate"],"f":"He and his cousins hid soldiers under monks' robes. The Grimaldi family still rules, and the coat of arms shows two friars with swords."},{"q":"The Monaco Grand Prix, first held in 1929, is unusual among major races because the track is made of what?","a":"Closed city streets","o":["Closed city streets","An oval of banked concrete","A desert salt flat","A frozen lake"],"f":"Cars pass within centimeters of barriers, harbor, and apartment blocks. The tunnel and the hairpin by the grand hotel are the famous corners."},{"q":"The Oceanographic Museum, on the cliff above the harbor, was directed for many years by which underwater explorer?","a":"Jacques Cousteau","o":["Jacques Cousteau","Thor Heyerdahl","Sylvia Earle","Robert Ballard"],"f":"Prince Albert I, a serious ocean scientist, founded the museum in 1910. Cousteau led it from 1957 to 1988."},{"q":"After Vatican City, Monaco is the world's second smallest country by what measure?","a":"Land area","o":["Land area","Population","Coastline length","Height above sea level"],"f":"It covers about 2 square kilometers, partly on land reclaimed from the sea. Vatican City is smaller still, and Nauru is the next smallest republic."},{"q":"People who hold Monegasque citizenship are only a minority of residents. Most inhabitants are what?","a":"Foreign nationals","o":["Foreign nationals","Seasonal fishermen","United Nations staff","Citizens of San Marino"],"f":"French and Italian residents have long outnumbered citizens. Strict rules decide who may become Monegasque, which is why the citizen body stays small."}]},{"id":"MDA","name":"Moldova","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇲🇩","latlng":[47,29],"q":[{"q":"Milestii Mici stores wine in tunnels that were originally dug as what?","a":"Limestone mines","o":["Limestone mines","Salt mines","Metro lines","Air-raid shelters only"],"f":"The galleries run for hundreds of kilometers, and only a fraction holds bottles. Cricova, nearer Chisinau, is another underground cellar of the same kind."},{"q":"Orheiul Vechi is a cliff monastery and cave complex above a bend in which river?","a":"The Raut","o":["The Raut","The Danube","The Dniester","The Prut"],"f":"Monks carved cells into the limestone. Below them are traces of Geto-Dacian and Tatar settlements, so the bend has been lived in for more than two thousand years."},{"q":"Stephen the Great ruled Moldavia in the late 1400s and fought to keep it from which expanding empire?","a":"The Ottoman Empire","o":["The Ottoman Empire","The Spanish Empire","The British Empire","The Mughal Empire"],"f":"His realm included present-day Moldova and part of Romania. He built a church after many of his battles, and several of those painted churches survive."},{"q":"Mamaliga, the everyday staple, is a porridge of which grain?","a":"Corn","o":["Corn","Buckwheat","Rice","Millet"],"f":"It is close to Italian polenta and to Romanian mamaliga. A lump of it with sheep cheese and sour cream is a basic meal."},{"q":"On 1 March people give each other a red-and-white thread to welcome spring. What is that token called?","a":"Martisor","o":["Martisor","Martenitsa","Kukeri","Surva"],"f":"It is worn until a tree flowers. Bulgaria's martenitsa is a close cousin of the same custom. Kukeri are masked winter rituals, a different event."}]},{"id":"MDG","name":"Madagascar","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇲🇬","latlng":[-20,47],"q":[{"q":"The island's main language is Austronesian. Its closest relatives are spoken where?","a":"Borneo","o":["Borneo","The Arabian Peninsula","The Andes","Southern Africa's click-language region"],"f":"Settlers from the Indonesian archipelago arrived, probably in the first millennium, before Bantu-speaking Africans. Genes and words both point across the Indian Ocean."},{"q":"Tsingy de Bemaraha, a UNESCO park, is a maze of what?","a":"Needle-sharp limestone pinnacles","o":["Needle-sharp limestone pinnacles","Granite domes","Sand dunes","Coral atolls"],"f":"Tsingy means a place where one cannot walk barefoot. Caves cut through the stone, and lemurs live in the pockets of forest on top."},{"q":"The Avenue of the Baobabs near Morondava is a row of which trees, left standing when fields replaced the forest?","a":"Grandidier's baobabs","o":["Grandidier's baobabs","Dragon blood trees","Joshua trees","Quiver trees"],"f":"Six of the world's baobab species grow only on this island. One African species and one Australian species live elsewhere. Dragon blood trees are the umbrellas of Socotra."},{"q":"Which primates evolved here and live wild, as native animals, only on this island?","a":"Lemurs","o":["Lemurs","Chimpanzees","Gorillas","Baboons"],"f":"People later carried a few to the Comoros, but they did not evolve there. Chimpanzees, gorillas, and baboons are mainland primates. Long isolation is why the lemurs survived."},{"q":"In famadihana, some highland families open the tomb every few years and do what?","a":"Rewrap the remains and celebrate","o":["Rewrap the remains and celebrate","Send the body out to sea","Burn the body on a river ghat","Seal the tomb and never return"],"f":"It is called the turning of the bones, with music and a shared meal. Not every community on the island does it. It is a rite of care for the dead."}]},{"id":"MDV","name":"Maldives","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇲🇻","latlng":[3.25,73],"q":[{"q":"For centuries, small white shells from these atolls were shipped as far as Africa and used as money. What are they called?","a":"Cowries","o":["Cowries","Conches","Nautiluses","Abalone"],"f":"The money cowrie was light and hard to fake. West African markets were still counting the shells long after the islands had moved on to other trade."},{"q":"These coral islands have almost no hills. Their average ground sits about how far above the sea?","a":"One to two meters","o":["One to two meters","About 50 meters","About 200 meters","About 1,000 meters"],"f":"There are no rivers. Fresh water sits in thin lenses under the sand, which is why a higher sea threatens the whole country."},{"q":"Grated coconut, onion, chili, and smoked tuna, eaten at breakfast with flatbread, is called what?","a":"Mas huni","o":["Mas huni","Garudhiya","Hoppers","Nasi lemak"],"f":"Garudhiya is the clear tuna broth served with rice, a different dish. Skipjack has always been the fish these islands depend on."},{"q":"Local tradition dates the king's conversion to Islam to which year?","a":"1153","o":["1153","622","1453","1753"],"f":"A North African visitor is remembered as the saint who persuaded him, and a mosque in Male is tied to the story. Ibn Battuta came later and wrote about serving as a judge."},{"q":"The local script, Thaana, is written in which direction?","a":"Right to left","o":["Right to left","Left to right","Top to bottom in columns","In a spiral"],"f":"The letters draw on Arabic and on local numerals. The spoken language, Dhivehi, is related to Sinhala of Sri Lanka, not to Arabic."}]},{"id":"MEX","name":"Mexico","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇲🇽","latlng":[23,-102],"q":[{"q":"Which chemist, born in Tepic, first synthesized norethindrone in 1951, a key step toward the oral contraceptive?","a":"Luis Miramontes","o":["Luis Miramontes","Alexander Fleming","Percy Julian","Selman Waksman"],"f":"He was in his twenties, working in Mexico City with Carl Djerassi and Jorge Rosenkranz. The compound was made from chemicals drawn from Mexican yams."},{"q":"Teotihuacan, with the Pyramids of the Sun and Moon, was already abandoned when which later people gave it that name?","a":"The Aztecs","o":["The Aztecs","The Inca","The Olmecs","The Spanish"],"f":"The name means the place where the gods were created. The city's own builders left almost no readable writing, so their name for it is unknown."},{"q":"Nixtamalization, developed in ancient Mesoamerica, soaks dried corn in water mixed with what?","a":"Lime or wood ash","o":["Lime or wood ash","Sea salt","Honey","Chili oil"],"f":"The alkaline bath unlocks niacin and makes the protein easier to use, which prevented pellagra. The same process produces masa for tortillas."},{"q":"Each autumn, monarch butterflies from farther north cluster in the oyamel fir forests mainly of which state?","a":"Michoacan","o":["Michoacan","Yucatan","Baja California","Sonora"],"f":"They can cover whole trees. The sanctuaries near the border of Michoacan and the State of Mexico are a UNESCO natural site."},{"q":"Spanish forces and Indigenous allies destroyed Tenochtitlan in 1521, on islands in a lake that was later drained. What was that lake called?","a":"Lake Texcoco","o":["Lake Texcoco","Lake Chapala","Lake Patzcuaro","Lake Cuitzeo"],"f":"The old islands are now the center of Mexico City. In 1978 the Templo Mayor was found under downtown streets. The soft lake bed is why so many old buildings there lean."}]},{"id":"MHL","name":"Marshall Islands","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇲🇭","latlng":[9,168],"q":[{"q":"From 1946 to 1958 the United States tested nuclear weapons at which atoll after moving its people away?","a":"Bikini Atoll","o":["Bikini Atoll","Chuuk Lagoon","Funafuti","Tarawa"],"f":"The swimsuit was named after the tests. Fallout and relocation meant many Bikinians could not safely return home."},{"q":"Marshallese stick charts were teaching tools for what, rather than maps carried on a voyage?","a":"Ocean swell patterns","o":["Ocean swell patterns","Star brightness","Bird migration dates","Coral species"],"f":"Shells stood for islands and curved sticks for reflected swells. Sailors memorized the lesson, then crossed the ocean without the chart."},{"q":"Kwajalein surrounds one of the world's largest lagoons. An atoll is mainly a ring of what?","a":"Low coral islets","o":["Low coral islets","Granite peaks","River deltas","Sandstone cliffs"],"f":"Reefs grow around a sinking volcano. When the volcano is gone, the reef and its islets remain around a lagoon."},{"q":"Besides the stars, traditional Marshallese navigators were especially skilled at reading what?","a":"The pattern of swells","o":["The pattern of swells","A magnetic compass","Floating pumice","The color of sand"],"f":"Swells bounce off distant islands and change how a canoe moves. That feeling of the water guided voyages before modern instruments."},{"q":"Castle Bravo, detonated at Bikini in 1954, was the largest U.S. test of which kind of weapon?","a":"A hydrogen bomb","o":["A hydrogen bomb","A torpedo","A chemical shell","A land mine"],"f":"The blast was far stronger than predicted. Fallout hit Rongelap and other atolls, and those communities had to be evacuated."}]},{"id":"MKD","name":"North Macedonia","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇲🇰","latlng":[41.83333333,22],"q":[{"q":"Anjez Gonxhe Bojaxhiu, later known as Mother Teresa, was born in 1910 in which city?","a":"Skopje","o":["Skopje","Tirana","Pristina","Ohrid"],"f":"Her parents were Albanian, and the city was then in the Ottoman Empire. She left for Ireland and India as a teenager and founded the Missionaries of Charity in Kolkata."},{"q":"Lake Ohrid is often called Europe's oldest lake. It is also unusual for the number of species that live where?","a":"Only in this lake","o":["Only in this lake","Only in the open ocean","Only on glaciers","Only in the Sahara"],"f":"Snails, trout, and other animals evolved in isolation over hundreds of thousands of years. The town of Ohrid, with its medieval churches, shares the UNESCO listing."},{"q":"Under an agreement with Greece, the country officially became North Macedonia in which year?","a":"2019","o":["2019","1991","2001","2018"],"f":"The Prespa Agreement settled a long dispute over the name Macedonia. The change took effect on 12 February 2019 and opened the door to closer ties with NATO and the European Union."},{"q":"Ohrid was once known as the Jerusalem of the Balkans because of how many of which buildings it had?","a":"Churches","o":["Churches","Lighthouses","Minarets only","Synagogues"],"f":"A medieval saying claimed it had 365 churches, one for each day. The real number was smaller, but the old town is still dense with monasteries and frescoed chapels."},{"q":"The town of Ohrid is famous for handmade pearls that are not from oysters. They are made from what?","a":"A paste from fish scales","o":["A paste from fish scales","Ground marble","Glass beads","Pressed amber"],"f":"The craft uses a secret emulsion painted onto a bead. It grew up beside the lake, where the fish were plentiful."}]},{"id":"MLI","name":"Mali","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇲🇱","latlng":[17,-4],"q":[{"q":"Mansa Musa of the Mali Empire made a famous pilgrimage to Mecca in which year?","a":"1324","o":["1324","1066","1453","1591"],"f":"His party carried so much gold that Cairo prices were said to sag for years. A Moroccan army took Timbuktu in 1591, long after his reign. He inherited the empire; he did not found it."},{"q":"The Great Mosque of Djenné is widely called the largest mud-brick building. The one standing now was rebuilt in which year?","a":"1907","o":["1907","1324","800","1960"],"f":"Older mosques stood on the site for centuries. Residents still replaster the walls in a yearly festival. The building you photograph is not an untouched medieval original."},{"q":"From the 14th century, Timbuktu was famous across the Islamic world especially as a center of what?","a":"Scholarship and manuscript libraries","o":["Scholarship and manuscript libraries","Atlantic shipbuilding","Coal mining","Porcelain export"],"f":"Sankore gathered teachers and students. Families hid manuscripts in private libraries that conservators still catalog. The city was a university town on the desert edge, not a seaport."},{"q":"Bogolan, or mud cloth, takes its dark patterns from a paint made of what?","a":"Fermented mud","o":["Fermented mud","Indigo alone","Melted gold","Crushed cochineal"],"f":"Cloth is dyed a base color first, then iron-rich mud darkens the drawing. Bamana makers are especially associated with it. Indigo is a different West African dye."},{"q":"Hereditary musicians who sing genealogies and the Sunjata epic are called what?","a":"Griots","o":["Griots","Marabouts","Troubadours","Imams"],"f":"A griot, or jeli, trains inside a family and may play the kora or the balafon. The epic tells how Sunjata founded the Mali Empire in the 13th century. An imam leads prayer; a griot keeps history aloud."}]},{"id":"MLT","name":"Malta","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇲🇹","latlng":[35.83333333,14.58333333],"q":[{"q":"The Ggantija temples on Gozo were built around 3600 BCE, which makes them older than what?","a":"The Great Pyramid of Giza","o":["The Great Pyramid of Giza","The cave paintings of Lascaux","The city of Jericho","Gobekli Tepe"],"f":"They are among the oldest free-standing stone buildings still standing. Lascaux, Jericho, and Gobekli Tepe are older, which is why the pyramid is the right comparison."},{"q":"In 1565 the Ottoman Empire besieged Malta and failed to take it from which knights?","a":"The Knights of St. John","o":["The Knights of St. John","The Templars","The Teutonic Knights","The Knights of the Garter"],"f":"The Great Siege ended in September when a relief force arrived. The knights then built Valletta, named for their grand master Jean de Valette."},{"q":"In 1942 King George VI awarded the George Cross for collective bravery to the people of Malta. It recognized their endurance of what?","a":"Axis bombing and siege","o":["Axis bombing and siege","A volcanic eruption","A famine of 1848","A pirate raid in 1565"],"f":"Malta sat on the supply route to North Africa, and the islands were among the most bombed places of the war. The cross still appears on the national flag."},{"q":"Maltese is the only Semitic tongue used as a language of the European Union. It is written in which script?","a":"The Latin alphabet","o":["The Latin alphabet","Arabic script","Cyrillic","Hebrew script"],"f":"It descended from Sicilian Arabic and later took many Italian and English words. Speakers of Arabic can hear old roots in everyday Maltese."},{"q":"The Hal Saflieni Hypogeum is an underground complex cut into the rock around 2500 BCE. It was used mainly as what?","a":"A burial place","o":["A burial place","A grain silo","A freshwater cistern","A gladiator school"],"f":"Rooms were painted with red spirals. Only a small number of visitors are allowed each day because breath and light damage the walls."}]},{"id":"MMR","name":"Myanmar","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇲🇲","latlng":[22,98],"q":[{"q":"Who, born in Pantanaw in 1909, was secretary-general of the United Nations from 1961 to 1971?","a":"U Thant","o":["U Thant","Dag Hammarskjöld","Kurt Waldheim","Boutros Boutros-Ghali"],"f":"He took office after Dag Hammarskjold died in a plane crash. U is a title of respect; he had no family surname in the Western sense. Waldheim and Boutros-Ghali held the post later."},{"q":"More than two thousand Buddhist temples still stand on a plain by the Irrawaddy, mostly from the 11th to 13th centuries. What is that plain called?","a":"Bagan","o":["Bagan","Mandalay","Sukhothai","Ayutthaya"],"f":"Bagan was the seat of the first Burmese empire. A 1975 earthquake damaged many monuments, and UNESCO listed the area in 2019. Sukhothai and Ayutthaya are in Thailand."},{"q":"The long 19th-century footbridge at Amarapura is built mainly of which wood?","a":"Teak","o":["Teak","Bamboo","Ebony","Pine"],"f":"U Bein is said to have used posts from a deserted royal palace. The bridge runs about 1.2 kilometers and is often called one of the longest teak footbridges."},{"q":"A rice-noodle soup in a fish broth, widely treated as the everyday national dish, is called what?","a":"Mohinga","o":["Mohinga","Pho","Laksa","Tom yum"],"f":"Catfish is the usual fish, and the bowl is finished with fritters, lime, and egg. Pho, laksa, and tom yum are the noodle soups of Vietnam, the Malay world, and Thailand."},{"q":"A pale yellow paste ground from tree bark is worn on the face as sunscreen and decoration. What is it called?","a":"Thanaka","o":["Thanaka","Kumkum","Otjize","Kohl"],"f":"People grind the wood on a flat stone with a little water. Otjize is the red butter mixture of the Himba in Namibia, and kohl is a dark eye cosmetic."}]},{"id":"MNE","name":"Montenegro","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇲🇪","latlng":[42.5,19.3],"q":[{"q":"Which prince-bishop and poet, born in Njegusi, wrote The Mountain Wreath?","a":"Petar II Petrovic-Njegos","o":["Petar II Petrovic-Njegos","Ivo Andric","Vuk Karadzic","Djura Jaksic"],"f":"He ruled Montenegro in the 1830s and 1840s and is buried on Mount Lovcen. The poem is about honor, faith, and the struggle with the Ottomans."},{"q":"The Bay of Kotor looks like a fjord but is actually a drowned river canyon. It opens into which sea?","a":"The Adriatic","o":["The Adriatic","The Black Sea","The Aegean","The Tyrrhenian"],"f":"Medieval towns such as Kotor and Perast climb its cliffs. The whole region is a UNESCO cultural landscape."},{"q":"The country's name, Montenegro in Italian and Crna Gora in the local language, means what?","a":"Black mountain","o":["Black mountain","Silver coast","High lake","Old bridge"],"f":"Venetian sailors saw dark forests on the mountains above the coast. Crna Gora is the same name in the South Slavic language."},{"q":"A referendum in 2006 led Montenegro to leave a union with which country?","a":"Serbia","o":["Serbia","Croatia","Albania","Italy"],"f":"The two had remained together as Serbia and Montenegro after Yugoslavia broke apart. Independence was declared on 3 June 2006."},{"q":"The Tara River canyon, cutting through Durmitor National Park, is often described as the deepest canyon in which continent?","a":"Europe","o":["Europe","Africa","Australia","South America"],"f":"The gorge reaches about 1,300 meters in places. Durmitor's glacial lakes and the canyon together are a UNESCO site."}]},{"id":"MNG","name":"Mongolia","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇲🇳","latlng":[46,105],"q":[{"q":"Temujin, later called Genghis Khan, was born in the 1160s beside which river, according to the Mongol chronicle?","a":"The Onon","o":["The Onon","The Yellow River","The Volga","The Amu Darya"],"f":"In 1206 he took the title Genghis Khan after uniting the steppe tribes. The empire his family built became the largest contiguous land empire in history."},{"q":"The midsummer festival Naadam is built around which three sports?","a":"Wrestling, archery, and horse racing","o":["Wrestling, archery, and horse racing","Falconry, polo, and shooting","Sumo, judo, and kendo","Polo, buzkashi, and tent pegging"],"f":"Child jockeys race for many kilometers across open steppe. A winning wrestler earns a title that can grow with later victories."},{"q":"Herders call their round felt tent what in Mongolian?","a":"A ger","o":["A ger","A teepee","A lavvu","A chum"],"f":"Turkic speakers call the same tent a yurt. The roof ring lets out smoke and lets in light, and a family can pack the whole frame onto animals in an afternoon."},{"q":"In the 1920s an American expedition found dinosaur eggs at the Flaming Cliffs in the Gobi. Who led it?","a":"Roy Chapman Andrews","o":["Roy Chapman Andrews","Mary Anning","Richard Leakey","Barnum Brown"],"f":"The red cliffs are also called Bayanzag. Andrews worked for the American Museum of Natural History. Anning collected fossils in England a century earlier, and Leakey worked in East Africa."},{"q":"The Soyombo, a column of flame, sun, moon, and triangles, was designed in the 17th century by which monk?","a":"Zanabazar","o":["Zanabazar","Padmasambhava","Tsongkhapa","Atisha"],"f":"Zanabazar was the first Jebtsundamba, the senior Buddhist figure of the Mongols, and a celebrated sculptor. The symbol he drew later became the state emblem."}]},{"id":"MOZ","name":"Mozambique","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇲🇿","latlng":[-18.25,35],"q":[{"q":"Which writer, born in Beira in 1955, wrote Sleepwalking Land in Portuguese?","a":"Mia Couto","o":["Mia Couto","José Craveirinha","Paulina Chiziane","Pepetela"],"f":"He trained as a journalist and a biologist. Craveirinha and Chiziane are major writers from this country, of other books. Pepetela is Angolan."},{"q":"Ilha de Moçambique, a UNESCO island of stone chapels and a fort, was built up by which European power?","a":"Portugal","o":["Portugal","Britain","The Netherlands","Oman"],"f":"The chapel of Nossa Senhora de Baluarte, begun in the 1520s, is among the oldest European buildings in the southern hemisphere. The administration later moved south toward today's Maputo. Oman was the power in Zanzibar."},{"q":"A fiery chili sauce and chicken, spread through Portuguese kitchens from this coast, is called what?","a":"Piri-piri","o":["Piri-piri","Berbere","Harissa","Jerk"],"f":"The chili came from the Americas on Portuguese ships. This coast made it a signature sauce. Berbere is Ethiopian, harissa is Tunisian, and jerk is Jamaican."},{"q":"Independence from Portugal, led by FRELIMO, came in which year?","a":"1975","o":["1975","1964","1980","1992"],"f":"Samora Machel became president. A civil war then lasted until 1992. The armed struggle had begun in 1964, which is not the same as independence."},{"q":"Makonde helmet masks danced at initiation are called what?","a":"Mapiko","o":["Mapiko","Chi wara","Nimba","Gelede"],"f":"The dancer is the lipiko. In the 20th century, Makonde carvers also became known for fantastical ebony figures called shetani. Chi wara and Nimba are West African masks."}]},{"id":"MRT","name":"Mauritania","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇲🇷","latlng":[20,-12],"q":[{"q":"Chinguetti, Ouadane, Tichitt, and Oualata are UNESCO caravan towns still known for libraries of what?","a":"Medieval manuscripts","o":["Medieval manuscripts","Roman mosaics","Printed Bibles from Mainz","Cuneiform tablets"],"f":"Families kept Qurans and books of law in desert houses. Sand is covering streets in Chinguetti. These were stops on the routes, not Roman colonies."},{"q":"The Richat Structure, rings in the desert nicknamed the Eye of the Sahara, is what?","a":"An eroded dome of rock","o":["An eroded dome of rock","A meteor crater","A volcanic caldera","A dried coral atoll"],"f":"Layers wear away at different speeds and leave circles about 40 kilometers across. The impact-crater idea was popular and is wrong. It is geology, not a bullseye someone built."},{"q":"Independence from France came in which year?","a":"1960","o":["1960","1945","1975","1991"],"f":"Moktar Ould Daddah became the first president. The state kept ties to both the Arab world and West Africa. 1975 was the year of the Portuguese colonies, not this one."},{"q":"Hospitality is three small glasses of sweet tea, poured from a height. The leaves are what?","a":"Green tea","o":["Green tea","Rooibos","Yerba mate","Roasted coffee leaves"],"f":"A proverb says the first glass is bitter as life, the second strong as love, and the third mild as death. The custom is shared across the western Sahara. Rooibos is South African."},{"q":"The Senegal River waters the south, while the rest of the country is mostly Sahara. The staple grains of that southern strip are mainly what?","a":"Millet and sorghum","o":["Millet and sorghum","Teff","Quinoa","Rye"],"f":"Dates and camels belong to the oases. Millet and sorghum belong to the river. Teff is the grain of the Ethiopian highlands. The two halves of the country are almost two climates."}]},{"id":"MUS","name":"Mauritius","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇲🇺","latlng":[-20.28333333,57.55],"q":[{"q":"The dodo lived only here and was extinct by the end of which century?","a":"The 17th century","o":["The 17th century","The 12th century","The 19th century","The 20th century"],"f":"Dutch sailors described it in the 1590s. A 1662 report is the last sighting most historians accept. The bird had no fear of people because no one had lived on the island."},{"q":"Le Morne, a UNESCO mountain, is honored as a refuge of whom?","a":"Maroons who had escaped slavery","o":["Maroons who had escaped slavery","Dutch governors","Exiled Napoleonic officers","Shipwrecked Vikings"],"f":"Communities hid on the rock. A story says some leaped from the cliffs when a party bringing news of freedom was mistaken for a raid. The site remembers resistance."},{"q":"Sega, the Creole dance, is typically sung in which language of the islands?","a":"Mauritian Creole","o":["Mauritian Creole","Dutch","Classical Arabic","Mandarin"],"f":"Enslaved Africans shaped the drums and the dance, and Indian and European influences joined later. The sega tambour of Rodrigues, specifically, is the form UNESCO listed. Hindi is widely spoken, but the lyrics are usually Creole."},{"q":"The Seven Coloured Earths of Chamarel are stripes of color in volcanic clay. They come from what?","a":"Minerals weathering at different rates","o":["Minerals weathering at different rates","Paint laid by artists","Crushed coral","Glacier flour"],"f":"Rain does not readily mix the colors. A waterfall sits nearby. Nobody raked the stripes into place."},{"q":"Dutch sailors named the island in 1598 after which prince?","a":"Maurice of Nassau","o":["Maurice of Nassau","Christopher Columbus","Vasco da Gama","James Cook"],"f":"Arab and Portuguese sailors had already noted the empty island and moved on. The Dutch left, then France and later Britain held it. The dodo did not survive those settlements."}]},{"id":"MWI","name":"Malawi","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇲🇼","latlng":[-13.5,34],"q":[{"q":"A teenager at Masitala built a windmill from scrap and a textbook, later told in The Boy Who Harnessed the Wind. Who was he?","a":"William Kamkwamba","o":["William Kamkwamba","Richard Turere","Kelvin Doe","Lewis Latimer"],"f":"The windmill powered lights and a pump. Turere invented lion lights in Kenya, and Doe built a generator from scrap in Sierra Leone. Latimer was a 19th-century American inventor."},{"q":"David Livingstone called Lake Malawi, with its fishing lights, by what name?","a":"The Lake of Stars","o":["The Lake of Stars","The smoke that thunders","The Mountains of the Moon","Victoria Nyanza"],"f":"Lamps on the boats looked like a sky on the water. The lake also holds hundreds of cichlid species found nowhere else, a famous case for how fast new species can form. The smoke that thunders is Victoria Falls."},{"q":"The colony of Nyasaland became independent, under a new name, in which year?","a":"1964","o":["1964","1957","1963","1994"],"f":"Hastings Kamuzu Banda became the first president and ruled for decades. 1963 was Kenya. Zambia also became independent in 1964. Nyasa is a word for a lake."},{"q":"The thick maize porridge that anchors a meal, eaten by hand with fish or greens, is called what here?","a":"Nsima","o":["Nsima","Fufu","Injera","Couscous"],"f":"Neighbors eat the same family of porridge under names such as ugali or sadza. Chambo, a fish from the lake, is a favorite beside it. Fufu is pounded root, not this pot of maize."},{"q":"Before independence the British called the territory what?","a":"Nyasaland","o":["Nyasaland","Rhodesia","Bechuanaland","Tanganyika"],"f":"Rhodesia was the colonial name to the south and west. Bechuanaland became Botswana. Tanganyika joined Zanzibar to become Tanzania. The lake and the Maravi kingdoms supplied the new name."}]},{"id":"MYS","name":"Malaysia","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇲🇾","latlng":[2.5,112.5],"q":[{"q":"Which actress, born in Ipoh in 1962, won the Academy Award for best actress for Everything Everywhere All at Once?","a":"Michelle Yeoh","o":["Michelle Yeoh","Gong Li","Maggie Cheung","Zhang Ziyi"],"f":"She trained as a ballet dancer and first became famous in Hong Kong action films. Gong Li was born in Shenyang, Zhang Ziyi in Beijing, and Maggie Cheung in Hong Kong."},{"q":"The Petronas Towers were the world's tallest buildings from 1998 until 2004. Which building took that title from them?","a":"Taipei 101","o":["Taipei 101","The Burj Khalifa","The Empire State Building","The Shanghai Tower"],"f":"The towers stand 451.9 meters to their tips and are linked by a skybridge. The Burj Khalifa, in Dubai, took the record only in 2010. The Shanghai Tower came later still."},{"q":"Malaya became independent from Britain in which year?","a":"1957","o":["1957","1945","1963","1965"],"f":"The wider federation called Malaysia, adding Sabah and Sarawak, was formed in 1963. Singapore left that federation in 1965. 1945 was the end of the Japanese occupation, not independence."},{"q":"Rafflesia, a parasite of rainforest vines here and in Indonesia, is famous because one bloom is what?","a":"Among the largest flowers on Earth","o":["Among the largest flowers on Earth","The oldest living tree","A flower that eats birds","The source of natural rubber"],"f":"Rafflesia arnoldii of Sumatra can span about a meter and smells of rotting meat, which attracts flies. The plant has no leaves of its own. Rubber comes from a different tree, Hevea."},{"q":"Coconut rice with pandan, sambal, anchovies, egg, and peanuts is called what?","a":"Nasi lemak","o":["Nasi lemak","Nasi goreng","Laksa","Hainanese chicken rice"],"f":"The name means fatty rice, from the coconut milk. It is the breakfast of stalls across the peninsula. Nasi goreng is fried rice, a different dish."}]},{"id":"NAM","name":"Namibia","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇳🇦","latlng":[-22,17],"q":[{"q":"Deadvlei, a white clay pan among the Namib dunes, is famous for what standing on the floor?","a":"Dead camel thorn trees","o":["Dead camel thorn trees","Petrified Roman columns","Living mangroves","Nesting penguins"],"f":"The trees died centuries ago when dunes cut off the water, and the dry air kept the wood. Sossusvlei's red dunes rise next door. The pan is not a ruined city."},{"q":"German forces drove the Herero and the Nama into the desert and camps in 1904-1908. Germany officially called those crimes a genocide in which year?","a":"2021","o":["2021","1918","1948","1990"],"f":"Tens of thousands died. Von Trotha's 1904 order against the Herero is a central document. Independence from South Africa came in 1990. The official recognition was not made in 1918 or 1948."},{"q":"Himba women mix butterfat and red ochre to cover skin and hair. What is that mixture called?","a":"Otjize","o":["Otjize","Thanaka","Kohl","Henna"],"f":"It protects skin in a hot dry climate and is a mark of beauty. Thanaka is the yellow bark paste of Myanmar. The Himba live mainly in the northwest."},{"q":"The Namib, along the Atlantic, is widely described as one of the world's what?","a":"Oldest deserts","o":["Oldest deserts","Youngest volcanic chains","Largest rainforests","Deepest lakes"],"f":"Parts of it may have been dry for tens of millions of years. Fog off the cold ocean lets beetles and plants live where rain almost never falls."},{"q":"Welwitschia, a Namib plant that can live more than a thousand years, grows how many true leaves?","a":"Two","o":["Two","None","Eight","Several hundred"],"f":"The two leaves split into ribbons, so the plant looks like a pile of straps. A long taproot hunts for water. It is neither a cactus nor a tree."}]},{"id":"NER","name":"Niger","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇳🇪","latlng":[16,8],"q":[{"q":"The historic mosque of Agadez, in a UNESCO old town, is known above all for what?","a":"A tall mud-brick minaret","o":["A tall mud-brick minaret","A glass spire","A marble Ottoman dome","A steel radio mast"],"f":"Timbers stud the tower so it can be replastered, as on other Sahelian mosques. Agadez was a crossroads of Saharan caravans. The tower's present form is often linked to the 16th century, with many later repairs."},{"q":"At the Gerewol, Wodaabe men dance for days in makeup and ornaments. Young women are mainly judging what?","a":"Their beauty","o":["Their beauty","A camel race","A recitation contest","A millet harvest"],"f":"The dance prizes teeth and the whites of the eyes. It happens in the rainy season, when the herds are together. It is not a race."},{"q":"Fossils of Jobaria and Suchomimus were dug at Gadoufaoua, in the Ténéré. Those rocks record a Sahara that was once what?","a":"A wetter land of rivers","o":["A wetter land of rivers","A single ice sheet","Open ocean over a trench","A coral reef at the pole"],"f":"Teams led by Paul Sereno excavated there in the 1990s. Suchomimus had a crocodile-like snout for fish. The dunes on top are young compared with the bones."},{"q":"The Cure Salée, near In-Gall, brings Tuareg and Wodaabe herders together each year mainly to do what?","a":"Give the animals salt and hold festivals","o":["Give the animals salt and hold festivals","Mine uranium","Sign a treaty with France","Elect a president"],"f":"Herds drink and lick minerals. Dances such as the Gerewol overlap with the gathering. Uranium is mined at Arlit, a different place and a different event."},{"q":"Tuareg men traditionally cover the face with an indigo veil. What is that veil called?","a":"A tagelmust","o":["A tagelmust","A keffiyeh","A burnous","A turban of the Sikhs"],"f":"The dye rubs off on the skin, which is why outsiders spoke of blue men. A burnous is a cloak, not the veil. The wrap can leave only the eyes showing."}]},{"id":"NGA","name":"Nigeria","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇳🇬","latlng":[10,8],"q":[{"q":"Which playwright, born in Abeokuta in 1934, won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1986?","a":"Wole Soyinka","o":["Wole Soyinka","Chinua Achebe","Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o","Nadine Gordimer"],"f":"He was imprisoned during the civil war of 1967-1970. Achebe, also from this country, did not receive the Nobel. Ngũgĩ is Kenyan, and Gordimer was South African."},{"q":"Which novelist, born in Ogidi in 1930, wrote Things Fall Apart?","a":"Chinua Achebe","o":["Chinua Achebe","Wole Soyinka","Ben Okri","Buchi Emecheta"],"f":"The 1958 novel follows Okonkwo as British rule arrives. Okri and Emecheta are major novelists connected to this country, but not the author of that book."},{"q":"Fela Kuti, born in Abeokuta in 1938, named a style Afrobeat. His shows were built around what?","a":"Long songs, a big band, and political words","o":["Long songs, a big band, and political words","Solo piano recitals","Gregorian chant","Italian opera"],"f":"He led Africa 70 and later Egypt 80 from the Kalakuta compound in Lagos. Afrobeats, with an s, is a later pop label. It is not the style he named in the 1970s."},{"q":"The Benin Bronzes were made for a court in which city, looted by a British force in 1897?","a":"Benin City","o":["Benin City","Ife","Lagos","Kano"],"f":"Plaques and heads from the palace were sold to museums. Ife, also in this country, cast famous heads too, but they are another court. The country of Benin took its name later and did not make these plaques."},{"q":"Nok figures, some more than 2,000 years old, found on the central plateau, are made of what?","a":"Fired clay","o":["Fired clay","Cast bronze","Carved ivory","Hammered gold"],"f":"Nok people also worked iron, but the famous figures are terracotta. Many turned up in tin mines. They are older than the cast heads of Ife and Benin."}]},{"id":"NIC","name":"Nicaragua","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇳🇮","latlng":[13,-85],"q":[{"q":"Which poet, born in Metapa in 1867, launched the Spanish-language modernismo movement with the book Azul?","a":"Ruben Dario","o":["Ruben Dario","Pablo Neruda","Octavio Paz","Jorge Luis Borges"],"f":"Azul appeared in 1888 and reshaped Spanish poetry on both sides of the Atlantic. His birthplace was later renamed Ciudad Dario."},{"q":"Ometepe, an island of the volcanoes Concepcion and Maderas, takes its Nahuatl name from which meaning?","a":"Two mountains","o":["Two mountains","Sacred water","Place of reeds","Fire island"],"f":"The island sits in the great lake of the country, and petroglyphs are scattered across the slopes. Bull sharks have reached that lake from the Caribbean, but that is a separate story."},{"q":"In 1856 an American adventurer seized Nicaragua, made himself president, and re-legalized slavery. Who was he?","a":"William Walker","o":["William Walker","Sam Houston","Davy Crockett","John C. Fremont"],"f":"Armies from across Central America drove him out. He was executed in Honduras in 1860, and Nicaragua remembers the fight as the National War."},{"q":"A nacatamal, steamed for celebrations, is corn dough and filling wrapped in what?","a":"Plantain leaves","o":["Plantain leaves","Corn husks","Banana paper","Clay"],"f":"It is larger than a Mexican tamal and often holds meat, rice, potato, and chili. Sunday is the traditional day to serve it."},{"q":"Sharks in Lake Nicaragua, once thought to be a separate species, are actually which sharks that swim up from the sea?","a":"Bull sharks","o":["Bull sharks","Great white sharks","Hammerhead sharks","Whale sharks"],"f":"Bull sharks cope with fresh water and can travel the San Juan River from the Caribbean. They are visitors, not a lake-only species."}]},{"id":"NLD","name":"Netherlands","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇳🇱","latlng":[52.5,5.75],"q":[{"q":"Which painter, born in Zundert in 1853, sold almost nothing in his lifetime and painted Sunflowers and The Starry Night?","a":"Vincent van Gogh","o":["Vincent van Gogh","Rembrandt van Rijn","Johannes Vermeer","Piet Mondrian"],"f":"The Starry Night was painted in France, but he was Dutch by birth. Rembrandt and Vermeer belong to the 17th century, two hundred years earlier."},{"q":"Antonie van Leeuwenhoek, a cloth merchant born in Delft, ground single lenses and became the first person to see what?","a":"Bacteria and other microbes","o":["Bacteria and other microbes","The moons of Jupiter","X-rays","The structure of DNA"],"f":"He called them animalcules and wrote careful letters to the Royal Society in London. He never published a book, but his microscopes were the best of the 1670s."},{"q":"A polder is farmland made by draining a lake or a piece of seabed and keeping the water out with what?","a":"Dikes and pumps","o":["Dikes and pumps","Terraces of stone","Canals that raise the land","Glass greenhouses"],"f":"Much of the country is at or below sea level. Windmills once did the pumping, and electric stations do it now."},{"q":"In 1637 the price of a fashionable flower collapsed after a speculative craze. Which flower?","a":"The tulip","o":["The tulip","The rose","The orchid","The lily"],"f":"Bulbs had been bid up to the price of houses. The crash ruined some traders, and the episode is an early lesson in financial bubbles."},{"q":"People often say Holland when they mean the whole country. Holland is actually what?","a":"Two western provinces","o":["Two western provinces","The capital city only","A former colony","The royal family name"],"f":"North Holland and South Holland contain Amsterdam, Rotterdam, and The Hague, so foreigners met that name first. The country's name is the Netherlands, the low countries."}]},{"id":"NOR","name":"Norway","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇳🇴","latlng":[62,10],"q":[{"q":"Which painter, born in 1863, made The Scream, a figure on a bridge under a blood-red sky?","a":"Edvard Munch","o":["Edvard Munch","Edvard Grieg","Henrik Ibsen","Gustav Vigeland"],"f":"He painted several versions. He wrote that he had felt a great scream pass through nature while walking near Oslo, then called Christiania."},{"q":"Norway's famous narrow inlets, with steep walls falling into deep water, were carved mainly by what?","a":"Glaciers","o":["Glaciers","Rivers alone","Volcanic rifts","Coral growth"],"f":"Ice Age glaciers overdeepened river valleys, and the sea flooded them when the ice melted. Sognefjord is the longest and deepest."},{"q":"Roald Amundsen, born near Fredrikstad, led the first expedition to reach which point in December 1911?","a":"The South Pole","o":["The South Pole","The North Pole by land","The summit of Everest","The source of the Nile"],"f":"He used skis and sled dogs and beat Robert Falcon Scott by about a month. Earlier he had been the first to sail the Northwest Passage."},{"q":"Alfred Nobel was Swedish, but his will places the peace prize ceremony in which Norwegian city?","a":"Oslo","o":["Oslo","Stockholm","Bergen","Copenhagen"],"f":"The other Nobel prizes are awarded in Stockholm. When Nobel wrote his will, Norway and Sweden were joined under one king, which helps explain the split."},{"q":"Brunost, or brown cheese, gets its color and sweetness because whey is boiled until the milk sugar does what?","a":"It caramelizes","o":["It caramelizes","It ferments into wine","It freezes","It turns into butter"],"f":"It is sliced thin with a special cutter and eaten on bread or waffles. Goat whey versions are sharper than the milder cow blends."}]},{"id":"NPL","name":"Nepal","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇳🇵","latlng":[28,84],"q":[{"q":"Lumbini, a garden on the southern plains, is honored as the birthplace of whom?","a":"Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha","o":["Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha","Mahavira","Guru Nanak","Laozi"],"f":"He was born there, traditionally in the 6th or 5th century BCE, when the place was part of the Shakya lands. Ashoka left a pillar in the 3rd century BCE. Mahavira, Nanak, and Laozi are other teachers, not of this garden."},{"q":"Sagarmatha, the world's highest peak, stands on the border with China. What is its usual English name?","a":"Mount Everest","o":["Mount Everest","K2","Kangchenjunga","Annapurna"],"f":"The Nepali name is Sagarmatha and the Tibetan name is Chomolungma. Climbers on this side start in the Khumbu. The mountain is shared with China; it is not wholly inside one country. Kangchenjunga is a different border peak."},{"q":"A war with Britain in 1814-1816 ended by treaty, and the kingdom was never made a colony of which empire?","a":"The British Empire","o":["The British Empire","The Mughal Empire","The Qing Empire","The Portuguese Empire"],"f":"The Treaty of Sugauli kept the kingdom independent. Britain later recruited Gurkha soldiers from the hills. Unequal pressure was real, but there was no colonial government here."},{"q":"Rice with lentil stew, eaten twice a day in many homes, is called what?","a":"Dal bhat","o":["Dal bhat","Thukpa","Momos","Ghee"],"f":"A saying holds that a day without it hardly counts. Momos, the filled dumplings, are loved but are not the twice-daily meal. Thukpa is a noodle soup."},{"q":"Hill soldiers recruited into the British and Indian armies take their name from the old kingdom of Gorkha. What are they called?","a":"Gurkhas","o":["Gurkhas","Janissaries","Cossacks","Samurai"],"f":"The curved kukri is part of the kit. Gorkha expanded in the 18th century under Prithvi Narayan Shah and grew into the modern state. Janissaries, Cossacks, and samurai were soldiers of other empires."}]},{"id":"NRU","name":"Nauru","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇳🇷","latlng":[-0.53333333,166.91666666],"q":[{"q":"Mining of which mineral, formed largely from ancient seabird droppings, stripped most of Nauru's interior?","a":"Phosphate","o":["Phosphate","Bauxite","Diamonds","Copper"],"f":"The plateau left behind is a field of sharp coral pinnacles. Phosphate briefly made the tiny republic wealthy, then the easy deposits ran out."},{"q":"Nauru is generally recognized as the world's smallest what?","a":"Island republic","o":["Island republic","Continent","Archipelago nation","Landlocked state"],"f":"Only Vatican City and Monaco are smaller countries, and neither is a republic. Nauru is a single island of about 21 square kilometers."},{"q":"In World War II, Nauru was occupied by which country's forces?","a":"Japan","o":["Japan","Germany","Italy","Spain"],"f":"Phosphate was the prize. Hundreds of Nauruans were deported to Truk for forced labor, and many did not survive."},{"q":"Nauru became independent in 1968 after a United Nations trusteeship led mainly by which country?","a":"Australia","o":["Australia","France","The United States","Japan"],"f":"Britain, Australia, and New Zealand had administered the island together. Australia took the leading postwar role."},{"q":"With no rivers, traditional fresh water on Nauru came mainly from rain and from which tree?","a":"The coconut palm","o":["The coconut palm","The baobab","The olive tree","The date palm"],"f":"Coconuts provided drink, food, and fiber. The island's wealth later came from the mined plateau, not from rivers or mountains."}]},{"id":"NZL","name":"New Zealand","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇳🇿","latlng":[-41,174],"q":[{"q":"Which physicist, born in Brightwater, is called a father of nuclear physics for revealing the atomic nucleus?","a":"Ernest Rutherford","o":["Ernest Rutherford","Niels Bohr","Marie Curie","Enrico Fermi"],"f":"He won the 1908 Nobel Prize in chemistry and later led Cambridge's Cavendish Laboratory. Element 104, rutherfordium, honors him."},{"q":"In 1840 many Maori chiefs and the British Crown signed which agreement?","a":"The Treaty of Waitangi","o":["The Treaty of Waitangi","The Antarctic Treaty","The Treaty of Versailles","The Schengen Agreement"],"f":"The English and Maori texts do not match perfectly on sovereignty. That gap is still central to New Zealand law and politics."},{"q":"A hangi cooks food with steam in an earth oven. Which people developed this method in New Zealand?","a":"The Maori","o":["The Maori","The Anangu","The Inuit","The Sami"],"f":"Hot stones and leaves keep soil off the food. A hangi is still the center of many gatherings and welcomes."},{"q":"Polynesian voyagers who became the Maori are thought to have settled New Zealand around which time?","a":"Around 1300","o":["Around 1300","Around 500","Around 1770","Around 900"],"f":"New Zealand was the last large habitable landmass people reached. Those navigators crossed thousands of kilometers of open Pacific."},{"q":"The flightless kiwi is unusual among birds because its nostrils open where?","a":"At the tip of its beak","o":["At the tip of its beak","On its wings","On its feet","Behind its ears"],"f":"Kiwis hunt by smell, probing soil for worms. New Zealanders took the bird's name as a nickname for themselves."}]},{"id":"OMN","name":"Oman","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇴🇲","latlng":[21,57],"q":[{"q":"The Dhofar hills produce the resin of Boswellia sacra, once shipped to Rome and Egypt. What is that resin?","a":"Frankincense","o":["Frankincense","Myrrh","Ambergris","Camphor"],"f":"The best groves drink the summer monsoon mist. UNESCO lists the frankincense sites of Dhofar. Myrrh comes from a related but different tree."},{"q":"Channels that split spring water among farms, still running in mountain villages, are called what?","a":"Aflaj","o":["Aflaj","Qanats","Roman aqueducts","Shadufs"],"f":"UNESCO inscribed five of these systems in 2006. A sundial or a watch tower can divide the flow by time. Iranian qanats are a cousin idea, usually dug underground."},{"q":"Each summer a monsoon greens the hills around Salalah while the rest of Arabia bakes. What is that season called?","a":"The khareef","o":["The khareef","The shamal","The harmattan","The kaskazi"],"f":"Mist drips from the frankincense trees, and waterfalls appear that do not exist in other months. The shamal is a dry north wind, and the harmattan is the dusty wind of West Africa."},{"q":"In the 19th century Said bin Sultan moved much of his court to an African island and built a trading realm there. Which island?","a":"Zanzibar","o":["Zanzibar","Madagascar","Socotra","Mauritius"],"f":"Cloves and ivory passed through his ports. After he died in 1856 the African and Arabian halves of the realm were split between two sons."},{"q":"A curved dagger in a decorated sheath, worn at the waist on formal days, is called what?","a":"A khanjar","o":["A khanjar","A kukri","A kris","A yataghan"],"f":"Its shape is on the national emblem. A fine silver sheath can take weeks. The kukri is Nepali, the kris is Malay, and the yataghan was an Ottoman short sword."}]},{"id":"PAK","name":"Pakistan","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇵🇰","latlng":[30,70],"q":[{"q":"Which physicist, born in Jhang in 1926, shared the 1979 Nobel Prize for uniting electromagnetism with the weak force?","a":"Abdus Salam","o":["Abdus Salam","C. V. Raman","Satyendra Nath Bose","Homi Bhabha"],"f":"He shared the prize with Sheldon Glashow and Steven Weinberg. Raman, Bose, and Bhabha were major physicists from India, and none of them received that 1979 prize."},{"q":"Mohenjo-daro, a UNESCO city of fired-brick streets and covered drains, belonged to which ancient civilization?","a":"The Indus Valley","o":["The Indus Valley","Mesopotamia","The Olmecs","Ancient Egypt"],"f":"Its mature age was roughly 2500 to 1900 BCE. The Great Bath is the most famous structure. The Indus script is still unread, so we know the engineering better than the politics."},{"q":"K2, the world's second-highest peak, stands on the border with China in which range?","a":"The Karakoram","o":["The Karakoram","The Himalaya","The Hindu Kush","The Pamir"],"f":"It had no widely used local name, so the survey label K2 stuck. It is a harder climb, climber for climber, than Everest, and it is not in the Himalaya proper."},{"q":"Long-haul trucks covered in flowers, poetry, and mirrored metal carry a folk art known as what?","a":"Truck art","o":["Truck art","Madhubani painting","Mughal miniature painting","Kalighat painting"],"f":"Workshops in Karachi and Rawalpindi made the style famous. A fully painted truck can take weeks and advertises the driver as well as the haul."},{"q":"Which singer, born in Lyallpur in 1948, carried qawwali from Sufi shrines to concert halls worldwide?","a":"Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan","o":["Nusrat Fateh Ali Khan","Abida Parveen","A. R. Rahman","Ravi Shankar"],"f":"He sang with a party of voices and a harmonium until his death in 1997. Abida Parveen is another great Sufi singer. Rahman is a film composer from India, and Shankar played the sitar."}]},{"id":"PAN","name":"Panama","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇵🇦","latlng":[9,-80],"q":[{"q":"Which salsa singer and songwriter, born in Panama City, later served as minister of tourism?","a":"Ruben Blades","o":["Ruben Blades","Willie Colon","Tito Puente","Celia Cruz"],"f":"His 1978 album Siembra, made with Willie Colon, became one of salsa's bestsellers. Colon and Puente were born in the United States, and Cruz was born in Cuba."},{"q":"The Panama Canal, opened in 1914, raises ships with locks onto which artificial lake?","a":"Gatun Lake","o":["Gatun Lake","Lake Nicaragua","Lake Atitlan","Lake Managua"],"f":"The canal is not a sea-level ditch. Locks lift ships about 26 meters to the lake, then lower them to the other ocean. A larger set of locks opened in 2016."},{"q":"Treaties signed in 1977 transferred the canal from the United States to Panama at noon on which date?","a":"31 December 1999","o":["31 December 1999","15 August 1914","4 July 1977","1 January 2005"],"f":"They are the Torrijos-Carter Treaties. The Canal Zone, a strip the United States had controlled since construction, came to an end that day."},{"q":"Molas, made by Guna women, are colorful cloth panels created mainly by which technique?","a":"Reverse applique","o":["Reverse applique","Batik wax resist","Tie-dye","Cross-stitch alone"],"f":"Layers of cloth are stacked and cut away so lower colors show through. Animals and geometric patterns are favorites, and a mola is part of a woman's blouse."},{"q":"When the Isthmus of Panama closed a few million years ago, it joined the continents and separated which two oceans?","a":"The Atlantic and the Pacific","o":["The Atlantic and the Pacific","The Indian and the Arctic","The Pacific and the Indian","The Atlantic and the Southern"],"f":"Animals could then walk between the Americas. Ancestors of llamas moved south, and ancestors of armadillos moved north, in what biologists call the Great American Biotic Interchange."}]},{"id":"PER","name":"Peru","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇵🇪","latlng":[-10,-76],"q":[{"q":"Which writer, born in Arequipa, won the 2010 Nobel Prize in Literature?","a":"Mario Vargas Llosa","o":["Mario Vargas Llosa","Gabriel Garcia Marquez","Jorge Luis Borges","Pablo Neruda"],"f":"Novels such as The Time of the Hero and The Feast of the Goat examine power and corruption. He later also became a Spanish citizen."},{"q":"The Inca estate of Machu Picchu sits on a ridge above which river's cloud-forest canyon?","a":"The Urubamba","o":["The Urubamba","The Amazon","The Orinoco","The Parana"],"f":"The royal estate was abandoned in the 1500s and became widely known after Hiram Bingham's 1911 visit. Its dry-stone terraces still grip the ridge."},{"q":"Giant figures made by clearing dark stones, some hundreds of meters across, lie on a dry coastal plateau. Which culture made them?","a":"The Nazca","o":["The Nazca","The Moche","The Chimu","The Wari"],"f":"The lines are easiest to see from above, yet they were laid out long before airplanes, by careful measurement on the ground."},{"q":"Ceviche, Peru's famous coastal dish, is raw fish or seafood cured in juice from which fruit?","a":"Citrus, usually lime","o":["Citrus, usually lime","Coconut","Grape","Apple"],"f":"Acid changes the proteins so the fish turns opaque, a bit like gentle cooking. Onions, chili, and cilantro are classic additions."},{"q":"In 1532, at Cajamarca, Spanish forces captured which Inca ruler, setting off the conquest of the empire?","a":"Atahualpa","o":["Atahualpa","Moctezuma","Montezuma's successor Cuauhtemoc","Tupac Amaru I"],"f":"Atahualpa had just won a civil war against his brother Huascar. Despite a huge ransom of gold and silver, the Spanish executed him the next year."}]},{"id":"PHL","name":"Philippines","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇵🇭","latlng":[13,122],"q":[{"q":"Which novelist and doctor, born in Calamba in 1861, wrote Noli Me Tangere and was executed in 1896?","a":"José Rizal","o":["José Rizal","Andres Bonifacio","Emilio Aguinaldo","Apolinario Mabini"],"f":"His novels attacked the power of the Spanish friars. Bonifacio led the Katipunan revolt, Aguinaldo headed the revolutionary government, and Mabini was its adviser. None of them wrote Noli."},{"q":"The Ifugao rice terraces of the Cordillera, a UNESCO landscape, were built on what?","a":"Steep mountain slopes","o":["Steep mountain slopes","A river delta","A coral atoll","The floor of a crater"],"f":"Farmers still repair the stone walls and the irrigation. They are a living landscape, not an empty ruin, and claims about their exact age are debated."},{"q":"After the Second World War, surplus American military vehicles were stretched into a loud, painted form of public transport. What is it called?","a":"A jeepney","o":["A jeepney","A tuk-tuk","A rickshaw","A tram"],"f":"The word blends jeep and jitney. Chrome, slogans, and painted scenes cover a traditional jeepney. A tuk-tuk is the three-wheeler of Thailand and its neighbors."},{"q":"The country is an archipelago. Counts are revised, but there are more than how many islands?","a":"7,000","o":["7,000","700","70,000","70"],"f":"One government count has cited 7,641, and new islets move the number. Only a few hundred of the islands have people living on them."},{"q":"A sour stew, often soured with tamarind and cooked with pork, fish, or shrimp, is called what?","a":"Sinigang","o":["Sinigang","Adobo","Tinola","Lechon"],"f":"Guava or kamias can supply the sourness instead of tamarind. Adobo is a vinegar-soy braise, tinola is a ginger broth, and lechon is a spit-roasted pig."}]},{"id":"PLW","name":"Palau","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇵🇼","latlng":[7.5,134.5],"q":[{"q":"Jellyfish Lake in the Rock Islands is famous for golden jellyfish that have mostly lost their what?","a":"Sting","o":["Sting","Color","Bell","Ability to swim"],"f":"Cut off from the sea and short of predators, their stings faded. Each day the jellyfish migrate across the lake following the sun."},{"q":"Palau's Rock Islands look like mushrooms because erosion cuts the limestone most strongly at which level?","a":"The waterline","o":["The waterline","The summit","The reef crest","The beach dune"],"f":"Waves and boring organisms undercut the rock, leaving a cap of forest on a narrow stone neck."},{"q":"A traditional Palauan meeting house, with a carved gable telling village stories, is called a what?","a":"Bai","o":["Bai","Maneaba","Fale","Langi"],"f":"Men historically gathered in the bai, while women often held real authority over family land and titles."},{"q":"UNESCO listed Palau's southern Rock Islands largely because of their limestone islets and what else?","a":"Coral reefs and marine lakes","o":["Coral reefs and marine lakes","Medieval castles","Roman aqueducts","Desert monasteries"],"f":"Hundreds of the islets are uninhabited. Some marine lakes hold species that evolved in isolation from the open lagoon."},{"q":"In 1944 one of the Pacific War's fiercest battles was fought on which Palauan island?","a":"Peleliu","o":["Peleliu","Guadalcanal","Iwo Jima","Saipan"],"f":"Commanders expected a short fight. Japanese troops in caves resisted for more than two months, and casualties were far higher than predicted."}]},{"id":"PNG","name":"Papua New Guinea","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇵🇬","latlng":[-6,147],"q":[{"q":"Papua New Guinea has more living languages than any other country. About how many are there?","a":"More than 800","o":["More than 800","About 40","About 120","About 300"],"f":"Many are spoken by only a few hundred people. Tok Pisin, an English-based creole, is the language strangers most often share."},{"q":"In 1942 Australian troops fought Japanese forces along which muddy mountain path toward Port Moresby?","a":"The Kokoda Track","o":["The Kokoda Track","The Burma Road","The Ho Chi Minh Trail","The Inca Trail"],"f":"The Owen Stanley Range campaign stopped a Japanese advance on Port Moresby. It became a defining Australian war story."},{"q":"Male birds of paradise, most of which live on New Guinea, are famous for what?","a":"Elaborate courtship dances","o":["Elaborate courtship dances","Building clay nests","Migrating to Antarctica","Singing underwater"],"f":"Some clear a patch of forest floor and dance for hours. Bright plumes evolved because females choose the most impressive males."},{"q":"A bilum, used to carry babies or produce, is made by which method?","a":"Hand-looping plant fiber","o":["Hand-looping plant fiber","Weaving silk on a loom","Carving one log","Felting sheep wool"],"f":"A bilum stretches as it fills. The pattern can show the region of the maker."},{"q":"The Sepik River is famous for carved masks and ancestor figures made for which buildings?","a":"Spirit houses","o":["Spirit houses","Gothic cathedrals","Buddhist stupas","Desert mosques"],"f":"Initiation houses display these carvings. The Sepik itself winds more than a thousand kilometers to the Bismarck Sea."}]},{"id":"POL","name":"Poland","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇵🇱","latlng":[52,20],"q":[{"q":"Which astronomer, born in Torun in 1473, argued that Earth orbits the Sun?","a":"Nicolaus Copernicus","o":["Nicolaus Copernicus","Galileo Galilei","Johannes Kepler","Tycho Brahe"],"f":"His book On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres appeared in 1543, the year he died. He had worked as a church canon in Frombork, on the Baltic."},{"q":"The Wieliczka salt mine, near Krakow, has chapels and chandeliers carved entirely from what?","a":"Rock salt","o":["Rock salt","Marble","Amber","Ice"],"f":"Miners worked there from the 13th century until the 1990s. The Chapel of St. Kinga, far underground, is the showpiece of the UNESCO site."},{"q":"In 1980 striking workers at the Gdansk shipyard formed a union that the government later banned. What was it called?","a":"Solidarity","o":["Solidarity","The Prague Spring","Charter 77","The Orange Alternative"],"f":"Lech Walesa, an electrician, became its face. The movement returned in 1989 and helped end one-party rule. Walesa was elected president in 1990."},{"q":"Pierogi are dumplings of dough wrapped around a filling. A classic savory filling is potato and what?","a":"Cheese","o":["Cheese","Chocolate","Fish sauce","Olives"],"f":"Boiled pierogi can be fried afterward until the edges crisp. Sweet versions with fruit appear at Christmas alongside the savory ones."},{"q":"Marie Sklodowska Curie, born in Warsaw in 1867, is the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different sciences. Which two?","a":"Physics and chemistry","o":["Physics and chemistry","Physics and peace","Chemistry and medicine","Peace and literature"],"f":"She shared the 1903 physics prize for radioactivity and won the 1911 chemistry prize for discovering radium and polonium. She did the later work in Paris, but she was born and educated in Warsaw."}]},{"id":"PRK","name":"North Korea","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇰🇵","latlng":[40,127],"q":[{"q":"Fighting in the Korean War stopped in 1953 with an agreement signed at Panmunjom. What kind of agreement was it?","a":"An armistice, not a peace treaty","o":["An armistice, not a peace treaty","A peace treaty that formally ended the war","A royal marriage alliance","A trade pact and nothing more"],"f":"The fighting began in June 1950. The armistice drew a line near the 38th parallel and created a demilitarized zone. No peace treaty has replaced it."},{"q":"The highest mountain on the Korean peninsula, a volcano with a crater lake, stands on the border with China. What is it called?","a":"Mount Paektu","o":["Mount Paektu","Mount Halla","Mount Kumgang","Mount Seorak"],"f":"Koreans call the lake Heaven Lake. Old foundation myths place the origin of the Korean people on this mountain. Hallasan is on Jeju, in the south, and Kumgang and Seorak are different ranges."},{"q":"Pyongyang is famous for cold buckwheat noodles served in an icy broth. What is that dish called?","a":"Raengmyon","o":["Raengmyon","Japchae","Tteokbokki","Kimchi jjigae"],"f":"In the south the same family of noodles is called naengmyeon. The broth is meant to be icy and the noodles chewy. Japchae is a stir-fried glass-noodle dish, served warm."},{"q":"UNESCO lists mural tombs, around Pyongyang and nearby, of which ancient kingdom?","a":"Goguryeo","o":["Goguryeo","Silla","Baekje","Goryeo"],"f":"Goguryeo ruled much of northern Korea and Manchuria until 668. Related tombs across the Chinese border have their own UNESCO listing. Silla and Baekje were the southern kingdoms of the same age."},{"q":"The demilitarized zone that has split the peninsula since 1953 is about how wide?","a":"4 kilometers","o":["4 kilometers","400 meters","40 kilometers","400 kilometers"],"f":"It runs near the old 38th parallel for roughly 250 kilometers. Because so few people enter, the strip has become a refuge for wildlife, including rare cranes."}]},{"id":"PRT","name":"Portugal","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇵🇹","latlng":[39.5,-8],"q":[{"q":"Which poet, born in Lisbon, wrote under dozens of invented names, which he called heteronyms?","a":"Fernando Pessoa","o":["Fernando Pessoa","Luis de Camoes","Jose Saramago","Eca de Queiros"],"f":"Alberto Caeiro, Ricardo Reis, and Alvaro de Campos were his most famous imaginary authors, each with a style of his own. The Book of Disquiet was pieced together after his death."},{"q":"On 1 November 1755 a huge earthquake, fires, and a tsunami destroyed much of Lisbon. The disaster pushed European thinkers to argue about what?","a":"How a good God could allow such suffering","o":["How a good God could allow such suffering","Whether the Earth was flat","How to invent the steam engine","Whether to ban all churches"],"f":"Voltaire used the quake in Candide. The Marquis of Pombal rebuilt the downtown on a grid, one of the first planned city centers in Europe."},{"q":"Fado, a UNESCO-listed song tradition, is typically a voice accompanied by which guitar?","a":"The Portuguese guitar","o":["The Portuguese guitar","The flamenco guitar","The oud","The balalaika"],"f":"The Portuguese guitar has a pear-shaped body and twelve steel strings. Classic fado dwells on saudade, a homesick longing."},{"q":"Azulejos, the painted tiles on churches and house fronts, came to Portugal from techniques learned where?","a":"The Islamic world, by way of Spain","o":["The Islamic world, by way of Spain","China, by way of Macau","The Netherlands only","Ancient Rome"],"f":"Early tiles copied Moorish patterns. Later blue-and-white scenes covered whole walls, and the name is related to the Arabic word for a polished stone."},{"q":"Ferdinand Magellan, who led the expedition that first sailed around the world, was born in Sabrosa. He sailed for Spain, but he was by birth what?","a":"Portuguese","o":["Portuguese","Spanish","Italian","Basque"],"f":"He died in the Philippines in 1521, so he did not complete the voyage himself. Juan Sebastian Elcano brought the remaining ship home to Spain."}]},{"id":"PRY","name":"Paraguay","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇵🇾","latlng":[-23,-58],"q":[{"q":"Which novelist, born in Asuncion, wrote I, the Supreme about a 19th-century dictator?","a":"Augusto Roa Bastos","o":["Augusto Roa Bastos","Gabriel Garcia Marquez","Mario Vargas Llosa","Isabel Allende"],"f":"The novel portrays Jose Gaspar Rodriguez de Francia, who ruled Paraguay in isolation after independence. Roa Bastos spent years in exile."},{"q":"UNESCO sites at Trinidad and Jesus de Tavarangue preserve ruins of settlements built by which religious order?","a":"The Jesuits","o":["The Jesuits","The Benedictines","The Franciscans","The Dominicans"],"f":"In these 17th- and 18th-century missions, Guarani communities and Jesuit priests built churches, workshops, and orchards."},{"q":"From 1864 to 1870 Paraguay fought Argentina, Brazil, and Uruguay in which devastating conflict?","a":"The War of the Triple Alliance","o":["The War of the Triple Alliance","The War of the Pacific","The Chaco War","The Paraguayan War of Independence"],"f":"Paraguay's population fell drastically, though historians still debate the exact toll. Recovery took generations."},{"q":"Despite its name, sopa paraguaya is not a soup. It is a baked dish based on which grain?","a":"Corn","o":["Corn","Rice","Quinoa","Wheat"],"f":"Think of a rich cornbread with cheese and onion. One popular story says a cook added too much cornmeal to a soup and the mistake stuck."},{"q":"Alongside Spanish, which Indigenous language is official and spoken by most people in the country?","a":"Guarani","o":["Guarani","Quechua","Aymara","Nahuatl"],"f":"Many Paraguayans switch between the two languages in a single conversation, a mix called jopara. That everyday bilingualism is rare in the Americas."}]},{"id":"PSE","name":"Palestine","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇵🇸","latlng":[31.9,35.2],"q":[{"q":"Which poet, born in al-Birwa in 1941, wrote Identity Card and became the best-known voice of Palestinian letters?","a":"Mahmoud Darwish","o":["Mahmoud Darwish","Khalil Gibran","Nizar Qabbani","Adonis"],"f":"His family left al-Birwa in 1948. He later lived in exile and in Ramallah, and died in 2008. Gibran was Lebanese, and Qabbani and Adonis were Syrian."},{"q":"Cross-stitch on village dresses, whose patterns once signaled a woman's home region, was inscribed by UNESCO in 2021. What is it called?","a":"Tatreez","o":["Tatreez","Kantha","Sashiko","Kente"],"f":"A dress from Ramallah does not look like one from Hebron or Gaza. The stitches are counted on the cloth, so the motifs stay geometric. Kantha, sashiko, and kente are textile traditions of Bengal, Japan, and Ghana."},{"q":"A pot of rice, meat, and fried vegetables is flipped onto a platter so the rice lands on top. What is that dish called?","a":"Maqluba","o":["Maqluba","Mansaf","Musakhan","Mandi"],"f":"The name means upside down. Cauliflower, eggplant, and chicken are a common filling. Mansaf is lamb with fermented dried yogurt, musakhan is chicken with sumac and onions, and mandi is a spiced rice cooked in a buried pot."},{"q":"A square cotton scarf in a black-and-white net pattern became a widely recognized emblem of Palestinian identity. What is it called?","a":"A keffiyeh","o":["A keffiyeh","A fez","A turban","A kufi"],"f":"The open weave shades the head and neck. Other regions wear keffiyehs in other colors; the black-and-white pattern is the one tied to Palestinian national dress."},{"q":"The autumn harvest that structures village life, and the oil pressed afterward, comes chiefly from which tree?","a":"The olive","o":["The olive","The date palm","The cedar","The carob"],"f":"Some village trees are centuries old. Families still pick by hand and take the fruit to a press. Figs and grapes matter too, but this is the tree of the harvest season."}]},{"id":"QAT","name":"Qatar","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇶🇦","latlng":[25.5,51.25],"q":[{"q":"The old pearling trade on the peninsula collapsed in the 1930s mainly because of what?","a":"Japanese cultured pearls","o":["Japanese cultured pearls","A ban on diving","The opening of the Suez Canal","The invention of nylon"],"f":"Cultured pearls flooded the market and the price of natural pearls crashed. Families who had crewed the dhows later moved into oil, and then into gas. The Suez Canal had opened much earlier, in 1869."},{"q":"In 2022 the men's football World Cup was played here, the first World Cup hosted in which part of the world?","a":"The Arab world","o":["The Arab world","South America","East Asia","West Africa"],"f":"Matches were scheduled in winter because the summer heat is extreme. South America, East Asia, and Africa had all hosted the tournament in earlier years."},{"q":"A satellite news network that changed Arabic-language broadcasting was founded in Doha in 1996. What is it called?","a":"Al Jazeera","o":["Al Jazeera","Al Arabiya","BBC Arabic","France 24"],"f":"It quickly drew viewers across the Arab world. Al Arabiya, based in Dubai, was launched later, in 2003. BBC Arabic is much older as a radio service and is not based in Doha."},{"q":"A giant gas field under the peninsula continues into Iranian waters, where it is called South Pars. What is it called on this side?","a":"The North Field","o":["The North Field","The Ghawar field","The Rumaila field","The Burgan field"],"f":"It is one of the largest gas fields on Earth. Ghawar, Rumaila, and Burgan are giant oil fields in Saudi Arabia, Iraq, and Kuwait."},{"q":"The national animal is a white antelope that was hunted nearly to extinction and later bred back into the region. Which antelope?","a":"The Arabian oryx","o":["The Arabian oryx","The addax","The dama gazelle","The markhor"],"f":"The last wild Arabian oryx were gone by the early 1970s. Zoos bred them, and herds have since been released in several Arabian countries. Seen from the side, the oryx may have fed the old unicorn legend."}]},{"id":"ROU","name":"Romania","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇷🇴","latlng":[46,25],"q":[{"q":"Which sculptor, born in Hobita, carved endlessly simplified birds and columns and worked mostly in Paris?","a":"Constantin Brancusi","o":["Constantin Brancusi","Auguste Rodin","Henry Moore","Alberto Giacometti"],"f":"The Endless Column and the Table of Silence stand in Targu Jiu, a public memorial to soldiers of the First World War. He had briefly been Rodin's assistant and then rejected that style."},{"q":"The painted monasteries of Bucovina, a UNESCO group, are famous because their frescoes cover what?","a":"The outside walls","o":["The outside walls","Only underground crypts","Stained-glass windows","Wooden ceilings alone"],"f":"Voronet blue and the ladders of souls at Sucevita were painted in the 15th and 16th centuries. The pictures taught Bible stories to people who could not read."},{"q":"Vlad III, ruler of Wallachia in the 15th century, inspired a later novel. What did Bram Stoker invent that Vlad was not?","a":"A vampire count living in a Transylvanian castle","o":["A vampire count living in a Transylvanian castle","A prince who fought the Ottomans","A ruler who impaled enemies","A member of the Draculesti family"],"f":"Vlad did impale captives and his father was called Dracul, so the nickname Dracula has a historical root. Bran Castle's link to him is mostly modern tourism."},{"q":"At the 1976 Olympics, which gymnast, born in Onesti, scored the first perfect 10 in Olympic gymnastics?","a":"Nadia Comaneci","o":["Nadia Comaneci","Olga Korbut","Larisa Latynina","Simone Biles"],"f":"The scoreboard could not show 10.0 and displayed 1.0 instead. She was 14 and won three gold medals in Montreal."},{"q":"The Danube Delta, where the river meets the Black Sea, is a UNESCO biosphere reserve known especially for what?","a":"Reed beds and birdlife","o":["Reed beds and birdlife","Coral atolls","Active volcanoes","Sandstone canyons"],"f":"Pelicans, herons, and dozens of fish species use the channels. It is the best-preserved large delta in Europe."}]},{"id":"RUS","name":"Russia","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇷🇺","latlng":[60,100],"q":[{"q":"Which chemist, born in Tobolsk, published the periodic table in 1869 and left gaps for elements not yet found?","a":"Dmitri Mendeleev","o":["Dmitri Mendeleev","Antoine Lavoisier","Marie Curie","John Dalton"],"f":"He arranged elements by atomic weight and by repeating properties. Several of the gaps were filled in his lifetime, which persuaded other scientists he was right."},{"q":"Lake Baikal, in Siberia, is the world's deepest lake and holds about what share of Earth's unfrozen surface fresh water?","a":"About one fifth","o":["About one fifth","About one hundredth","About half","Nearly all of it"],"f":"It is also one of the oldest lakes, and most of its species, including the nerpa seal, live nowhere else. The lake is a UNESCO natural site."},{"q":"Yuri Gagarin, born in Klushino, became the first person to orbit Earth on 12 April of which year?","a":"1961","o":["1961","1957","1969","1975"],"f":"His flight, Vostok 1, lasted 108 minutes. 1957 was Sputnik, the first satellite, and 1969 was the first Moon landing."},{"q":"Which composer, born in Votkinsk, wrote Swan Lake, The Nutcracker, and the 1812 Overture?","a":"Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky","o":["Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky","Modest Mussorgsky","Igor Stravinsky","Sergei Rachmaninoff"],"f":"He trained at the St. Petersburg Conservatory and brought European forms together with Russian melodies. Stravinsky and Rachmaninoff belonged to the next generations."},{"q":"Construction of the Trans-Siberian Railway began in 1891 to link Moscow with which Pacific port?","a":"Vladivostok","o":["Vladivostok","Murmansk","Arkhangelsk","Odessa"],"f":"The line runs about 9,000 kilometers and took until 1916 to complete in full. It is still the main land route across Siberia."}]},{"id":"RWA","name":"Rwanda","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇷🇼","latlng":[-2,30],"q":[{"q":"Research on mountain gorillas in Volcanoes National Park was led for years by which American scientist?","a":"Dian Fossey","o":["Dian Fossey","Jane Goodall","Biruté Galdikas","Joy Adamson"],"f":"She founded the Karisoke center in 1967 and was killed in 1985. Goodall studied chimpanzees in Tanzania, Galdikas orangutans in Borneo, and Adamson lions in Kenya. These gorillas also live in Uganda and DR Congo."},{"q":"Imigongo panels, in black, white, and red, are built up on boards using what as the base?","a":"Cow dung","o":["Cow dung","Beeswax","Powdered glass","Papyrus pulp"],"f":"The dung is shaped into ridges and then painted. The craft is linked to the old court at Nyanza. It is wall art, not pottery."},{"q":"Intore dancers, once performers for the king, traditionally carry what?","a":"Spears and shields","o":["Spears and shields","Fishing nets","Prayer wheels","Polo mallets"],"f":"Sisal headdresses shake as they leap. The dance outlived the monarchy and is now a stage art as well as a heritage performance."},{"q":"A nickname calls the country the land of a thousand hills. The land really is mostly what?","a":"Steep green ridges","o":["Steep green ridges","A coastal plain","A sand sea","A river delta"],"f":"Farms cover the slopes from bottom to top. There is no seacoast. The nickname is a picture of the terrain, not a literal count."},{"q":"Lake Kivu, on the western border, holds large amounts of which dissolved gases in its deep water?","a":"Methane and carbon dioxide","o":["Methane and carbon dioxide","Liquid mercury","Dissolved gold","Helium only"],"f":"The gases come from volcanic activity, and methane is tapped for power. The lake is shared with DR Congo. A sudden release would be dangerous, so the water is watched."}]},{"id":"SAU","name":"Saudi Arabia","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇸🇦","latlng":[25,45],"q":[{"q":"Hegra, also called Mada'in Salih, is a UNESCO site of rock-cut tombs made by the same people who carved Petra. Who were they?","a":"The Nabataeans","o":["The Nabataeans","The Phoenicians","The Hittites","The Sabaeans"],"f":"Hegra was their southern city. Petra, in Jordan, was the more famous one. The Sabaeans were a kingdom of ancient Yemen, and the Hittites ruled in Anatolia."},{"q":"Muslims face a cube-shaped shrine in Mecca when they pray, and the hajj ends its rites there. What is that shrine called?","a":"The Kaaba","o":["The Kaaba","The Dome of the Rock","Al-Aqsa","The Prophet's Mosque"],"f":"Pilgrims walk around it seven times. The Prophet's Mosque is in Medina. The Dome of the Rock and Al-Aqsa are in Jerusalem, not in Mecca."},{"q":"Commercial oil in the east was confirmed in 1938 when which well came in?","a":"Dammam No. 7","o":["Dammam No. 7","Spindletop","Drake's well","Baba Gurgur"],"f":"It was nicknamed Prosperity Well. Drake's well was drilled in Pennsylvania in 1859, Spindletop gushed in Texas in 1901, and Baba Gurgur is near Kirkuk in Iraq."},{"q":"Abdulaziz Ibn Saud proclaimed the kingdom in which year, after decades of conquest across the peninsula?","a":"1932","o":["1932","1744","1918","1973"],"f":"A first Saudi state had begun with a pact at Diriyah in 1744, but the modern kingdom's founding date is 1932. The 1973 oil embargo came long after."},{"q":"The fruit of the oasis, eaten fresh or pressed, and the tree that makes desert towns possible, is what?","a":"The date","o":["The date","The olive","The fig","The pomegranate"],"f":"A date palm can fruit for decades if its roots reach water. Medina's ajwa dates are famous, and the great oasis of Al-Ahsa is itself a UNESCO landscape."}]},{"id":"SDN","name":"Sudan","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇸🇩","latlng":[15,30],"q":[{"q":"The steep pyramids at Meroë outnumber Egypt's. They belong to rulers of which kingdom?","a":"Kush","o":["Kush","Aksum","Mali","Great Zimbabwe"],"f":"Kushite kings ruled from Napata and later Meroë. In the 8th century BCE they conquered Egypt as its 25th dynasty. Their pyramids are smaller and steeper than Giza's."},{"q":"Piye, a Kushite king, conquered Egypt and began which dynasty of pharaohs?","a":"The 25th dynasty","o":["The 25th dynasty","The 4th dynasty","The 18th dynasty","The 1st dynasty"],"f":"Taharqo, a later king of the same line, built widely in Egypt and in Nubia. The 4th dynasty raised the Great Pyramid. The 18th included Hatshepsut and Tutankhamun."},{"q":"Two branches of the Nile meet at Khartoum. Which pair?","a":"The White Nile and the Blue Nile","o":["The White Nile and the Blue Nile","The Niger and the Benue","The Blue Nile and the Zambezi","The Atbara and the Congo"],"f":"The Blue Nile, from Lake Tana in Ethiopia, brings most of the flood. The White Nile comes from the Great Lakes and flows more steadily. The Atbara joins farther north."},{"q":"A thin fermented sorghum bread, lighter than Ethiopian injera, is called what?","a":"Kisra","o":["Kisra","Injera","Chapati","Canjeero"],"f":"It is the everyday bread with stews. Injera is thicker and usually made with teff. Canjeero is the Somali flatbread, another cousin."},{"q":"A British-Egyptian army destroyed the Mahdist state in 1898 at which battle?","a":"Omdurman","o":["Omdurman","Adwa","Dongola","Toski"],"f":"Thirteen years earlier, in 1885, the Mahdists had taken Khartoum and killed Charles Gordon. Adwa, in 1896, was Ethiopia's victory over Italy. Omdurman is the 1898 battle, just across the river from Khartoum."}]},{"id":"SEN","name":"Senegal","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇸🇳","latlng":[14,-14],"q":[{"q":"Which poet, born in Joal in 1906, was the first president and a founder of Négritude?","a":"Léopold Sédar Senghor","o":["Léopold Sédar Senghor","Aimé Césaire","Léon-Gontran Damas","Birago Diop"],"f":"He led the country from 1960 to 1980 and then stepped down. Césaire was Martinican and Damas was from French Guiana; they were his partners in the movement. Birago Diop was a Senegalese poet of other work."},{"q":"Gorée, facing Dakar, is a UNESCO memorial of the slave trade. The honest way to treat the House of Slaves is as what?","a":"A memorial, not a proven tally of captives","o":["A memorial, not a proven tally of captives","The only port that shipped captives","A Roman prison","A Dutch shipyard"],"f":"The island was one of many places in the trade. Historians dispute how many people passed through that house. The lesson is the trade, not a single number on a wall."},{"q":"Ceebu jën, a fish-and-rice dish of Saint-Louis inscribed by UNESCO in 2021, is often called what in French?","a":"Thiéboudienne","o":["Thiéboudienne","Yassa","Mafé","Pastels"],"f":"The name means rice with fish. The fish is browned, then the rice and vegetables cook in the same pot. Yassa is onion and lemon. Mafé is a peanut sauce."},{"q":"Which singer, born in Dakar in 1959, became the global voice of mbalax?","a":"Youssou N'Dour","o":["Youssou N'Dour","Baaba Maal","Ismaël Lô","Touré Kunda"],"f":"Mbalax sets sabar drums inside a pop band. His group, the Super Étoile, carried the Dakar sound abroad. Baaba Maal, from Podor, sings a northern style."},{"q":"Lake Retba, north of Dakar, sometimes turns pink because of what?","a":"Salt-loving microbes","o":["Salt-loving microbes","Paint in the runoff","Dissolved copper","A dye spilled in 1970"],"f":"The color depends on sun and salt, so the lake is not pink every day. Workers still rake salt on the shore. Other very salty lakes turn color for the same reason."}]},{"id":"SGP","name":"Singapore","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇸🇬","latlng":[1.36666666,103.8],"q":[{"q":"Who was prime minister from 1959 to 1990 and is remembered as the founding leader of the modern city-state?","a":"Lee Kuan Yew","o":["Lee Kuan Yew","Tunku Abdul Rahman","Ferdinand Marcos","Suharto"],"f":"He was born in Singapore in 1923 and died in 2015. Tunku Abdul Rahman led Malaya to independence. Marcos and Suharto led the Philippines and Indonesia."},{"q":"Open-air food centers, where stalls and shared tables serve many cuisines, were inscribed by UNESCO in 2020. What is that culture called?","a":"Hawker culture","o":["Hawker culture","Izakaya culture","Cha chaan teng culture","Dim sum culture"],"f":"From the 1970s, vendors were moved off the streets into licensed centers. One center can serve Chinese, Malay, and Indian dishes side by side. An izakaya is a Japanese bar, and a cha chaan teng is a Hong Kong cafe."},{"q":"The national flower is an orchid hybrid first raised in a local garden in 1893. What is its name?","a":"Vanda Miss Joaquim","o":["Vanda Miss Joaquim","Rafflesia arnoldii","The moth orchid","Sakura"],"f":"Agnes Joaquim, an Armenian resident, is credited with the cross of Vanda hookeriana and Vanda teres. It does not grow as a wild species. Rafflesia is a giant parasite of the rainforest, not a garden orchid."},{"q":"The city left the Federation of Malaysia and became independent in which year?","a":"1965","o":["1965","1957","1963","1971"],"f":"It had joined Malaysia only two years earlier, in 1963. The split followed disputes over politics and trade. 1957 was the independence of Malaya, and 1971 was the formation of the United Arab Emirates."},{"q":"Much of the present port and the Marina Bay district exists because land was what?","a":"Reclaimed from the sea","o":["Reclaimed from the sea","Lifted by a volcano","Built on a coral atoll","Left by a glacier"],"f":"Hills were cut down and the earth dumped offshore, so the island is larger than it was in the 1960s. There are no natural lakes; the reservoirs are dammed river mouths."}]},{"id":"SLB","name":"Solomon Islands","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇸🇧","latlng":[-8,159],"q":[{"q":"The long battle for Guadalcanal, a turning point in the Pacific War, was fought in which years?","a":"1942 to 1943","o":["1942 to 1943","1914 to 1915","1950 to 1951","1968 to 1969"],"f":"Both sides fought for Henderson Field and the surrounding seas. The campaign stopped Japan's advance farther south."},{"q":"In 1568 a Spanish explorer named the islands after King Solomon because he hoped to find what?","a":"Gold","o":["Gold","Silk","Diamonds","Amber"],"f":"Alvaro de Mendana imagined mines as rich as Solomon's. The gold was mostly a legend, and the biblical name remained."},{"q":"A nguzu nguzu, lashed to the prow of a western Solomon Islands war canoe, was meant to guard against what?","a":"Harmful spirits","o":["Harmful spirits","Icebergs","Sandstorms","Volcanic ash"],"f":"The figure often has a large head and hands held under the chin. It faced forward as the canoe traveled."},{"q":"Before coins, many islanders paid bride wealth and settlements with strings of discs cut from what?","a":"Shells","o":["Shells","Glass bottles","Iron nails","Clay tablets"],"f":"Shell money is still given at weddings and reconciliations in some islands, alongside the modern dollar."},{"q":"Marovo Lagoon in the New Georgia islands is best described as a vast enclosure of what?","a":"Salt water and coral reefs","o":["Salt water and coral reefs","A freshwater crater","A glacial fjord","A dry salt pan"],"f":"It is a double-barrier lagoon: an outer reef shelters an inland sea that itself contains more reefs and village islets."}]},{"id":"SLE","name":"Sierra Leone","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇸🇱","latlng":[8.5,-11.5],"q":[{"q":"Portuguese sailors called the coastal mountains Serra Lyoa. In English that means what?","a":"Lion Mountains","o":["Lion Mountains","Gold Coast","White Cape","Green Point"],"f":"The country's English name is a translation of the Portuguese one. The ridges behind the harbor suggested a lion. The Gold Coast was a name for a different shore, now Ghana."},{"q":"Freetown was founded in the late 18th century as a settlement for whom?","a":"Freed slaves","o":["Freed slaves","Portuguese convicts","British tea planters","French legionnaires"],"f":"Black Loyalists from the American Revolution, and later people taken off slave ships, were settled here. The British colony grew around that town. It was not a convict colony."},{"q":"Bunce Island, in the estuary above Freetown, was a British fort used for what?","a":"Holding captives for the slave trade","o":["Holding captives for the slave trade","Mining diamonds","Building locomotives","Curing tea"],"f":"Traders sent people especially toward the rice coasts of South Carolina and Georgia. The ruins are a memorial. Diamond digging came later, and inland."},{"q":"A civil war that began in 1991 ended in which year?","a":"2002","o":["2002","1961","1991","2014"],"f":"Independence from Britain had come in 1961. After 2002 a special court and a truth commission followed. 2014 was the Ebola epidemic, a different crisis."},{"q":"The leafy stew served with rice, often in palm oil, is made especially from the leaves of which plant?","a":"Cassava","o":["Cassava","Tea","Grape","Baobab"],"f":"The sauce is called plasas. Rice farming on this coast mattered on both sides of the Atlantic, because enslaved farmers carried rice skills to the American South."}]},{"id":"SLV","name":"El Salvador","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇸🇻","latlng":[13.83333333,-88.91666666],"q":[{"q":"Which archbishop, born in Ciudad Barrios, was shot while saying Mass in 1980 and canonized in 2018?","a":"Oscar Romero","o":["Oscar Romero","Helder Camara","Desmond Tutu","Thomas Merton"],"f":"He had denounced the army's killings during the civil war. Pope Francis declared him a saint in 2018."},{"q":"Joya de Ceren, called the Pompeii of the Americas, is a Maya farming village preserved by what around the year 600?","a":"Volcanic ash","o":["Volcanic ash","A river flood","Desert sand","Glacial ice"],"f":"People fled, so almost no bodies were found, but meals, gardens, and sleeping mats remained. It is a UNESCO site."},{"q":"The national dish is a thick stuffed corn cake cooked on a griddle and served with curtido. What is it called?","a":"A pupusa","o":["A pupusa","A tamale","An arepa","A gordita"],"f":"Curtido is a lightly fermented cabbage slaw. A national pupusa festival celebrates the many fillings, from beans and cheese to squash."},{"q":"Among the seven Central American countries, El Salvador is the smallest in land area and the only one without a coast on which sea?","a":"The Caribbean Sea","o":["The Caribbean Sea","The Pacific Ocean","The Gulf of Mexico","The Labrador Sea"],"f":"Its whole shoreline faces the Pacific. The land area is about 21,000 square kilometers, smaller than each of its neighbors."},{"q":"The Chapultepec Peace Accords, which ended a 12-year civil war, were signed in which year?","a":"1992","o":["1992","1979","1980","2006"],"f":"They were signed in Mexico City. The FMLN guerrilla army became a legal political party, and the wartime security forces were remade."}]},{"id":"SMR","name":"San Marino","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇸🇲","latlng":[43.76666666,12.41666666],"q":[{"q":"Tradition says a Christian stonemason founded a community on Mount Titano in the year 301. What was his name?","a":"Marinus","o":["Marinus","Benedict","Francis","Dominic"],"f":"The story says he fled persecution in the Roman Empire and built a chapel on the mountain. San Marino means Saint Marinus, and the state calls itself the oldest republic on that basis."},{"q":"The historic center and Mount Titano are a UNESCO site crowned by three fortresses. They are usually called what?","a":"The Three Towers","o":["The Three Towers","The Three Bridges","The Three Domes","The Three Gates"],"f":"Guaita, Cesta, and Montale stand on separate crags. Their silhouettes are the image on coins and on the flag."},{"q":"San Marino is not an EU member. Under an agreement with Italy, which money do its shops use?","a":"The euro","o":["The euro","The Swiss franc","The old lira","The pound sterling"],"f":"It had used the Italian lira and later signed a deal to mint its own euro coins. Those coins are legal tender across the eurozone."},{"q":"The Crossbowmen's Corps, in medieval costume, still competes in a ceremony that dates to the 16th century. A crossbow shoots what?","a":"A bolt, or short arrow","o":["A bolt, or short arrow","A round musket ball","A javelin on a string","A discus"],"f":"The festival is held in medieval dress on the slopes of the mountain. It is a civic ritual, not a military force."},{"q":"Napoleon, who redrew much of Italy, chose to do what about San Marino in 1797?","a":"Respect its independence","o":["Respect its independence","Annex it to France","Give it to the pope","Sell it to Austria"],"f":"A local leader, Antonio Onofri, persuaded him that the tiny republic was no threat. San Marino later sheltered Giuseppe Garibaldi when he was on the run in 1849."}]},{"id":"SOM","name":"Somalia","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇸🇴","latlng":[10,49],"q":[{"q":"The rock shelters of Laas Geel hold vivid paintings of cattle and people. They are about how old?","a":"Several thousand years","o":["Several thousand years","A few decades","Only the 15th century","The age of dinosaurs"],"f":"Archaeologists documented them in 2002, though herders already knew the shelters. Dates are still argued, so a range of several thousand years is the careful answer. They show herders, not dinosaurs."},{"q":"A formal oral poem, the gabay, is judged especially on which craft?","a":"Alliteration and meter","o":["Alliteration and meter","End-rhyme on every line","Written calligraphy","A drum solo"],"f":"A gabay can run to a hundred lines that share one opening sound. Poets were political voices as well as artists. The poem is memorized, not calligraphed."},{"q":"In 1972 the Somali language was standardized in which script, after a literacy campaign?","a":"Latin letters","o":["Latin letters","Arabic letters","Osmanya letters","Ge'ez letters"],"f":"Osmanya, invented in the 1920s by Osman Yusuf Kenadid, was a real rival, and Arabic script had been used for centuries. Ge'ez is the script of Amharic. The 1972 choice was Latin."},{"q":"The north has long exported the dried sap of Boswellia trees. What is that sap called?","a":"Frankincense","o":["Frankincense","Myrrh","Ambergris","Gum arabic"],"f":"The tears of resin were burned in ancient Egypt and Rome. Myrrh comes from a different tree, Commiphora, which also grows in the Horn. Gum arabic comes from acacia, farther west."},{"q":"A spongy fermented flatbread, cousin to injera and eaten with stew or ghee, is called what?","a":"Canjeero","o":["Canjeero","Kisra","Chapati","Injera"],"f":"It is also called laxoox. Sorghum or wheat flour ferments overnight. Injera is the thicker teff bread of Ethiopia, and kisra is the thinner bread of Sudan."}]},{"id":"SRB","name":"Serbia","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇷🇸","latlng":[44,21],"q":[{"q":"Which physicist and inventor, born in Idvor in 1854, improved long-distance telephone lines and taught at Columbia University?","a":"Mihajlo Pupin","o":["Mihajlo Pupin","Nikola Tesla","Albert Einstein","Guglielmo Marconi"],"f":"The loading coil, or Pupin coil, let telephone signals travel farther. Tesla was a Serb by heritage too, but he was born in today's Croatia, not in Idvor."},{"q":"Which tennis player, born in Belgrade in 1987, won Wimbledon for the first time in 2011?","a":"Novak Djokovic","o":["Novak Djokovic","Rafael Nadal","Roger Federer","Stan Wawrinka"],"f":"He grew up in Belgrade in the 1990s and first practiced in an emptied swimming pool. Nadal is Spanish, Federer is Swiss, and Wawrinka is Swiss."},{"q":"A slava is a family's annual feast for the saint its ancestors chose. UNESCO lists the custom as heritage of Serbia. The celebration is centered on what?","a":"A special bread and a candle","o":["A special bread and a candle","A public horse race","A midnight swim","A masked parade"],"f":"A priest or the head of the family blesses the bread, the kolac, and a boiled wheat dish called koljivo. The duty passes from father to son."},{"q":"The Vinca culture, named for a site near Belgrade, flourished around 5000 BCE and left figurines and signs that some scholars compare to what?","a":"An early form of writing","o":["An early form of writing","Egyptian hieroglyphs copied exactly","The Greek alphabet","Cuneiform from Babylon"],"f":"The signs are older than Sumerian writing, and experts disagree on whether they record language. The settlements also smelted copper very early."},{"q":"In 1389 Serbian forces under Prince Lazar met the Ottomans under Sultan Murad at which battle, in which both leaders died?","a":"The Battle of Kosovo","o":["The Battle of Kosovo","The Battle of Nicopolis","The Siege of Belgrade","The Battle of Mohacs"],"f":"The battle did not immediately end Serbian independence, but it became the central story of later epic poetry. It was fought on the field of Kosovo Polje, the blackbird field."}]},{"id":"SSD","name":"South Sudan","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇸🇸","latlng":[7,30],"q":[{"q":"A referendum led to independence on 9 July of which year?","a":"2011","o":["2011","1956","2005","2018"],"f":"The vote followed a 2005 peace agreement with Khartoum. 1956 was Sudan's own independence. The date is the fact to remember; 'newest country' is a title that expires."},{"q":"The Sudd, where the White Nile spreads out, is one of the world's largest what?","a":"Freshwater wetlands","o":["Freshwater wetlands","Sand seas","Coral reefs","Lava plateaus"],"f":"Papyrus swamp soaks up a great deal of the river. The Jonglei Canal, meant to cut through the swamp, was never finished. It is a wetland, not a dune field."},{"q":"The seasonal movement of white-eared kob across the eastern plains is one of the largest migrations of what?","a":"Land mammals","o":["Land mammals","Salmon","Monarch butterflies","Arctic terns"],"f":"Herds follow the rain in numbers that rival the famous migrations farther east. Tiang and gazelles share the plains. It is a mammal movement, not a bird or a fish run."},{"q":"Among many Dinka and Nuer families, songs and even personal names are tied to what?","a":"A favorite ox","o":["A favorite ox","A fishing boat","A gold weight","A coffee tree"],"f":"Horns of a display ox may be trained into a shape, and poems praise its color. Cattle are wealth and identity, not only meat. Farming matters too, but the herd carries the prestige."},{"q":"The White Nile's water, before it reaches the Sudd, has come mainly from which lake system?","a":"The Great Lakes, above all Victoria","o":["The Great Lakes, above all Victoria","Lake Tana","Lake Chad","Lake Tanganyika alone"],"f":"From Lake Victoria the river crosses Uganda and then spreads into the swamp. The Blue Nile, which joins at Khartoum, starts at Lake Tana in Ethiopia and supplies most of the flood."}]},{"id":"STP","name":"São Tomé and Príncipe","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇸🇹","latlng":[1,7],"q":[{"q":"Pico Cão Grande, a needle of rock hundreds of meters high on the larger island, is what?","a":"A volcanic plug","o":["A volcanic plug","A coral stack","A river-cut butte","A termite mound"],"f":"It is the hard core of an old volcano, left when softer rock wore away. The islands are volcanic peaks near the equator in the Gulf of Guinea. The needle stands on São Tomé, not on the smaller island."},{"q":"Portuguese settlers found the islands empty in the late 15th century. Their first plantation crop, grown with enslaved labor, was what?","a":"Sugar","o":["Sugar","Rubber","Tea","Cloves"],"f":"Cocoa replaced sugar in the 19th century. Enslaved people were brought from the mainland. Cloves were Zanzibar's fortune, not this one's."},{"q":"Tchiloli is a local theatre of tales about Charlemagne. Performers tell the stories with what?","a":"Music, dance, and elaborate costume","o":["Music, dance, and elaborate costume","Javanese shadow puppets","Noh masks","Kabuki makeup"],"f":"The plays arrived with Portuguese culture and were remade on the islands. They are danced as much as spoken. They are not an Asian stage form."},{"q":"Forro, the creole of the larger island, grew out of Portuguese mixed with languages of whom?","a":"Enslaved Africans from the mainland","o":["Enslaved Africans from the mainland","Dutch settlers","Arab sailors","Chinese miners"],"f":"A smaller creole, Angolar, is linked to communities that escaped the plantations. Bioko and Annobón, nearby, belong to Equatorial Guinea. The smaller island here is Príncipe."},{"q":"In the 19th century, shaded plantations called roças made the islands a noted exporter of which crop?","a":"Cocoa","o":["Cocoa","Cloves","Rubber","Tea"],"f":"After slavery was formally ended, Portugal still brought contract workers from Angola, Cape Verde, and the mainland under harsh conditions. The pretty estate houses sat on that labor."}]},{"id":"SUR","name":"Suriname","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇸🇷","latlng":[4,-56],"q":[{"q":"Which anti-colonial writer, born in Paramaribo, wrote We Slaves of Suriname?","a":"Anton de Kom","o":["Anton de Kom","Multatuli","V. S. Naipaul","Derek Walcott"],"f":"Published in 1934, the book attacks Dutch colonial rule and slavery's legacy. De Kom was later arrested by the Nazis in the Netherlands and died in a camp."},{"q":"The historic center of Paramaribo, a UNESCO site, is known for Dutch colonial buildings made largely of what?","a":"Wood","o":["Wood","Marble","Adobe","Basalt"],"f":"White wooden houses, palaces, and churches line the river. The style mixes Dutch plans with tropical timber construction."},{"q":"Newspapers, laws, and classrooms in Suriname use mainly which European language?","a":"Dutch","o":["Dutch","English","Portuguese","French"],"f":"The country was Dutch Guiana until independence in 1975. English dominates in Guyana, and French Guiana is an overseas region of France."},{"q":"After slavery ended, Dutch planters brought indentured workers to Suriname especially from India and which other place?","a":"Java","o":["Java","Japan","Ireland","Morocco"],"f":"Their descendants shaped the food and festivals of Paramaribo. Javanese nasi and Indian roti are everyday dishes."},{"q":"The Central Suriname Nature Reserve protects a huge block of mostly uninhabited what?","a":"Tropical rainforest","o":["Tropical rainforest","Alpine tundra","Coral desert","Temperate prairie"],"f":"UNESCO listed it for its undisturbed forest and animals such as jaguars, giant armadillos, and many bird species."}]},{"id":"SVK","name":"Slovakia","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇸🇰","latlng":[48.66666666,19.5],"q":[{"q":"Which leader, born in Uhrovec, tried to give Czechoslovakia socialism with a human face in 1968?","a":"Alexander Dubcek","o":["Alexander Dubcek","Vaclav Havel","Imre Nagy","Lech Walesa"],"f":"The Prague Spring ended when Warsaw Pact troops invaded in August. Dubcek was later expelled from the party, and he returned to public life only after 1989."},{"q":"Spis Castle, a UNESCO ruin above the town of Spisske Podhradie, is one of the largest castle complexes in which region?","a":"Central Europe","o":["Central Europe","Iberia","Scandinavia","The British Isles"],"f":"It grew from a 12th-century fort into a stone town on a hill. A fire in 1780 left it a ruin, which is how visitors see it now."},{"q":"Bryndzove halusky, often called the national dish, are potato dumplings topped with sheep cheese and what else?","a":"Fried bacon","o":["Fried bacon","Paprika goulash","A fried egg","Poppy seeds"],"f":"Bryndza is a tangy sheep cheese. Halusky are small dumplings pressed through holes into boiling water, cousins of Central European spaetzle."},{"q":"In the 1840s Ludovit Stur led the codification of standard Slovak. He based the written language mainly on which kind of speech?","a":"A central Slovak dialect","o":["A central Slovak dialect","Czech, unchanged","Hungarian","Latin"],"f":"Before Stur, Protestants had often written in a form of Czech. His choice gave the national movement a standard of its own."},{"q":"On 1 January 1993 Czechoslovakia split peacefully into the Czech Republic and Slovakia. What is that split often called?","a":"The Velvet Divorce","o":["The Velvet Divorce","The Velvet Revolution","The Prague Spring","The Ten-Day War"],"f":"The Velvet Revolution was the 1989 end of one-party rule. The later split was negotiated by politicians, and there was no war."}]},{"id":"SVN","name":"Slovenia","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇸🇮","latlng":[46.11666666,14.81666666],"q":[{"q":"Which architect, born in Ljubljana, designed the city's Triple Bridge and the national library?","a":"Joze Plecnik","o":["Joze Plecnik","Otto Wagner","Le Corbusier","Antoni Gaudi"],"f":"He also remade Prague Castle for the new Czechoslovak president after 1918. His Ljubljana work mixes classical columns with local craft."},{"q":"Lake Bled's island is reached by wooden boats called pletna. The island's main landmark is what?","a":"A pilgrimage church","o":["A pilgrimage church","A royal palace","A lighthouse","A casino"],"f":"Tradition says a groom should carry his bride up the 99 steps. The castle on the cliff above the lake is a separate medieval fortress."},{"q":"The white horses of the Spanish Riding School in Vienna descend from a stud founded in 1580 at which Slovenian village?","a":"Lipica","o":["Lipica","Bled","Piran","Ptuj"],"f":"The breed is the Lipizzaner, named for that village, then in the Habsburg lands. Foals are dark and usually turn white as they grow."},{"q":"The olm, a blind cave salamander nicknamed the human fish, lives in the underground waters of the karst, including which famous cave?","a":"Postojna Cave","o":["Postojna Cave","Waitomo Caves","Mammoth Cave","Lascaux"],"f":"Its skin is pale pink, and it can go years without food. Postojna's galleries were carved by the Pivka River and now include a small tourist railway."},{"q":"After a brief conflict in 1991, Slovenia left Yugoslavia. That conflict is known as what?","a":"The Ten-Day War","o":["The Ten-Day War","The Bosnian War","The Croatian War","The Velvet Divorce"],"f":"Fighting lasted from 27 June to 7 July 1991. The Brioni Agreement ended it, and the European Community recognized Slovenia the next year."}]},{"id":"SWE","name":"Sweden","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇸🇪","latlng":[62,15],"q":[{"q":"Which botanist, born in Rashult in 1707, invented the two-name system still used for species?","a":"Carl Linnaeus","o":["Carl Linnaeus","Charles Darwin","Gregor Mendel","Alexander von Humboldt"],"f":"Homo sapiens is one of his names. He taught at Uppsala and sent students around the world to collect plants."},{"q":"Alfred Nobel, born in Stockholm, made a fortune from dynamite and left it to fund prizes. He died in which year, after writing that will?","a":"1896","o":["1896","1867","1901","1914"],"f":"Dynamite, patented in 1867, was his safer form of nitroglycerin. The first prizes were awarded in 1901, five years after his death."},{"q":"Anders Celsius, born in Uppsala, proposed a temperature scale in 1742. In his original version, what was 0 degrees?","a":"The boiling point of water","o":["The boiling point of water","The freezing point of water","Absolute zero","Body temperature"],"f":"He set 100 at freezing. After his death the scale was flipped to the form used today, with 0 at freezing and 100 at boiling."},{"q":"The warship Vasa sank in Stockholm harbor on its maiden voyage in 1628. It was raised in 1961 and is preserved because the cold water did what?","a":"It kept shipworms from eating the oak","o":["It kept shipworms from eating the oak","It turned the wood to stone","It froze the ship in a glacier","It covered the hull in coral"],"f":"The ship was top-heavy with cannon and heeled over in a gust. About 98 percent of the original timber survived in the mud."},{"q":"Swedish midsummer is celebrated around the longest day with dances around a pole decorated with what?","a":"Flowers and leaves","o":["Flowers and leaves","Candles and ice","Fishing nets","Paper lanterns only"],"f":"The pole is a maypole, even though the feast is in June. Herring, new potatoes, and strawberries are the classic table."}]},{"id":"SWZ","name":"Eswatini","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇸🇿","latlng":[-26.5,31.5],"q":[{"q":"The kingdom became independent from Britain in which year, fifty years before it renamed itself in English?","a":"1968","o":["1968","1910","1966","2018"],"f":"The 2018 English name, Eswatini, means land of the Swazi and matches siSwati. 2018 was the renaming, not independence. 1966 was the year for Botswana and Lesotho."},{"q":"At Umhlanga, unmarried girls take part by doing what?","a":"Cutting reeds for the queen mother's enclosure","o":["Cutting reeds for the queen mother's enclosure","Racing horses","Diving for pearls","Weaving silk"],"f":"The reeds repair screens around the royal residence. The gathering is usually in late August or early September. Incwala, around the December solstice, is a separate royal rite."},{"q":"Incwala, the main royal ritual, is timed to what?","a":"The first fruits, near the summer solstice","o":["The first fruits, near the summer solstice","Easter only","The Arctic winter moon","The rice harvest of a delta"],"f":"Young men collect sacred branches, and the king tastes the new crops. It is older and more restricted than the reed dance. December is summer in this hemisphere."},{"q":"The kingdom took shape during the same 19th-century upheaval that remade the Zulu world. That upheaval is called what?","a":"The Mfecane","o":["The Mfecane","The Mahdist war","The Fulani jihads","The Scramble"],"f":"Sobhuza I and later Mswati II consolidated the kingdom while wars and migrations rolled across the region. The Mahdist war was in Sudan. The Fulani jihads were in West Africa. The Scramble came later and was European."},{"q":"Sibebe, a huge bare dome just outside Mbabane, is a face of which rock?","a":"Granite","o":["Granite","Limestone","Sandstone","Basalt"],"f":"Granite weathers into smooth sheets, which is why the climb is one continuous rock. It is not a volcano. No world ranking is required to see that it is enormous."}]},{"id":"SYC","name":"Seychelles","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇸🇨","latlng":[-4.58333333,55.66666666],"q":[{"q":"The coco de mer, a palm native only to these islands, produces the world's largest what?","a":"Seed","o":["Seed","Leaf","Flower","Tree trunk"],"f":"A nut can weigh more than 15 kilograms. It grows wild only in the Vallée de Mai on Praslin and on Curieuse. The species name maldivica wrongly points at the Maldives."},{"q":"Aldabra, a raised coral atoll, is famous for a huge population of what?","a":"Giant tortoises","o":["Giant tortoises","Komodo dragons","Dodos","Penguins"],"f":"Isolation saved the Aldabra giant tortoise after sailors wiped out similar animals on other Indian Ocean islands. The dodo was Mauritian. There are no Komodo dragons here."},{"q":"The islands had no residents when France began settling Mahé in 1770. Britain took them during which wars?","a":"The Napoleonic Wars","o":["The Napoleonic Wars","The Crusades","The First World War","The wars of Italian unification"],"f":"Britain administered the islands from Mauritius and made them a separate colony in 1903. There was no local population when the French arrived. Enslaved Africans were brought with the settlers."},{"q":"Moutya, listed by UNESCO in 2021, was traditionally danced by enslaved people in what setting?","a":"At night, around a fire, away from the plantation house","o":["At night, around a fire, away from the plantation house","Inside a cathedral","On a navy parade ground","In a courtroom"],"f":"Drums are warmed at the fire, and dancers answer sung lines. It was comfort and resistance at once. Sega is a related Creole dance; moutya is the form UNESCO listed here."},{"q":"The inner islands, including Mahé, are ancient granite. Outer islands such as Aldabra are mostly what?","a":"Coral atolls","o":["Coral atolls","Active volcanoes","Dunes blown from Arabia","Stranded ice"],"f":"The granite islands are peaks of an old continental fragment, which is why Mahé has mountains. The outer islands are coral on submerged foundations, which is why Aldabra is flat."}]},{"id":"SYR","name":"Syria","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇸🇾","latlng":[35,38],"q":[{"q":"Which poet, born in Damascus in 1923, is famous for Arabic love poems quoted across the region?","a":"Nizar Qabbani","o":["Nizar Qabbani","Mahmoud Darwish","Khalil Gibran","Adonis"],"f":"He was a diplomat as well as a poet. Adonis, another major Syrian poet, was born in a village near Latakia, not in Damascus. Darwish was Palestinian and Gibran Lebanese."},{"q":"Tablets from Ugarit, modern Ras Shamra, preserve the earliest known what?","a":"Alphabetic cuneiform","o":["Alphabetic cuneiform","Chinese movable type","Hangul","The Greek vowel alphabet"],"f":"The city was destroyed around 1190 BCE. Its script wrote consonants with wedges. An earlier alphabet, Proto-Sinaitic, was not cuneiform, and the Greek vowels came later."},{"q":"In the 3rd century a ruler of Palmyra seized Egypt and much of the Roman East. Who was she?","a":"Zenobia","o":["Zenobia","Cleopatra","Boudica","Theodora"],"f":"She ruled for her young son after her husband Odaenathus died. The emperor Aurelian defeated her around 272. Cleopatra's Egypt was three centuries earlier, and Boudica rebelled in Britain."},{"q":"A reversible patterned weave, and a famous patterned steel, both take their English name from which city?","a":"Damascus","o":["Damascus","Aleppo","Homs","Palmyra"],"f":"Damask cloth shows its design on both sides. Europeans gave the name Damascus steel to watery-patterned blades, many of them forged from Indian wootz that had been traded through the city."},{"q":"Giant wooden wheels on the Orontes at Hama, turned by the current itself, are called what?","a":"Norias","o":["Norias","Sakias","Shadufs","Qanats"],"f":"A noria lifts river water into a channel without an animal or an engine. The largest at Hama are about 20 meters across. A sakia is turned by an animal, and a shaduf is a hand lever."}]},{"id":"TCD","name":"Chad","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇹🇩","latlng":[15,19],"q":[{"q":"The Lakes of Ounianga stay full in the middle of the Sahara. They are fed mainly by what?","a":"Underground water","o":["Underground water","A glacier","The Nile flood","A pipe from the sea"],"f":"Some lakes are fresh and others, a short walk away, are extremely salty. Wind would dry them if groundwater did not keep arriving. UNESCO lists the group. They are not an irrigation project."},{"q":"The Ennedi Massif, a UNESCO plateau of sandstone, is known for what?","a":"Rock arches and prehistoric paintings","o":["Rock arches and prehistoric paintings","Roman cities","Medieval cathedrals","Terraced rice"],"f":"Arches such as Aloba stand over desert valleys. Paintings show cattle from a wetter time. Herders still use the massif. It is not a Roman ruin."},{"q":"Toumaï, a skull found in the Djurab in 2001, is the fossil Sahelanthropus. It is about how old?","a":"About 7 million years","o":["About 7 million years","About 7,000 years","About 700,000 years","About 66 million years"],"f":"It may lie near the split between humans and chimpanzees, but scientists still debate its exact place. It is not a dinosaur, and it is vastly older than the first cities."},{"q":"The shallow lake that gave the country its name has shrunk since the 1960s mainly because of what?","a":"Drier rains and water taken for irrigation","o":["Drier rains and water taken for irrigation","One earthquake","A dam on the Congo","A volcano filling the basin"],"f":"The lake is shared with Nigeria, Niger, and Cameroon. Because it is shallow, a change in rain or irrigation moves the shore a long way. It has not vanished."},{"q":"Zakouma National Park, in the greener southeast, is known for bringing back which animals after heavy poaching?","a":"Elephants","o":["Elephants","Giant pandas","Tigers","Polar bears"],"f":"Herds crashed in the early 2000s and later recovered under tighter protection. The southeast is savanna, a different country from the Saharan north. Pandas and tigers were never here."}]},{"id":"TGO","name":"Togo","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇹🇬","latlng":[8,1.16666666],"q":[{"q":"In Koutammakou, a UNESCO landscape, the Batammariba build tower-houses called what?","a":"Takienta","o":["Takienta","Tata forts","Ksour","Trulli"],"f":"Animals and grain sit below, and people live above, with a terrace. The same houses continue across the border in Benin. They are homes, not empty ruins."},{"q":"Sylvanus Olympio, the first president after independence in 1960, was assassinated in which year?","a":"1963","o":["1963","1958","1960","1991"],"f":"The killing was among the first military coups in independent Africa. Gnassingbé Eyadéma later dominated politics for decades. 1960 was independence, not the assassination."},{"q":"In the south, vodun shrines are part of community life, shared with Benin. Vodun is best described as what?","a":"A West African religion of spirits","o":["A West African religion of spirits","A school of Buddhism","A branch of Islam","A colonial law code"],"f":"Enslaved people carried related practices to Haiti, where they became Vodou. The shrines here and in Benin are the older home. It is a religion, not a statute book."},{"q":"Germany's colony of Togoland was split after the First World War between which two powers?","a":"Britain and France","o":["Britain and France","Spain and Portugal","Belgium and Italy","The United States and Liberia"],"f":"The British section joined the Gold Coast and is now part of Ghana. The French section became this country in 1960. The narrow shape is that colonial cut, not a canyon."},{"q":"The hills that catch the rain, shared with Ghana and Benin, became a zone for which colonial cash crops?","a":"Coffee and cocoa","o":["Coffee and cocoa","Dates and olives","Teff and barley","Quinoa and rye"],"f":"The coast is hotter and the far north is drier. Coffee and cocoa still grow on the wetter slopes. Dates belong to Saharan oases, and teff to Ethiopia."}]},{"id":"THA","name":"Thailand","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇹🇭","latlng":[15,100],"q":[{"q":"Which film director, born in Bangkok in 1970, won the Palme d'Or for Uncle Boonmee Who Can Recall His Past Lives?","a":"Apichatpong Weerasethakul","o":["Apichatpong Weerasethakul","Bong Joon-ho","Ang Lee","Wong Kar-wai"],"f":"His films mix village life with ghosts and folk belief. Bong Joon-ho is South Korean, Ang Lee is Taiwanese-American, and Wong Kar-wai is from Hong Kong."},{"q":"A ruined royal city of temple prangs, north of Bangkok, was destroyed by a Burmese army in 1767. What is it called?","a":"Ayutthaya","o":["Ayutthaya","Sukhothai","Angkor","Bagan"],"f":"It had traded with Europe and Japan and was one of the largest cities of the 1600s. Sukhothai is an earlier Thai kingdom. Angkor is in Cambodia and Bagan is in Myanmar."},{"q":"The April festival of the traditional new year is famous for water thrown in the streets. What is it called?","a":"Songkran","o":["Songkran","Loy Krathong","Yi Peng","Thingyan"],"f":"It falls in the hottest weeks. Loy Krathong, with baskets of flowers set on the water, comes months later. Thingyan is the related new-year festival in Myanmar."},{"q":"A stand-up fighting art that scores fists, elbows, knees, and shins is called what?","a":"Muay Thai","o":["Muay Thai","Pencak silat","Bokator","Muay Lao"],"f":"Before a bout, fighters perform the wai khru, a ritual dance to their teachers. Pencak silat belongs to the Malay world, bokator is Khmer, and Muay Lao is the related art of Laos."},{"q":"A coup in 1932 ended absolute royal rule and introduced what?","a":"A constitution","o":["A constitution","A communist republic","Direct colonial rule","A shogunate"],"f":"The monarchy continued, but it was no longer absolute. Unlike most neighbors, the country was never made a colony of a European empire, though it did sign unequal treaties."}]},{"id":"TJK","name":"Tajikistan","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇹🇯","latlng":[39,71],"q":[{"q":"Which poet of the Samanid court, from the Panjakent area, is remembered as a founder of classical Persian verse?","a":"Rudaki","o":["Rudaki","Ferdowsi","Hafez","Omar Khayyam"],"f":"He lived in the 9th and 10th centuries, and only a fraction of his poems survive. The tomb honored today is at Panjrud, near Panjakent. Ferdowsi, Hafez, and Khayyam came later, in lands of present-day Iran."},{"q":"In the high Yaghnob valley, a small community still speaks a living descendant of which ancient language of the Silk Road?","a":"Sogdian","o":["Sogdian","Tocharian","Elamite","Hittite"],"f":"Sogdian merchants once ran caravans from Samarkand to China. Yaghnobi is the main tongue that still descends from it. Tocharian was spoken in the Tarim Basin and is extinct."},{"q":"The country's highest peak, in the Pamirs, was renamed for a 9th-century ruler who patronized Persian learning. Who?","a":"Ismail Samani","o":["Ismail Samani","Timur","Babur","Cyrus"],"f":"Soviet maps called it Stalin Peak and then Communism Peak. Ismail Samani's own tomb is in Bukhara, across the border in Uzbekistan. The Pamirs themselves are nicknamed the Roof of the World."},{"q":"Flatbread soaked in yogurt or sour milk, then topped with fried onion, is a dish especially associated with home cooking here. What is it called?","a":"Qurutob","o":["Qurutob","Plov","Lagman","Beshbarmak"],"f":"Qurut is dried yogurt, crumbled into water to make the sour base. Plov and lagman are loved too, but they are shared across Central Asia. Beshbarmak is the meat-and-noodle dish of the Kazakh and Kyrgyz table."},{"q":"The Fedchenko, flowing down from the Pamirs, is famous as one of the world's longest what outside the polar regions?","a":"Mountain glaciers","o":["Mountain glaciers","Navigable rivers","Railway tunnels","Irrigation canals"],"f":"It runs on the order of 70 kilometers, though ice measurements shift a little from survey to survey. It is a glacier, not a road and not a canal."}]},{"id":"TKM","name":"Turkmenistan","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇹🇲","latlng":[40,60],"q":[{"q":"A gas crater in the Karakum collapsed and was set alight in 1971. It became known by what nickname?","a":"The Door to Hell","o":["The Door to Hell","The Gates of Hell at Hierapolis","The Flaming Cliffs","The Eternal Flame of Yanar Dag"],"f":"Drillers hit a cavity, the ground fell in, and they lit the escaping gas expecting a short burn. It burned for decades. The Gates of Hell at Hierapolis are a Roman site in Türkiye, and the Flaming Cliffs are in Mongolia."},{"q":"A desert horse with a metallic golden coat, bred for endurance, is the breed this country prizes above others. What is it called?","a":"The Akhal-Teke","o":["The Akhal-Teke","The Arabian","The Przewalski","The Andalusian"],"f":"The shine comes from the structure of the hair, not from dye. These were the horses of Turkmen riders for centuries. Przewalski's horse is the wild horse of the Mongolian steppe."},{"q":"Merv, a UNESCO city of the Silk Road in the Margiana oasis, was destroyed in 1221 by which army?","a":"The Mongols","o":["The Mongols","The Romans","Alexander's Macedonians","The Ottomans"],"f":"Writers of the time described Merv as one of the largest cities on Earth. The Mongol attack is remembered as unusually destructive. Alexander had passed through many centuries earlier, and the city recovered."},{"q":"Tribal carpets repeat a geometric badge that identifies the weaver's group. What is that badge called?","a":"A gul","o":["A gul","A mandala","A paisley","A cartouche"],"f":"Tekke, Yomut, and Saryk guls are all different. The deep red carpets of the region traveled west on the same routes as silk."},{"q":"A Soviet canal hundreds of kilometers long carries Amu Darya water across which desert to irrigate cotton?","a":"The Karakum","o":["The Karakum","The Kyzylkum","The Taklamakan","The Gobi"],"f":"The Karakum Canal, begun in the 1950s, is one of the longest irrigation canals ever dug. Taking that water helped shrink the Aral Sea. The Kyzylkum lies mostly farther east, in Uzbekistan."}]},{"id":"TLS","name":"Timor-Leste","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇹🇱","latlng":[-8.83333333,125.91666666],"q":[{"q":"Who, born in Dili, shared the 1996 Nobel Peace Prize for work toward a peaceful end to the occupation?","a":"José Ramos-Horta","o":["José Ramos-Horta","Xanana Gusmão","Mari Alkatiri","Nicolau Lobato"],"f":"He shared the prize with Bishop Carlos Filipe Ximenes Belo. Xanana Gusmão led the armed resistance. Nicolau Lobato was killed in 1978, before the prize existed."},{"q":"After Portugal withdrew in 1975, which country occupied the territory until a 1999 referendum?","a":"Indonesia","o":["Indonesia","Australia","The Netherlands","Japan"],"f":"Indonesia invaded in December 1975. A United Nations mission then ran the transition, and independence was restored on 20 May 2002. Japan had occupied the island only during the Second World War."},{"q":"Handwoven ceremonial cloth, with patterns that differ by region, was inscribed by UNESCO in 2021. What is it called?","a":"Tais","o":["Tais","Batik","Songket","Ikat of Sumba"],"f":"Tais is given at weddings and funerals. The method is a form of ikat, but the local name and the motifs are distinct. Sumba is an Indonesian island with its own ikat tradition."},{"q":"After the early trade in sandalwood, which crop became the main farm product sold abroad?","a":"Coffee","o":["Coffee","Cloves","Tea","Cocoa"],"f":"Most of it is arabica grown in the hills. Sandalwood had drawn Portuguese traders in the 1500s. Cloves were the fortune of Zanzibar, not of this island."},{"q":"Besides the eastern half of the island, the country includes a coastal exclave inside the western half. What is that exclave called?","a":"Oecusse","o":["Oecusse","Atauro","West Timor","Jaco"],"f":"Oecusse was a pocket of Portuguese rule surrounded by Dutch, later Indonesian, land. Atauro and Jaco are islands. West Timor is the Indonesian half of the island."}]},{"id":"TON","name":"Tonga","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇹🇴","latlng":[-20,-175],"q":[{"q":"Tonga kept its own monarchy and, unlike most Pacific islands, was never what?","a":"Annexed as a colony","o":["Annexed as a colony","Visited by Europeans","Converted to Christianity","Drawn on a map"],"f":"It was a British protected state from 1900 to 1970, but it was not taken over as a colony. The royal line continued throughout."},{"q":"The Ha'amonga 'a Maui, a coral-stone gateway on Tongatapu, is often nicknamed the Polynesian what?","a":"Stonehenge","o":["Stonehenge","Colosseum","Parthenon","Great Wall"],"f":"Two uprights support a lintel. Tradition credits the hero Maui, and a king is thought to have raised it around the 1200s."},{"q":"At a formal Tongan welcome the highest-ranking guest is served first with which pepper-root drink?","a":"Kava","o":["Kava","Mate","Sake","Mead"],"f":"The cup is passed in silence, and a set pattern of clapping is part of the etiquette. Kava is not alcoholic."},{"q":"Tongan ngatu, a decorated cloth given at weddings and funerals, is beaten from the bark of which tree?","a":"The paper mulberry","o":["The paper mulberry","The cork oak","The rubber tree","The cinnamon tree"],"f":"Women paste small sheets into long cloths and rub on stenciled patterns. A fine ngatu is a major gift."},{"q":"The terraced stone langi near Lapaha are tombs of which ancient sacred line of rulers?","a":"The Tu'i kings","o":["The Tu'i kings","Spanish viceroys","Dutch governors","British admirals"],"f":"The Tu'i Tonga once held religious authority felt across parts of western Polynesia. The great stone terraces still mark their tombs."}]},{"id":"TTO","name":"Trinidad and Tobago","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇹🇹","latlng":[11,-61],"q":[{"q":"Which writer, born in Chaguanas, won the 2001 Nobel Prize in Literature?","a":"V. S. Naipaul","o":["V. S. Naipaul","Derek Walcott","Samuel Selvon","C. L. R. James"],"f":"A House for Mr Biswas draws on his father's life in Trinidad. Walcott, a later Nobel laureate, was born in St. Lucia. Selvon and James were Trinidadian but did not win the prize."},{"q":"The steelpan was invented in Trinidad in the 20th century by tuning the tops of what discarded objects?","a":"Oil drums","o":["Oil drums","Church bells","Ship anchors","Bamboo stalks"],"f":"After bans on African drums, players first used bamboo, then metal. Tuners hammer each pan into separate notes. UNESCO lists the tradition."},{"q":"The Pitch Lake at La Brea is the world's largest natural deposit of what?","a":"Asphalt","o":["Asphalt","Obsidian","Rock salt","Amber"],"f":"Parts of the surface can be walked on, and softer patches slowly swallow heavy objects. Walter Raleigh caulked his ships with the pitch in 1595."},{"q":"The muddy pre-dawn parade that opens Trinidad Carnival, from a French phrase for daybreak, is called what?","a":"J'ouvert","o":["J'ouvert","Mardi Gras","Crop Over","Junkanoo"],"f":"It begins in the dark with rhythm, mud, and oil. The bright costume parade, pretty mas, follows on Carnival Tuesday."},{"q":"Tobago's Main Ridge Forest Reserve was set aside in 1776 chiefly to protect what?","a":"Rainfall for the plantations","o":["Rainfall for the plantations","A royal deer park","Naval oak timber","A quarantine station"],"f":"Scientists argued that clearing the ridge would dry the island. It is often called the oldest protected forest reserve in the Western Hemisphere."}]},{"id":"TUN","name":"Tunisia","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇹🇳","latlng":[34,9],"q":[{"q":"Which historian, born in Tunis in 1332, wrote the Muqaddimah on how states rise and fall?","a":"Ibn Khaldun","o":["Ibn Khaldun","Ibn Battuta","Ibn Sina","Averroes"],"f":"He served courts from Fez to Cairo and looked for patterns, not just chronicles. Ibn Battuta was the traveler from Tangier. Ibn Sina was the physician from near Bukhara. Averroes worked in Córdoba."},{"q":"Hannibal set out with elephants to cross the Alps against Rome from which city?","a":"Carthage","o":["Carthage","Leptis Magna","Alexandria","Utica"],"f":"The city stood near today's Tunis and fought Rome in three Punic Wars. Rome destroyed it in 146 BCE and later built a Roman town on the ruins. Leptis Magna is in Libya. Utica was a neighbor and a rival, not his base."},{"q":"The Roman amphitheater at El Jem, in the inland town once called Thysdrus, held about how many people?","a":"About 35,000","o":["About 35,000","About 3,500","About 350,000","About 350"],"f":"It was built in the 3rd century and is one of the largest amphitheaters anywhere. Rome's Colosseum is bigger. A great deal of the El Jem structure is still standing."},{"q":"Harissa, a paste inscribed by UNESCO in 2022, is pounded mainly from garlic, spices, and what?","a":"Dried chili peppers","o":["Dried chili peppers","Black olives","Saffron threads","Cocoa nibs"],"f":"Families dry the peppers, then grind them with salt and coriander. It is on the table at ordinary meals. The UNESCO listing is the skill and the shared practice, not a single brand."},{"q":"The great mosque and old university at the heart of the Tunis medina, founded in the 7th century, is called what?","a":"The Zaytuna Mosque","o":["The Zaytuna Mosque","The Kairaouine","Al-Azhar","The Umayyad Mosque"],"f":"Zaytuna was a seat of learning for centuries. The Kairaouine is in Fez, Al-Azhar is in Cairo, and the Umayyad Mosque is in Damascus. The medina's markets are still arranged by craft."}]},{"id":"TUR","name":"Türkiye","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇹🇷","latlng":[39,35],"q":[{"q":"Mustafa Kemal led the war of independence and died in 1938. In 1934 parliament gave him which surname?","a":"Atatürk","o":["Atatürk","Inönü","Karabekir","Orbay"],"f":"Atatürk means father of the Turks. He was born in Salonica, now in Greece, not in Anatolia. Ismet Inönü, who succeeded him, received a different surname under the same 1934 law."},{"q":"Göbekli Tepe, in the southeast, was built around 9600 BCE. Its builders were still what?","a":"Hunter-gatherers","o":["Hunter-gatherers","Pottery-making farmers","Bronze-smithing townsfolk","Chariot armies"],"f":"The T-shaped pillars went up before pottery was in use here, and before farming was thought to come first. The site is thousands of years older than Stonehenge or the pyramids."},{"q":"Justinian's great domed church, in what is now Istanbul, was completed in 537. What is it called?","a":"Hagia Sophia","o":["Hagia Sophia","The Blue Mosque","The Süleymaniye Mosque","The Chora Church"],"f":"Anthemius of Tralles and Isidore of Miletus were its architects. The Blue Mosque and the Süleymaniye were built in Ottoman centuries. The building's legal status has changed more than once since 1453, so the question is about the 6th-century church."},{"q":"UNESCO inscribed a ceremony in which followers turn as prayer. Which order performs it?","a":"The Mevlevi","o":["The Mevlevi","The Bektashi","The Naqshbandi","The Jesuits"],"f":"The order follows Jalal al-Din Rumi, who is buried in Konya. He was born in Balkh, in present-day Afghanistan, not in Anatolia. The Bektashi and Naqshbandi are other Sufi paths and do not perform this turning."},{"q":"The mound of Hisarlik, in the northwest, is identified with which city of Homer's Iliad?","a":"Troy","o":["Troy","Mycenae","Sparta","Knossos"],"f":"Heinrich Schliemann dug here in the 1870s and damaged upper layers. Later work showed a stack of cities, one of them Late Bronze Age. Mycenae, Sparta, and Knossos are real, but they are in Greece."}]},{"id":"TUV","name":"Tuvalu","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇹🇻","latlng":[-8,178],"q":[{"q":"Tuvalu means eight standing together. Why was the ninth island, Nui, left out of that count?","a":"Its people spoke a different language","o":["Its people spoke a different language","It is an underwater volcano","It belongs to Fiji","It was found only by aircraft"],"f":"Nui's speech is a Micronesian language related to Gilbertese. The other islands are Polynesian, so early groupings skipped Nui."},{"q":"Before it chose the name Tuvalu, Europeans knew the group as which islands?","a":"The Ellice Islands","o":["The Ellice Islands","The Gilbert Islands","The Line Islands","The Phoenix Islands"],"f":"The name honored Alexander Ellice, owner of a British ship. Islanders voted to separate from the Gilberts and became independent in 1978."},{"q":"Funafuti and the other inhabited islands are coral atolls sitting only a few meters above what?","a":"High tide","o":["High tide","The snow line","The tree line","A desert plateau"],"f":"There is almost no high ground. King tides already wash across parts of Funafuti, so rising seas are an immediate local threat."},{"q":"In a fatele, seated singers speed up while the dancers mainly do what?","a":"Stamp and clap faster","o":["Stamp and clap faster","Juggle fire","Walk across coals","Dive from wooden towers"],"f":"The rhythm builds until it is very fast. Fatele is performed at community celebrations and is the best-known national dance."},{"q":"Tuvalu's scattered islands have a combined land area of only about how much?","a":"26 square kilometers","o":["26 square kilometers","260 square kilometers","2,600 square kilometers","26,000 square kilometers"],"f":"That is smaller than many cities. The ocean zone around the atolls is enormous by comparison."}]},{"id":"TZA","name":"Tanzania","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇹🇿","latlng":[-6,35],"q":[{"q":"Farrokh Bulsara, later Freddie Mercury, was born in 1946 in which place?","a":"Stone Town, Zanzibar","o":["Stone Town, Zanzibar","Dar es Salaam","Nairobi","Mombasa"],"f":"His family was Parsi, from India, and lived in Zanzibar for work. He went to school in India and later formed Queen in Britain. He was not born in London."},{"q":"The highest point on Kilimanjaro, Africa's highest mountain, is called what?","a":"Uhuru Peak","o":["Uhuru Peak","Batian","Margherita Peak","Mawenzi"],"f":"Uhuru means freedom in Swahili. Mawenzi is the jagged secondary summit of the same volcano. Batian is the top of Mount Kenya. Margherita is in the Rwenzori, on the Uganda border."},{"q":"Olduvai Gorge became famous because Mary and Louis Leakey found what there?","a":"Early human fossils and stone tools","o":["Early human fossils and stone tools","Dinosaur eggs only","A pharaoh's tomb","A hoard of Roman coins"],"f":"Mary Leakey found the Zinjanthropus skull in 1959. Footprints at nearby Laetoli show early relatives walking upright. The gorge is lake sediment, not a royal cemetery."},{"q":"In 1964 two states united, and the new name blended both. Which pair?","a":"Tanganyika and Zanzibar","o":["Tanganyika and Zanzibar","Tanga and Zanzibar City","Tangier and Zanzibar","Tana and the Zambezi"],"f":"Tanganyika became independent in 1961 and Zanzibar in 1963. Julius Nyerere was the first president of the union. The name is a blend, not an ancient word."},{"q":"Bright animal paintings on square boards, a style begun around 1968, are named for which painter?","a":"Edward Saidi Tingatinga","o":["Edward Saidi Tingatinga","George Lilanga","Robino Ntila","Ibrahim el-Salahi"],"f":"He used bicycle paint on board and was killed in 1972. Apprentices kept the style going. Lilanga was a later artist from this country, with a different, sculptural pop. El-Salahi is Sudanese."}]},{"id":"UGA","name":"Uganda","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇺🇬","latlng":[1,32],"q":[{"q":"Green cooking bananas, steamed and mashed as the staple in the south, are called what?","a":"Matoke","o":["Matoke","Posho","Chapati","Rolex"],"f":"They are a savory variety, not the sweet fruit. Posho is maize porridge. Chapati came with the Indian Ocean trade, and a rolex is a chapati rolled around egg, a street snack, not the staple."},{"q":"Bwindi Impenetrable Forest is a UNESCO park famous for tracking which animals?","a":"Mountain gorillas","o":["Mountain gorillas","Bonobos","Orangutans","Giant pandas"],"f":"A large share of the remaining mountain gorillas live in Bwindi. Others live in the Virunga volcanoes of Rwanda and DR Congo. Bonobos live only west of the Congo River."},{"q":"In 1862 Speke reached the outlet of Lake Victoria at today's Jinja and named Ripon Falls. Those falls are now what?","a":"Under the water of a dam","o":["Under the water of a dam","Taller than in 1862","A dry canyon","A marine sanctuary"],"f":"The Owen Falls dam, now called Nalubaale, covered them. Speke's claim to have found the main source of the Nile was debated for years, but the outlet is real. The water still leaves the lake here."},{"q":"Which poet, born in Gulu in 1931, wrote Song of Lawino?","a":"Okot p'Bitek","o":["Okot p'Bitek","Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o","Wole Soyinka","David Rubadiri"],"f":"Lawino scolds her husband for dropping Acholi ways. Okot wrote in Acholi and in English. Rubadiri was Malawian, Ngũgĩ is Kenyan, and Soyinka is Nigerian."},{"q":"The Kasubi Tombs, burial place of Buganda's kings, are built mainly of what?","a":"Wood, thatch, and reed","o":["Wood, thatch, and reed","Cut stone","Fired brick","Poured concrete"],"f":"A fire in 2010 destroyed the main house, and rebuilding has used the same crafts. Buganda is a kingdom inside the country, with its own king, the kabaka. The tombs are not stone pyramids."}]},{"id":"UKR","name":"Ukraine","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇺🇦","latlng":[49,32],"q":[{"q":"Which engineer, born in Zhytomyr in 1907, was the chief designer of the Soviet program that put the first satellite and the first person into space?","a":"Sergei Korolev","o":["Sergei Korolev","Wernher von Braun","Yuri Gagarin","Konstantin Tsiolkovsky"],"f":"His identity was kept secret during his life, and he was called only the chief designer. Gagarin was the pilot, not the engineer who built the rocket."},{"q":"On 26 April 1986 a reactor exploded at which nuclear plant north of Kyiv?","a":"Chernobyl","o":["Chernobyl","Zaporizhzhia","Fukushima","Three Mile Island"],"f":"A flawed test and a dangerous reactor design drove the explosion. A concrete and steel shelter now covers the ruined unit, and the exclusion zone remains."},{"q":"Pysanky are Easter eggs decorated by drawing a design in wax and dipping the egg in dye. The wax is used so that each layer does what?","a":"It protects the color underneath","o":["It protects the color underneath","It hardens the shell into porcelain","It adds the dye itself","It makes the egg edible"],"f":"The method is the same idea as batik. Traditional patterns include wheat, stars, and endless lines, and regions had their own symbols."},{"q":"A medieval realm of East Slavic and Norse elites, remembered as Rus, had its political center in which city?","a":"Kyiv","o":["Kyiv","Moscow","Novgorod","Warsaw"],"f":"Its rulers converted to Christianity under Volodymyr in 988. Later states in Ukraine, Belarus, and Russia all look back to it, so it is shared history, not the property of one modern country."},{"q":"In 2022 UNESCO inscribed the culture of preparing Ukrainian borscht, a beet soup, on its list of heritage in need of safeguarding. The red color comes mainly from what?","a":"Beets","o":["Beets","Tomatoes","Paprika","Red wine"],"f":"Recipes differ by region and by family, and a dollop of sour cream is common. The listing protects the practice of making it, not a single official recipe."}]},{"id":"URY","name":"Uruguay","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇺🇾","latlng":[-33,-56],"q":[{"q":"Which writer of unsettling jungle stories, born in Salto, is a classic of River Plate literature?","a":"Horacio Quiroga","o":["Horacio Quiroga","Jorge Luis Borges","Pablo Neruda","Gabriel Garcia Marquez"],"f":"Collections such as Cuentos de amor de locura y de muerte draw on the forests of Misiones, where he lived for years after leaving Uruguay."},{"q":"Uruguay hosted and won the first FIFA World Cup in which year?","a":"1930","o":["1930","1924","1934","1950"],"f":"The final was in Montevideo, and Uruguay beat Argentina 4-2. The 1950 tournament, a common wrong guess, was the one Uruguay won in Brazil."},{"q":"Colonia del Sacramento, a UNESCO old town on the River Plate, was founded in 1680 by settlers from which country?","a":"Portugal","o":["Portugal","Britain","The Netherlands","France"],"f":"Spain and Portugal traded control of the outpost for a century. Its cobbled streets still show that tug-of-war."},{"q":"Candombe, a drumming tradition of Uruguay's Afro-descendant communities, is built around which instrument?","a":"The tambor drum","o":["The tambor drum","The highland bagpipe","The sitar","The alphorn"],"f":"Comparsas parade with three sizes of drum: chico, repique, and piano. UNESCO recognizes candombe as intangible heritage."},{"q":"The old meat plant at Fray Bentos, now a UNESCO site, shipped which tinned food to Europe for about a century?","a":"Corned beef","o":["Corned beef","Salt cod","Olive oil","Canned pineapple"],"f":"Justus von Liebig promoted beef extract, and a factory town grew up around the river port. The brand Oxo came from that extract."}]},{"id":"USA","name":"United States","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇺🇸","latlng":[38,-97],"q":[{"q":"Which biologist, born in Springdale, Pennsylvania, helped start the modern environmental movement with the 1962 book Silent Spring?","a":"Rachel Carson","o":["Rachel Carson","John Muir","Aldo Leopold","Henry David Thoreau"],"f":"The book showed how the pesticide DDT harmed birds and other wildlife. Muir, Leopold, and Thoreau wrote earlier classics, not Silent Spring."},{"q":"Yellowstone, set aside in 1872, is recognized as the world's first what?","a":"National park","o":["National park","National forest","Wildlife refuge","Marine sanctuary"],"f":"President Ulysses S. Grant signed the law. Its geysers, including Old Faithful, sit on one of the largest volcanic systems on the continent."},{"q":"Jazz took shape in the early 1900s especially in which city, from blues, ragtime, and brass-band traditions?","a":"New Orleans","o":["New Orleans","Chicago","New York","Kansas City"],"f":"Those other cities became jazz capitals later. The early style grew in the city's African American neighborhoods, including the brass bands of funerals and parades."},{"q":"The Grand Canyon was cut mainly by which river, through rock layers up to nearly two billion years old?","a":"The Colorado River","o":["The Colorado River","The Mississippi River","The Columbia River","The Rio Grande"],"f":"The deep canyon itself is geologically young compared with the rocks it exposes. The oldest layer, the Vishnu basement, formed about 1.7 billion years ago."},{"q":"The Nineteenth Amendment, ratified in 1920, forbids denying a citizen the vote on account of what?","a":"Sex","o":["Sex","Age","State of residence","Literacy"],"f":"It followed decades of organizing. Wyoming Territory had already granted women the vote in 1869, long before the amendment made the rule national."}]},{"id":"UZB","name":"Uzbekistan","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇺🇿","latlng":[41,64],"q":[{"q":"Which physician, born near Bukhara around 980, wrote the Canon of Medicine?","a":"Ibn Sina","o":["Ibn Sina","Al-Biruni","Al-Khwarizmi","Al-Farabi"],"f":"Latin Europe knew him as Avicenna, and the Canon stayed a medical textbook for centuries. Al-Biruni was a great scientist of Khwarizm. Al-Khwarizmi gave his name to the algorithm. Neither wrote this book."},{"q":"The Registan, a square framed by three tiled madrasas, is the heart of which city?","a":"Samarkand","o":["Samarkand","Bukhara","Khiva","Merv"],"f":"Timur made Samarkand his seat in the late 14th century, but the madrasas on the square are mostly 15th to 17th century. His grandson Ulugh Beg built the oldest of the three. Merv is in Turkmenistan."},{"q":"UNESCO inscribed the culture of rice cooked in one cauldron with meat, carrots, and chickpeas. What is that dish called?","a":"Plov","o":["Plov","Lagman","Shashlik","Manti"],"f":"It is also called palov or osh, and the inscription dates to 2016. An oshpaz cooks it for weddings in a giant kazan. Lagman is noodles, and manti are steamed dumplings."},{"q":"The walled inner town of Khiva, a UNESCO cluster of tiled colleges in the Khwarizm oasis, is called what?","a":"Itchan Kala","o":["Itchan Kala","The Registan","The Ark","Shah-i-Zinda"],"f":"The name means the inner fortress. The Ark is the citadel of Bukhara, and Shah-i-Zinda is a street of tiled tombs in Samarkand."},{"q":"The Aral Sea, once one of the world's largest lakes, shrank drastically in the late 20th century. The main cause was what?","a":"Soviet irrigation for cotton","o":["Soviet irrigation for cotton","A meteor impact","A dam across the Volga","A single year of drought"],"f":"Canals took the Amu Darya and the Syr Darya before they reached the lake. Fishing ports were left among salt flats. The sea did not vanish in one season; it broke into much smaller remnants."}]},{"id":"VAT","name":"Vatican City","cont":"Europe","emoji":"🇻🇦","latlng":[41.9,12.45],"q":[{"q":"Michelangelo painted the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel between 1508 and 1512. The most famous panel shows God reaching toward whom?","a":"Adam","o":["Adam","Moses","David","Peter"],"f":"He worked on scaffolding just under the vault, mostly lying on his back. The Last Judgment, on the altar wall, came later, in the 1530s."},{"q":"The Swiss Guard, founded in 1506, still protects the pope. New recruits must be Swiss Catholic men who have finished what?","a":"Swiss military training","o":["Swiss military training","A degree in theology","Service in the French Foreign Legion","A vow of silence"],"f":"Their striped Renaissance uniform is for ceremony. On 6 May 1527 many guards died covering the pope's escape during the Sack of Rome, which is why new members are sworn in on that date."},{"q":"Vatican City is the world's smallest country by land area. It became an independent state in 1929 under which agreement with Italy?","a":"The Lateran Treaty","o":["The Lateran Treaty","The Treaty of Versailles","The Congress of Vienna","The Schengen Agreement"],"f":"From 1870 until 1929 the popes had refused to recognize the Italian kingdom that had taken Rome. The treaty created the present state of about 44 hectares."},{"q":"When a pope dies or resigns, cardinals elect the next one in a locked meeting. What is that meeting called?","a":"A conclave","o":["A conclave","A synod","An ecumenical council","A consistory"],"f":"The word means with a key. Ballots are burned so that black smoke means no decision and white smoke means a new pope has been chosen."},{"q":"Gian Lorenzo Bernini designed the great oval in front of St. Peter's, ringed by columns. It is meant to feel like what?","a":"The embracing arms of the church","o":["The embracing arms of the church","A Roman racetrack","A fortress ditch","A Greek theater"],"f":"The colonnades were finished in the 1660s. An Egyptian obelisk, moved there in 1586, marks the center of the square."}]},{"id":"VCT","name":"Saint Vincent and the Grenadines","cont":"North America","emoji":"🇻🇨","latlng":[13.25,-61.2],"q":[{"q":"Which poet and jazz flugelhorn player, born in St. Vincent, performed in Britain with the Joe Harriott quintet?","a":"Shake Keane","o":["Shake Keane","Wynton Marsalis","Hugh Masekela","Dizzy Gillespie"],"f":"His poem cycle The Volcano Suite responds to the 1979 eruption of La Soufriere. He later returned home to work in cultural affairs."},{"q":"In 1793 William Bligh finally landed breadfruit trees here, meant to provide cheap food for whom?","a":"Enslaved plantation workers","o":["Enslaved plantation workers","The royal navy's officers","A new wine industry","Migrating songbirds"],"f":"His first voyage ended in the 1789 mutiny on the Bounty. Kingstown's botanic garden, founded in 1765, is among the oldest in the Americas and still grows breadfruit."},{"q":"La Soufriere, the island's active volcano, forced large evacuations with an explosive eruption in which year?","a":"2021","o":["2021","1995","2004","2010"],"f":"Earlier eruptions in 1902 and 1979 had also emptied the slopes. A lava dome in the crater collapses during the biggest blasts."},{"q":"The Tobago Cays, a marine park of uninhabited Grenadine islets, are protected especially for what?","a":"Coral reefs and sea turtles","o":["Coral reefs and sea turtles","Penguin colonies","Freshwater crocodiles","Alpine meadows"],"f":"Several scenes in the Pirates of the Caribbean films were shot in these waters. The cays belong to St. Vincent and the Grenadines, not to the country of Trinidad and Tobago."},{"q":"St. Vincent and the Grenadines became independent from Britain in which year?","a":"1979","o":["1979","1962","1966","1983"],"f":"The state includes St. Vincent plus Grenadine islands such as Bequia, Mustique, and Union Island. Carriacou and Petite Martinique, farther south, belong to Grenada."}]},{"id":"VEN","name":"Venezuela","cont":"South America","emoji":"🇻🇪","latlng":[8,-66],"q":[{"q":"Which leader, born in Caracas, led independence armies across northern South America and gave his name to Bolivia?","a":"Simon Bolivar","o":["Simon Bolivar","Jose de San Martin","Bernardo O'Higgins","Antonio Jose de Sucre"],"f":"Bolivar's campaigns helped free Venezuela, Colombia, Ecuador, Peru, and Bolivia from Spanish rule. Sucre, a close general, was a different person."},{"q":"Which cascade in Canaima National Park is recognized as the world's highest uninterrupted waterfall?","a":"Angel Falls","o":["Angel Falls","Kaieteur Falls","Iguazu Falls","Gocta Falls"],"f":"It drops off Auyan-tepui. The English name honors pilot Jimmie Angel, who landed on the mesa in 1937, not a biblical angel. The Pemon name is Kerepakupai-meru."},{"q":"Over Lake Maracaibo, the Catatumbo region is famous for storms that produce what on many nights of the year?","a":"Frequent lightning","o":["Frequent lightning","Nightly snow","Red sandstorms","Auroras"],"f":"Warm lake air meets cooler winds from the Andes, building huge thunderclouds. Sailors once used the flashes as a natural lighthouse."},{"q":"Joropo, often called Venezuela's national dance and music, traditionally features harp, maracas, and which small guitar?","a":"The cuatro","o":["The cuatro","The banjo","The balalaika","The ukulele"],"f":"The cuatro has four strings. Couples dance quick steps while poets improvise verses at country festivals on the llanos."},{"q":"Reports of the flat-topped mountain Roraima helped inspire Arthur Conan Doyle's novel about a plateau of prehistoric life. What is that novel called?","a":"The Lost World","o":["The Lost World","King Solomon's Mines","Journey to the Center of the Earth","The Time Machine"],"f":"Roraima is a tepui where Venezuela meets Guyana and Brazil. Its cliffs isolate plants and animals that live on the summit."}]},{"id":"VNM","name":"Vietnam","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇻🇳","latlng":[16.16666666,107.83333333],"q":[{"q":"Which poet, born in 1766, wrote The Tale of Kieu, the classic verse novel of the language?","a":"Nguyễn Du","o":["Nguyễn Du","Ho Xuan Huong","Nguyen Trai","Ho Chi Minh"],"f":"The story follows Kieu, who sacrifices herself for her family. Ho Xuan Huong wrote sharp folk poems, Nguyen Trai was a 15th-century statesman, and Ho Chi Minh wrote political verse, not this tale."},{"q":"Thousands of limestone towers rise from the sea in the northeast, a UNESCO landscape whose name means descending dragon. What is the bay called?","a":"Ha Long Bay","o":["Ha Long Bay","Phang Nga Bay","Lan Ha Bay","Bai Tu Long Bay"],"f":"Fishers still live on the water, and the limestone is full of caves. Phang Nga is a similar bay in Thailand. Lan Ha and Bai Tu Long are neighboring waters, not the named UNESCO bay of the descending dragon."},{"q":"Puppets on poles, hidden under the water, perform on a flooded stage. That art is called what?","a":"Water puppetry","o":["Water puppetry","Shadow puppetry","Noh","Wayang kulit"],"f":"It grew up in the flooded rice fields of the Red River delta. The puppeteers stand in the water behind a screen. Wayang kulit is the leather shadow theatre of Indonesia."},{"q":"A long tunic worn over trousers, now a dress for festivals and school ceremonies, is called what?","a":"An ao dai","o":["An ao dai","A kimono","A kebaya","A hanbok"],"f":"The modern cut, with a high collar and side slits, took shape in the 20th century from older tunics. A kebaya is the fitted blouse of the Malay world, and a hanbok is Korean."},{"q":"In 1954 a French army was defeated in a northwestern valley, ending French rule in Indochina. What was that battle?","a":"Dien Bien Phu","o":["Dien Bien Phu","The Tet Offensive","The battle of Hue","The fall of Saigon"],"f":"Viet Minh guns had been hauled onto the hills around the French camp. The Tet Offensive and the battle of Hue came in 1968, during the later American war, and Saigon fell in 1975."}]},{"id":"VUT","name":"Vanuatu","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇻🇺","latlng":[-16,167],"q":[{"q":"On Pentecost Island, men jump from tall towers with vines tied to their ankles. What is this called?","a":"Land diving","o":["Land diving","Firewalking","Pearl diving","Outrigger racing"],"f":"The nagol jumps are meant to bless the yam harvest. Films of the dives inspired modern bungee jumping."},{"q":"Before independence in 1980, Britain and France ruled the islands at the same time under what system?","a":"A condominium","o":["A condominium","A caliphate","A shogunate","A city-state"],"f":"The New Hebrides had two police forces, two courts, and two school systems. People joked that even the rules of the road were doubled."},{"q":"Mount Yasur on Tanna is famous because visitors can watch which frequent volcanic activity from the rim?","a":"Bursts of lava","o":["Bursts of lava","A boiling mud pot only","Calving glaciers","Sand avalanches"],"f":"Yasur has erupted almost continuously for centuries. Its regular explosions make it one of the most approachable active volcanoes."},{"q":"Bislama, Vanuatu's national creole, takes most of its words from which European language?","a":"English","o":["English","Spanish","Portuguese","Dutch"],"f":"It grew on ships and plantations so people from different islands could work together. French also shaped everyday speech."},{"q":"At independence in 1980 the colonial name New Hebrides was replaced by a name from local words for land. Those words are usually translated as what?","a":"The land that stands","o":["The land that stands","Many waters","High chiefs","Sacred fire"],"f":"The old name had honored the Hebrides of Scotland. Kanaky, easy to confuse with it, is a name from the independence movement in New Caledonia."}]},{"id":"WSM","name":"Samoa","cont":"Oceania","emoji":"🇼🇸","latlng":[-13.58333333,-172.33333333],"q":[{"q":"Which novelist, a founder of modern Pacific literature, was born in Apia?","a":"Albert Wendt","o":["Albert Wendt","Chinua Achebe","Gabriel Garcia Marquez","Derek Walcott"],"f":"His novel Leaves of the Banyan Tree follows a Samoan family across generations. He also taught and championed other Pacific writers."},{"q":"Which author of Treasure Island lived his last years in Samoa and is buried on Mount Vaea?","a":"Robert Louis Stevenson","o":["Robert Louis Stevenson","Robert Burns","Walter Scott","Arthur Conan Doyle"],"f":"Stevenson settled at Vailima, above Apia, in 1890 and died there in 1894. Samoans called him Tusitala, the teller of tales."},{"q":"Traditional Samoan tatau is tapped into the skin with a comb made of bone or tusk and what else?","a":"A wooden mallet","o":["A wooden mallet","A quill pen","A hot iron brand","A sewing needle"],"f":"The English word tattoo comes from Polynesian tatau. A full pe'a or malu is a long, painful ceremony and a major mark of identity."},{"q":"Samoa became independent on 1 January of which year, the first small Pacific island state to do so in that era?","a":"1962","o":["1962","1946","1970","1980"],"f":"It had been German Samoa, then a New Zealand territory. The date-line neighbor American Samoa remained separate."},{"q":"In 2011 Samoa skipped an entire calendar date by shifting across the international date line. Which date vanished?","a":"30 December 2011","o":["30 December 2011","29 February 2000","1 January 2000","4 July 1976"],"f":"Thursday the 29th was followed by Saturday the 31st. The jump put Samoa on the same business week as Australia and New Zealand."}]},{"id":"YEM","name":"Yemen","cont":"Asia","emoji":"🇾🇪","latlng":[15,48],"q":[{"q":"On Socotra, an umbrella-shaped tree that bleeds red resin grows wild nowhere else. What is it called?","a":"The dragon blood tree","o":["The dragon blood tree","The baobab","The frankincense tree","The coco de mer"],"f":"The species is Dracaena cinnabari, and the resin was used as dye and medicine. Frankincense trees grow on the southern Arabian mainland. The coco de mer is a palm of the Seychelles."},{"q":"A Red Sea port gave its name to a style of coffee because the beans were shipped from there. What was the port?","a":"Mocha","o":["Mocha","Aden","Muscat","Jeddah"],"f":"The coffee plant was domesticated in the Ethiopian highlands. Yemeni merchants made the drink a world trade, and Europeans first met the beans at Mocha. The plant was not native to Arabia."},{"q":"Shibam, a UNESCO town of mud-brick tower houses in the Hadramaut, is nicknamed what?","a":"The Manhattan of the desert","o":["The Manhattan of the desert","The Venice of the East","The Pompeii of Arabia","The Petra of the South"],"f":"Most of the towers were built from the 16th century on, packed inside a walled rectangle and rebuilt after floods. They are mud brick, not the stone tombs of Petra."},{"q":"The ancient kingdom of Saba kept a great earthen dam at Marib to water the desert. That dam is remembered because it what?","a":"Collapsed in late antiquity after centuries of repairs","o":["Collapsed in late antiquity after centuries of repairs","Was built by Roman engineers","Was a wall against the sea","Still holds its original reservoir"],"f":"Inscriptions record repairs over a thousand years. Arab tradition treats the final failure, around the 6th century, as a disaster that scattered communities. A modern dam nearby is a different structure."},{"q":"Tower houses in the old city of Sana'a, outlined in white gypsum, are built mainly of what?","a":"Stone below and brick above","o":["Stone below and brick above","Mud brick like Shibam","Blocks of marble","Coral stone"],"f":"The white outlines make the old city look patterned from the street. Shibam, in the Hadramaut, is the mud-brick tower town. Sana'a's houses stand on stone, with baked brick in the upper floors."}]},{"id":"ZAF","name":"South Africa","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇿🇦","latlng":[-29,24],"q":[{"q":"Nelson Mandela was born in 1918 in which village?","a":"Mvezo","o":["Mvezo","Soweto","Qunu","Johannesburg"],"f":"He grew up in Qunu, nearby, in the Thembu line. Soweto was the township of his later politics. He became president in 1994, after 27 years in prison."},{"q":"On 3 December 1967 Christiaan Barnard led the first human-to-human heart transplant at which hospital?","a":"Groote Schuur Hospital","o":["Groote Schuur Hospital","Baragwanath Hospital","The Mayo Clinic","Hammersmith Hospital"],"f":"Louis Washkansky lived 18 days. The donor was Denise Darvall. Barnard had trained partly in the United States, but the operation was in Cape Town."},{"q":"In 1925 Abraham Perold crossed Pinot Noir with Cinsaut, then called Hermitage, and created which grape?","a":"Pinotage","o":["Pinotage","Chenin blanc","Cabernet Sauvignon","Shiraz"],"f":"The name joins Pinot and Hermitage. He planted the seeds at Stellenbosch and almost forgot them. Chenin blanc is widely grown here too, but it is a French grape, not this cross."},{"q":"Robben Island, in Table Bay, is remembered especially as the prison that held whom for 18 years?","a":"Nelson Mandela","o":["Nelson Mandela","Steve Biko","Desmond Tutu","Albert Luthuli"],"f":"He spent 18 of his 27 prison years there. The island had been a leper colony and a military post before the apartheid prison. Biko died in custody on the mainland. Tutu and Luthuli were not its long-term prisoners."},{"q":"The Cradle of Humankind, a UNESCO landscape of caves near Johannesburg, is famous for what?","a":"Fossils of early human relatives","o":["Fossils of early human relatives","San paintings only","Diamond pipes","Voortrekker forts"],"f":"Sterkfontein yielded the skull called Mrs. Ples and the skeleton Little Foot. The gold reefs of the Witwatersrand are a different geology nearby. These caves are about bones, not gems."}]},{"id":"ZMB","name":"Zambia","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇿🇲","latlng":[-15,30],"q":[{"q":"Who led the country to independence in 1964 and was its first president?","a":"Kenneth Kaunda","o":["Kenneth Kaunda","Robert Mugabe","Julius Nyerere","Samora Machel"],"f":"He headed the United National Independence Party and ruled until 1991. Independence Day is 24 October 1964. Mugabe, Nyerere, and Machel led Zimbabwe, Tanzania, and Mozambique."},{"q":"Livingstone named a waterfall Victoria Falls. The older name, Mosi-oa-Tunya, means what?","a":"The smoke that thunders","o":["The smoke that thunders","The boiling pot","The place of rainbows","The end of the river"],"f":"The falls stand on the border with Zimbabwe, so neither country owns them alone. Mist rises far above the gorge, which is the smoke. The Boiling Pot is a gorge downstream, a different feature."},{"q":"The maize porridge eaten by hand with a relish of vegetables, beans, or fish is called what?","a":"Nshima","o":["Nshima","Fufu","Injera","Couscous"],"f":"Neighbors call a similar porridge nsima or sadza. Many people do not count a meal as real without it. Fufu is a pounded root, and injera is a fermented bread."},{"q":"At Kuomboka, when the Zambezi flood rises, the Lozi king does what?","a":"Moves by barge from the plain to higher ground","o":["Moves by barge from the plain to higher ground","Plants the first maize","Crowns a chief in a cave","Sends cattle to the coast"],"f":"The royal barge, the Nalikwanda, is paddled by a black-and-white team. The Barotse floodplain is under water for part of the year, so the move is practical as well as ceremonial."},{"q":"The colonial name Northern Rhodesia honored which businessman and politician?","a":"Cecil Rhodes","o":["Cecil Rhodes","David Livingstone","Henry Morton Stanley","Jan van Riebeeck"],"f":"Southern Rhodesia became Zimbabwe, and Nyasaland became Malawi. The new name comes from the Zambezi, a break with Rhodes. Livingstone saw the falls, but the colony was not named for him."}]},{"id":"ZWE","name":"Zimbabwe","cont":"Africa","emoji":"🇿🇼","latlng":[-20,30],"q":[{"q":"Great Zimbabwe, the stone city that gave the country its name, flourished mainly in which centuries?","a":"The 11th to the 15th","o":["The 11th to the 15th","The 1st to the 3rd","The 18th and the 19th","The 7th century BCE"],"f":"Walls were stacked without mortar, and gold went out to the Indian Ocean. Early European visitors doubted that local people built it; archaeology shows they did. The modern state took the name at independence in 1980."},{"q":"A sculpture movement encouraged from the 1950s, strongly associated with Shona artists, is known for carving what?","a":"Local stone such as serpentine","o":["Local stone such as serpentine","Imported Carrara marble only","Ice","Venetian glass"],"f":"Frank McEwen at the National Gallery supported the early workshop. Artists such as Nicholas Mukomberanwa became famous. The stone is local, and the styles are modern, not copies of the ancient birds."},{"q":"Which novelist, born in Mutoko in 1959, wrote Nervous Conditions?","a":"Tsitsi Dangarembga","o":["Tsitsi Dangarembga","Doris Lessing","NoViolet Bulawayo","Yvonne Vera"],"f":"The novel, published in 1988, follows Tambu. Vera and Bulawayo are also major novelists from this country. Lessing grew up here but was born in Iran, and she did not write that book."},{"q":"Kirsty Coventry won Olympic gold in the 200 meter backstroke at which two Games?","a":"Athens 2004 and Beijing 2008","o":["Athens 2004 and Beijing 2008","Atlanta 1996 and Sydney 2000","London 2012 and Rio 2016","Moscow 1980 and Los Angeles 1984"],"f":"She was born in Harare and also won other medals at those Games. The golds are history. Later swimmers cannot rewrite them."},{"q":"Soapstone birds excavated at Great Zimbabwe were found mainly in which part of the site?","a":"The hill complex","o":["The hill complex","A Portuguese shipwreck","A Roman forum","The outer valley fields"],"f":"Most came from the eastern enclosure on the hill. Several have been returned from South Africa and from Europe. Images of the birds became a national emblem. They are soapstone, not gold."}]},{"id":"ATA","name":"Antarctica","cont":"Antarctica","emoji":"🇦🇶","latlng":[-90,0],"q":[{"q":"Who led the first expedition confirmed to reach the South Pole, arriving in December 1911?","a":"Roald Amundsen","o":["Roald Amundsen","Robert Falcon Scott","Ernest Shackleton","Richard Byrd"],"f":"Amundsen's team skied with sled dogs and reached the pole on 14 December 1911. Scott arrived about a month later, and his party died on the return."},{"q":"Which volcano on Ross Island is the world's southernmost active volcano and holds a lava lake?","a":"Mount Erebus","o":["Mount Erebus","Mount Vinson","Mount Etna","Deception Island"],"f":"Erebus has been erupting for decades. Deception Island, farther north, is also an Antarctic volcano, but it is not the southernmost."},{"q":"Through the Antarctic winter, a male emperor penguin balances the single egg on his feet and covers it with what?","a":"A flap of belly skin","o":["A flap of belly skin","A nest of twigs","A burrow of sand","A ring of stones"],"f":"He does not eat for about two months while the female returns to the sea. Chicks hatch into one of the coldest seasons on Earth."},{"q":"The Antarctic Treaty, signed in 1959, sets the continent aside mainly for what?","a":"Peaceful scientific research","o":["Peaceful scientific research","Commercial mining","Nuclear weapons tests","One country's rule"],"f":"It froze competing territorial claims and banned military bases and nuclear explosions. The treaty entered into force in 1961."},{"q":"Blood Falls, at the snout of Taylor Glacier, runs red mainly because of what in the brine?","a":"Iron that rusts on contact with air","o":["Iron that rusts on contact with air","Red algae","Copper ore","Penguin guano"],"f":"The water is so salty that it stays liquid below the usual freezing point. It seeps from an ancient brine pocket under the ice."}]}];
